      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>MCDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF-R A ISING FLOUR "" /RAEDY S USED. THE SUNDERED HEARTS</p>
        <p>IVIADE-UP DISHES</p>
        <p>OR, A WAR OF THE HOUSEHOLD.</p>
        <p>By the Author of "The Double Life," "The Bailiff's Scheme," &amp;e., Sta.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVIII: NOEL WEIR,</p>
        <p>When Dora stole Oat O ecretly and abruptly from the lodgings at Lowater Crescent, the one great idea possessing her soul was that of escape from the odious Narrs, and from Felix Warner, who had become no less odious to her.</p>
        <p>But, as she hurried out of the Crescent, turning the nearest corner, and discovered that she was not pursued, her speed involuntarily slackened, her thoughts grew Calmer, and the question pro- sented itself to her—where was she going? The question brought her to an abrupt stand- still.</p>
        <p>What friend had she to whom she could go in this hour of her need, and who would shelter and protect her?</p>
        <p>- Her desolation and helplessness came home to her soul at that moment with a crushing and awful force. Alone and friendless in the streets of London !</p>
        <p>She looked up at the houses grimly bordering the street with a vain wistfulness—a homesick longing—that had in it something of the bitterness of death.</p>
        <p>"Nowhere to go ?" she murmured piteously. "What shall I do ?"</p>
        <p>In the midst of her despair, like a ray of sun- light flashing through darkness, came the thought of her ;ejected lover, the young squire of Weir Hall.</p>
        <p>"Noel will help me 1" she thought, her heart leaping with sadden hope. "I will go to him. I know his hotel. He will befriend me." _</p>
        <p>She quickened her • steps, moving fotward rapidly. She had gone but a brief distance when she be- held coming down. the lonely, nearly deserted street a light, swinging figure, surmountei by a noble, handsome face, now grave and thoughtful</p>
        <p>in its expression almost to sternness. It was Squire Weir. She recognised him afar off, and wondered with- in herself, with keen reproach, how she could ever have thought him plain, and how she could ever have preferred to him the finical, courtly and bland Felix Warner.</p>
        <p>Squire Weir appeared to recognise the young girl at the same moment, despite the concealment afforded by her black veil. No mourning garments could shroud Dora's graceful, lithe little figure beyond his recognition. He approached her, his face kindling, his hands outstretched.</p>
        <p>" Dora !" he exclaimed, halting at her side.</p>
        <p>"Noel ! 0, Noel I" cried Dorli, all her anguish finding expression in that wailing cry. The young squires face paled. "What is it, Dora ?" he asked. "What has happened ?" Dora put back her veil, revealing her white, tear-stained face and swollen eyes, and regarded him with a piteous appeal that went to his soul. "0, Noel !" she said, her lips quivering. "I have left them. lam alone—all alone."</p>
        <p>"Not alone while I live, Dora," responded the young squire, earnestly. "You were going to Inc V' _ _</p>
        <p>"Yea. I had nowhere else to go." A smile, radiant in its brightness, transfigured the face of Noel Weir. Dora depended upon him, then I She turned first to him in her sore strait. But the smile faded, as Dora dropped her veil, flushing under the scrutiny of some passer. Noel drew her arm through his, and conducted her slowly through the street. They walked on for some time in silence, Dora leaning heavily upon the young squire's arm, and feeling a delicious sense of peace and restfulness that was doubly grateful after her late terror and despair. At length the pair approached the Kensington Gardens, at that hour, after five o'clock, alive with gaily dressed people. The young squire led Dora to a secluded bench at some distance from the slowly moving throng, seated her, and then, as he sat down beside her, said, with a grave and gentle tenderness: "We are as much alone here, Dora, as if the Gardens were deserted. Na one has an eye for us. Now tell me all about it." Dora drew a moaning sigh, and her tears dropped softly under her veil. " fo begin at the beginning, Noel," she said, "Jack Narr has come." The young squire uttered an exclamation. "He came this afternoon," said Dora. "He is what I expected to see him—coarse, vulgar and riotous. He was drunk—"</p>
        <p>"My poor Dora I" "He treated me with a strange mixture of defer- ence and familiarity. He called me Miss Dora, and seemed to regard me as his superior. But that may have been because 1 am educated, Noel. Edu- cation has made a wide gulf between the Narrs and me." _ _</p>
        <p>" It has only widened a gulf that Nature made, Dora."</p>
        <p>"He seemed to feel the distance between us. I have tried to feel a daughter's affection for those people, Noel, but I cannot. There are instincts of the soul which we cannot force. My whole being revolts against them. I cannot call that woman mother. I cannot call that man father. It is as if something within me made me shrink from such a profanity of those holy names. It is an awful step between poor papa and Jack Narr I" and Dora shuddered. "A wide step, indeed 1" said the young squire. "He—Jack Narr—came in, as I said," continued Dora' "and was in the midst of a declaration that he meant to be supported by me, or—or by my husband, when I should marry—when Felix Warner came forward from the doorway, where he had been standing long enough to hear what Narr had said—" _ _ _</p>
        <p>" Warner !" interposed the young squire, his face flushing. "He has come, then ?" "Yes. He heard what Jack Narr said. Felix Warner is a bitterly proud man. The sight of the Narrs, their coarseness, their vulgarity, Narr's good-natured drunken pompousness, Mrs. Narr's servile insolence—for her manner NVELS both servile and insolent—all disgusted him. The Narrs re- tired to their rear room, and Warner talked to me for some time, professing his love, yet betraying a strangeness of manner that chilled me. Finally— how can I tell you, Noel I—Warner told me of his high rank, and said how impossible it was that he could marry the daughter of such disreputable people, and offered me his love and protection at the price of what I prized most on earth—my honour." Dora flung back her veil at this juncture. There were tears in her eyes now. They flashed with an unquenchable haughtiness of spirit. The bright scarlet glowed in her cheeks, and a woman's bit- terest scorn and indignation showed themselves in every line and feature of her spirited and glorious face. "He dared insult you thus ?" cried Noel Weir, his face darkening with a just anger. " Yes. He loves me still, after his se'fish fashion ; but, although he might have wedded the well- portioned daughter of Squire Chessom, he cannot lower himself to marry the daughter of vagrant Jack Narr I He could not see that, in either case, I am the same girl." lie shall answer to me for this !" ejaculated the young squire, his ardent young blood flaming. "No, Noel. You must not quarrel with him. For my sake, don't," pleaded Dora. "All I have left is my good name. Do not let that be dragged into a quarrel." "You are right, Dora," said Noel, with an effort. "The snake shall go his way. At least, he felt your scorn and indignation ?" "Yes. I ordered him to leave. It seemed as if my love for him had turned to hatred against hitn —but that was hardly so. I never loved him. I was pleased with him, an,l flattered by his atten- tions ; nothing more. But I loathed him as he stood there, daring to pollute my ears with his words. Before he could go, the Narrs came in. Calculating upon their baseness, \Varner told them of hir proposition to me. The woman in- vited him to. have a conference with her on the subject. She seemed willing to sell me to him for gold—"</p>
        <p>"Monstrous !" ejaculated the young squire. " 1 went into my room, a 8,205 e of my danger overwhelming me. I sa.m, that 1 must escApe, and al; once. And tso I stole out, withsmt a thought of where I was going, and had just decided to go to yea when I saw you. There is my story, Noel. Now what am I to do ?" She looked at him with an infinite trust and reli- ?rice, her dark eyes glowing like lighted lamps. She moved a little nearer to him. “pa,pti, trusted you, Noel," she whispered. "You will be my friend now, won't you?" The young squire returned the gaze with one of love and tenderness too great for words, and he answered, in a choking voice;</p>
      </div>
