      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>BOBBY: "Mamma, where is dreamland?" 'Mamma: "I don't know, my son. Why do you ask ? " Bobby: "1 dreamed last night I had a bicycle. _I want to go and get it."</p>
        <p>" Did :you in</p>
        <p>iit with tin</p>
        <p>ten.iiis bla,z</p>
        <p>t yours the buirs-c</p>
        <p>on the farm</p>
        <p>the first time t</p>
        <p>The lady won</p>
        <p>xen t the house furii.ished. She Illis three boys" Owner: "Add the value of the furniture to the rent."</p>
        <p>A COUNTRY AGGRAVATION.7-BlabZer : "Out in the country where you live don't you find it aggra- vating to hurry to catch the trains?" Mazner : "Oh, no! It's hurrying to miss them that we find aggravating." " MAMMA !" "Well ?" "You licked me last week for thrashing Jimmie Watts, and papa licked ,me yesterday 'cause Johnny Phelps walloped me." Well ? " "I'm wondering what'll happen some time when it's a draw."</p>
        <p>MRS. SKINNER: "I'm glad to hear you say you have such a good appetite." Mr. Newboarder 4about to take his first meal, and much pleased at the prospect) : ." Landladies generally fear a good appetite." Mrs. Skinner: "I don't. When a man has a good appetite he can eat almost any- thing." SEASIDE LANDLORD (to departing guest): "I trust I may rely upon your recommending my .establichment ?" Cleaned-out Guest: "I don't happen to have at this moment a mortal enemy in the world 1" _</p>
        <p>AN UP-TO-DATE PARROT. —Mrs Dingle: "I'm awfully glad to see you, Mrs. Stone; I wanted to tell you that our old parrot will soon be able to ' talk again." Mrs. Stone: "I don't understand you; I thought you said he was deaf and dumb." So he is, but he's learned to make the deaf and dumb signals with his toes." A SHORT STORY.—Chap. I. : "I think you are just the bestest, goodest husband in all the world!" ' Chap. II.: "I wonder how much she wants ? " Chap. III.: "And he gave it to me without Ilussing a bit. I wonder what he has been up to ?" -</p>
        <p>LOVE'S FINANCES.—De Garry: "As you intend to marry her, why did you consent to her riding a bicycle, when you are so opposed to them ? " Merritt: "Well, I knew she would have her way in the end, and I calculated that by giving in no w her father would have to pay for I.i bicycle." MAN OVERBOARD: "Help—help !" Stranger: Phuy don't yez swim?" I don't know how." " Begorry, ye've got an illigant chance to learn." AT A RECENT DUET.—" I thought, count, that you were a dead shot?" "I am." "And yet, though you said you would shoot your adversary , ,through the heart, you hit him in the foot?" " was an error of judgment. I thought his heal was in his boots; it turned out to be in his ;mouth."</p>
        <p>THE REASON.—Mrs. M'Lubberty : " Oi wonder i how thim poor divils av pedlars can kape sowl an' body togither sellin' colly-buttons an' sich-like." M‘Lubberty : "Sure, dhey buy thim in lar-r-r-rge llots for half nawthin', d' yez moind, an' turn roight ;around an' sell them in small quantities for twoice as much. An' dhere yez a-r-r-re !" NOTHING REMARKABLE.—" Why, I don't see anything very remarkable about that," she said to her husband, who had been telling her of the wonderful lightning-change artist he had seen at ' the theatre the night before. "I know a woman who can run up stairs when a visitor is coming up the steps from the sidem alk and change her drem :and get down stairs again and be in the parlour saying, Why, I'm so glad to see you,' before the visitor has had a chance to sit down."</p>
        <p>A CRUEL DELUSION.—Caspar Corker: "What's de matter wid yer foot?" Jonas Deadheat : "Yet 'know dat 'ouse round yere on de corner?" ' "Yep." "I went up dere las' night, an' as I went up de path I saw er dog squatted in de dark ready to jump, an' I kicked 'm." "An' he bit you.'' "No—it wuz a iron dog." TitlssAND (furiously): "Here's my best meer• sehaum pipe broken! How, in the name of sense, did that happen ?" Wife: " I don't know, except that when I got up this morning I found yout meerschaum pipe in the front ball, and your over. • shoes on the parlour mantelpiece." Husband (mildly): "Oh, well, accidents will happen. presume there has been an earthquake or some. thing." A STRANGE FANCY.—Brown (after visiting a sick friend) : "Poor fellow ! Did you notice that he was slightly delirious ? " Jones : "No ; I didn't. He seemed to me to be quite rational.' Brown: "Oh, no ! Didn't you hear him say .11E knew just how he got the cold !" NOTHING, IF NOT IRISH.—Mrs. O'Flaherty : '6 see by the paper that the railroad's afther payin' 'twice as much fer cuttin' off a man's leg as it did fer killing wan. I don't understand it at all, at all." Mr. ("Flaherty: "An' sure that's raison- able. It takes so much less for a man to live on •afther he's dead." _ _</p>
        <p>THE ONE THING IMPOSSIBLE.—ReginaId: "Oh, if there were only something I could do to prove ,the depth of my affections." Edith: "There is. ,Don't talk nonsense, please." MANY a man wears a yachting cap who doesn't 'know a man-o'-war from a floating mud-dredge. "I RATHER like that idea," said Winkle, gazing ;at a man who was playing a drum, cymbals, clarionet, and four other instruments at one time. " Why ?" asked Tupman. "You have only to !kill one man to blot out an entire band," was the .sanguinary reply. UNRELIABLE.—" Dear papa," wrote the little girl, "I sent you a kiss last week by the carrier. ,I hope he gave it to you all right. Hereafter I'll isend 'em by letter, because the carrier is very !ugly, and I don't like giving them to him, and neither mamma nor nurse will do it for me.:</p>
        <p>STEPPING BAC! WAILD. Bcitg aAked why members of her sex always stepped backward oil 'a street-car, a woman replied : "Because it is the most awkward, unnatural, and dangerous way. I will illustrate." Thereupon she stepped off in the manner indicated, cracking her crown. At the funeral the family pastor explained that the ways of womankind are Past finding out. A HOPELESS C..lASE.—Caller (at insane asylum). : "I've been talking with that man in No. 46. in my opinion he is perfectly sane." Keeper (raising .his voice): "By the way, Mr. Dabson, 11,w arc</p>
