      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>I- XAMINEE, FPI!) Y OCTOBER 23. 1896</head>
        <p>THE I- XAMINEE, FPI!) Y OCTOBER 23. 1896</p>
        <p>" Dora, I will be your friend and brother, and may God deal with me as I deal with you. I will not takly advantage of your desolation to urge you to reconsider your decision against me, but I assure you I would more than ever esteem it an honour if you would become my wife. But we will not talk of that now. Do not think of me as your lover or suitor, but regard me as your brother whose greatest happiness is to care for you," "Thank you, -Noel," said the girl, lowly. "We will be sister acd brother." "Then the first use I shall make of my brotherly influence will be to advise you to adhere to your determination and not return to the Narrs. You have received a timely warning of their character, and your safety lies in keeping away from them. There can be no question, Dora, that, if they find you, they can compel you to return to them. The law gives them control over an unmarried daughter who is under age." "Where am I to go, Noel ?" "Mr. Chessom gave you a letter to his promised wife, Miss Coningsby, in Russell Square. This Miss Coningsby is still young, of course. . She must have a woman's heart. Go to her first of all, Dora, and tell her all your story. She may obtain for you a situation as resident governess in her own family, or that of some friend, and once installed in such a situation, it will be almost impossible for the Narrs or Warner to trace you. What do you think of the project ?" "It strikes me favourably. It is the best thing kcan do. But if she refuse to befriend me, Noel ?""</p>
        <p>"I have just eugaged rooms in a good lodging- house, so as to be near you," replied the young squire. "I will give them up to you and go back to my hotel. And then we will consider what is next to be done."</p>
        <p>"I must see Miss Coningsby to-day, Noel," ex- claimed Dora. "I see that the sooner lam settled in a secure home, the better it will be for me. Can we not go to Russell Square immediately ?" Squire Weir assented, and Dora lowered her veil and arose, taking her proffered irrn. They passed out of the Gardens and along the street. ' -.lied an empty cab, the</p>
        <p>Noel</p>
        <p>on sUn</p>
        <p>young pair entered it, 47ind the ord</p>
        <p>er was given to</p>
        <p>ic1I1_.;(</p>
        <p>The young squirt-) exerted himself to che..:r Dore, and he was so far successful that she was quite self-possessed and calm when at last the cab, ac- cording to Weir's direction, halted at the corner of the square. "I will remain here while you are gone," said the young man. "It might not do if I were seen awaiting you in front of the door, and I dare not lose sight of you. You know the number, Dora. If Miss Coningsby will befriend you, you need only to oome and tell me, or to send me a mes- sage." The cabman opesed the door, and Dora stepped out, hurrying along the square to the proper num. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The young squire looked after her with a yearn- ing tenderness, his sad eyes beaming with a look of ineffable love and patiencb. "She never loved Warner," he said to himself. "Perhaps I may yet win her to be my wife—my noble, spirited, glorious Dora! If I could but win her love I " and he sighed deeply. Meanwhile Dora had discovered the number she sought, and mounted the steps, and sounded the knocker thrice, heavily. A servant presently admitted her, and ushered her into a sombre drawing-room, saying, in re- sponse to Dora's inquiry, that Miss Coningsby was at home.</p>
        <p>Dora took from her pocket-book a card, on which was written the name she had always borne, and sent it to the lady. She then took possession of a stuffed chair, and awaited, with a wildly beating heart, the lady's arrival. Mr. Coningsby was a solicitor, who had ac- quired, partly by inheritance, partly by marriage, and partly by his own efforts, a comfortable prop- erty. it was understood that his eldest daughter —the betrothed wife of Edmund Chessom—would have a handsome dowry. He was a widower, and Miss Coningsby presided over his household and superintended the care of his younger chil- dren, who were also provided with a resident governess. Of these facts Dora was utterly ignorant. Ed- mund Chessom had said little to enlighten her concerning the family to which he proposed -to ally himself. The minutes passed wearily in waiting, and a half hour dragged slowly away. "Miss Coningsby is not well bred," she thought, half resentfully, "to keep me waiting so long. Poor Noel! He will tire waiting for me !" At last, however, the clicking of boot heels On the tiles forming the hall floor and the rustling of silk were heard, and then the door of the dim and sombre apartment opened, and Miss Con- ingsby swept into the room.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIX. MISS CONINGSBY.</p>
        <p>Miss Coningsby was a fair, plump woman, of some five-and-twenty years, tall, and with a supercilious carriage and manner that qaite spoiled her good looks. It was evident that fifteen or twenty years more would transform her into a heavy British matron of formidable proportions. She was at- tired in a dinner costume of rose-coloured tissue over silk, and her dress was remarkable for the length of its train as for the extreme lowness of the corsage and the shortness of its sleeves. No greater contrast could have been conceived than she presented to her slight, girlish visitor, with the exquisite, flower-like face and bright appealing eyes. Miss Coningsby held in her plump, jewelled hand Dora's card. She looked at it and the girl doubtfully. "You are the—the young person who sent me this card r she questioned, superciliously. Dora arose, bowing assent. "Resume your seat, Miss—Miss Narr," said Miss Coningsby, sinking languidly upon a sofa near. "I was rather surprised at your retaining a name to which, as I understand, you are not entitled. Your real name, Mr. Chessom informs me, is N arr."</p>
        <p>Dora's cheeks flushed as she sat down again:</p>
        <p>"Squire Chessom adopted me as his daughter, madam," she said, quietly, "and gave me his name. It is a gift from which I cannot lightly part." " Ah ! But have you not considered how unplea- sant it may be for those to whom the name right- fully belongs to have it dragged through the associations which must now be yours? Is it just to Mr. Chessom to retain his name—to pass your- self off as his sister?"</p>
        <p>Dora's face flushed yet more hotly. "I have never uttered his name to any one since I left the Grange madam," said replied, with effort controlling her own spirit. "Mr. Chessom is not my brother, and I have no wish to claim him as such, although his lather was a father to me." " Ah, yes," said Miss Coningsby, with a fashion- able drawl, "I have heard that the eccentric old squire was your benefactor. But, of course, as he is dead, your connection with the family ceases. Mr. Chessom, however, with a kindness that does him credit, has written to me to beg me to interest myself in your fortunes. He informs inc that the eccentric squire lavished a vast amount of money upon your education, and that you will, of course, choOse to support yourself and your mother. It is about this, I presume, that you have called upon me to-day ?'' "Yes, madam," replied Dora, with a quiet self- respect which Miss Coningsby did not seem to like. "Im a competent to teach the English bmuches, French, German and music. You will find me a conscientious teacher—:"</p>
        <p>"Oh, I don't doubt that," drawled Miss Con- ingsby. "You ought to be conscientious, and all that, when you are paid for it. I can give you two music pupils—my sisters—three French pupils, including my sisters and my young brother. I will also recommend you to my friends, but oaly upon the condition that you take your real name and call yourself Narr." "But that name is distasteful to me," said Dora, hesitatingly. Miss Coningsby arched her brows. " Distasteful ! Your own name! Of course, it is not so finely sounding as Chessom, but thea it 'a your own. I cannot consent to impose upon people a teacher under a false name, nor can I introduce a music-teacher under a name I expect to bear at some future time. It is quite impos- sible."</p>
        <p>Dora's heart sank. To give up the name the old squire had bestowed upon her seemed like snapping the last link that held her to the happy past. Miss Coningsby watched the changes in Dora's face, as a boy watches the struggles of fly he has impaled on the wall. "Wcll ?" she questioned, impatiently. " You k:iow now the only condition upon which I can Euisi,3l. you. What have you to say ?" "Let me tell you my story first, madam," ex- claimed Dora, impetuously. "You must have a heart to feel for one of your own sex, beset with difficulties and in sore trouble."</p>
        <p>"Be brief, then," said Miss Coningsby, with barely perceptible curiosity, looking at her watch.</p>
        <p>Dora hastened to tell her story, beginning from the point of leaving the Grange. All the fire and impetuosity of her nature leaped into her face, manner, and voice, as she told of the Narrs, of Warner, and of Noel Weir. Her beauty and loveliness became fairly radiant under her excite- ment, and she passed from anger to pathos, from indignation to pleading, with unconscious rapidity awl with childlike ease.</p>
        <p>"You see, madam, the danger I would be in if I remain with the Narrs," she said, in conclusion, "It is impossible for me to remain with them longer. Will you not give me shelter—a home ?" "My sisters are already provided with a resi- dent governess," said Miss Coningsby, untouched by Dora's narration—" a staid, respectable woman of mature age. Of course, I cannot discharge her." "But will you not recommend me to sorrm one who may be in want of a governess?" asked Dora. "I cannot do that conscientiously," said the lady, coldly. "In the first place, I cannot give credence to your story. It is impossible that a mother should be willing to consign her child to a life of d4honour, You MUPt have exaggerated the facts. Of coure, 110 gotletnan—especially one allied to a noble family—would marry a vagrant's daughter. But if he made you vile propositions, you should simply dismiss him with dignity. Your place, Miss Narr, is with your parents. A mother is her daughter's best guardian—" "Such a mother ?"</p>
        <p>"1 have only the word of a discontented young girl that she is bad. 1 doubt that she is vile. Mr. Chesaom spoke of her as a very decent person." "I doubt if she is my mother," said Dora, bit- terly. "Papa fancied that I might have been her nurse-child, kept as her own, and in the place of her own—"</p>
        <p>_ "Worse and worse !" exclaimed Miss Coningsby, with a look of shocked propriety. "It is plain, Mis:i Narr, that you are a romantic young person, with a very ill-balanced mind. I have always thought it a. poor plan to take a child out of the lower ranks and educate it like one of the upper and my idea is now -confirmed. You .had turn to you mother at once, and assume, lot -Vet done, a filial relation to her. r 'better education, Shift 20111'S</p>
        <p>Tie c</p>
        <p>quietly to the levcl "Madam," sail 'Dora, patiently, "I do not despise the Narrs because they are poor and uneducated, but I do despise them because they are base. I cannot respect them, nor like them, Heaven knows that I would be proud of the poor- est and humblest parent if that parent were honest, sober, and virtuous. But my soil recoils from them!"</p>
        <p>11-tilaiA. Squire 'll'J!</p>
        <p>"You will have to come to liking them," re- marked Miss Coniugsby, with severity. "I am shocked at you, Miss Narr. I call your conduct highly disgraceful. This Squire Weir is no fit as- sociate for you, and by going about with him you are ruining your good name." "He is my best friend." "More likely he is the wolf, and your parents the innocent ones. He is inciting you to a rebel- lion against your parents, who are your lawful protectors. You are a romantic, headstrong. ill- advised girl, and you should return at once to your mother. Whatever her faults, however low she may be, she is your mother." "1 don't believe it 1" cried Dora. "I can't be. it 1" "This to me I" cried Miss Coningsby, with in- creasing severity. "Unhappy girl, you are travel- ling swiftly the downward road 1 I shall write to Mr. Ciiessom at once telling him of the unfor- tunate course you have taken. Unless you give up your Squire Weir, assume your real name, and live with your parents, I utterly decline to assist you in any way. When you can come to me in hu- mility as Dora Narr, and tell me that you are liv- ing with your parents in filial obedience, and that you wish to work for them, then, and not till then, will I assist you 1" She arose, severe, cold, and supercilious. Dora arose also. "You refuse to believe me, then, madam?" she said, her pure, sweet face pale and piteous. "I do.</p>
        <p>"May Haven forgive you for turning away one who sorely needed help? " said the girl, in a quaver- ing voice. Miss Coningsby made no response, save to say coldly, with great stress on the name: "Good evening, Miss Narr." Dora replied with a gentle dignity and self-re speet, and went out of the room and out of the house. . .</p>
        <p>She halted on the steps, noting a cab at the corner,. in which the young squire was awaiting her. At the same moment she marked a man lounging against the area railing of the Coningsby house—a man whose face ol low cunning, 'Ander a shock of red hair, proclaimed him to be Jack Narr I At the sight of her, Narr's face lighted up with sinister exultation. "Caught my bird the first time trying !" he cried. The old woman was right She thought you'd make for this Come along, Miss Dora. I'm your father, and if you cry out or resist me, call a p'leeceman !" He stood at the bottom of the steps and held out his arms to intercept her.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XX. A FIBB,T CHALLENGE.</p>
        <p>At first Lord Champney paid no heed to the drooping fi,gure and despairing attitude of the Lady Barbara, L s she leaned against the low atone balustrade, and watched him sail away into the brigl,t sunshine flooding the summer sea, but at last he looked at her.</p>
        <p>She caught his gaze, and held out her arms to him in a wild imploring. His lordship's lips curled savagely. A wild gleam shot from his eyes. " She thinks to call me back l" he muttered, un- der his breath. She is afraid I will harm her thrling colonel'; By Heaven ! when she looks on him again, she will look on his lifeless corpse !" He clutched the tiller so tightly that the veins started in bold relief on his hands. "I could almost curse her !" he said. "She is as fatal wid alluring as one of those false beacons the wreckers light on their treacherous shores ! And I love her! I, her husband, who am going to avenge myself upon this colonel, whose love-letter to her lies in my bosom l First, to destroy him I And then—"</p>
        <p>He set his lips together tightly, and an awful shadow fell upon his face. The woman on the cliff caught up from a bench near her a sea-glass, and scrutinized him with despairing earnestness through its magnifying lens..</p>
        <p>She marked that look and shadow on his features, and her despair deepened into a wild foreboding and terror. "There is murder in his heart 1" she thought. "Poor, noble, misguided Sidney! The colonel is an unerring shot, and as cool as a smiling demon. Be will kill Sieiney, and say he did it in self- defence 1 0, my husband! Or, Sidney may by some chance kill his enemy, and he will then be arrested for murder, our unhappy troubles become the talk of all England, and, worse than all, Sidney will suffer the penalty of his crime I I must save him—but how ?"</p>
        <p>She thought the matter over quickly. She could not go to Cromer and warn the colonel, nor beg him too flee. It wae to late to make a further appeal to Lord Champney. AlLshe could do would be to write a note to the infamous Effingham, beg- ging him to keep out of her husband's way, else murder would be done. _ -</p>
        <p>Dra-wing---a -note-book from her pocket, she hastened to write the note in guarded language on a leaf in her book, tore the leaf out and has- tened into the house for an envelope. This was soon found, the leaf inclosed and sealed, and duly addressed, and the Lady Barbara then went out into the rose-garden in search of the ga.rdener'e son, a lad of twelve, who had often served her as a page. The boy, a bright and keen little fellow, was soon found.</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara addressed him on the subject with simple directness. "My lad," she said, "you go over to Cromer twice a week. !lave you ever seen an officer there called Colonel Effingham ?" "The one with the fine uniform, my lady, and the end-black horse ?" asked the boy, eagerly.</p>
        <p>"Yes. Do you know where he 10-dges.-?" - "Yes, my lady: lie was here in the grounds to- day. I saw him myself." "I want you to go to Cromer immediately, and give this note into the hauds of Colonel Effingham. /Jake haste, my boy. lf it. reaches him in time, you shall have a bright new 'sovereign. Take the rmn horse—he's tile swiftest—and go by. the shork:st route. This is a matter ol life and death. And remember, boy, that I want you to be seevet." lier look and her ma.uuer impressed the. lad even more than her words. -</p>
        <p>En took the note, hiding it in his bosom, a.nd zvomised hpi_4izit_obedienee to her coifinkaudg.</p>
        <p>:nen no numea szarnes, saaafea -me roan horse, and in m few minute more was flying away on his errand at a break-neck speed. The Lady Barbara returned to the cliff and the sea glass. By this time the boat was well out upon the nun- lit waters. ,he baroness watched its progress with a yearning gaze. And while she was thus absorbed in plans for compassing his safety, Lord Champney was think- ing of her and Effingham with an awful bitterness and wrath.</p>
        <p>He sat in his boat as grimly as a carved figure- head; but within his soul his uncaged passions raged like so many unloosed demons. " Why," said he to himself, with a bitter scorn, nis eyes flaming, "there have been times since I flame back to Sarcair that I have doubted that woman's guilt. She's such a consummate hypocrite. And all the while she was carrying on a love cor- respondence with this Effingham, who has the worst reputation of any man in England. Why, they talk of kicking him out of the army for ruin- ing so many homes; and yet Barbara Champney, who used to be the most high-minded, pure and toble woman in the world—Barbara Champney is one oi his hundred loves. It is enough to drive a cooler man than I am- to utter frenzy. I'll kill him as I'd kill a raging wild beast." The more Lord Champney brooded over his supposed wrongs, the wilder with rage he grew. By the time his journey approached termination he was deathly calm, but none the less full of his deadly purpose. He sailed past the lighthouse, and on through Cromer Bay, or "the devil's throat," as the bay has been named by the fishermen, on account of the pel.ls of its navigation, and finally stranded upon the open beach, where two or three vessels were loading and unloading, and where a few fisher craft were drawn up, lying in the broad sunshine.</p>
        <p>Lord Champney leaped out upon the beach and strode up the nearest street, unheeding the offera of a crippled individual who offered to guide him to Vie ruins of the ancient walls and abbey, to the Tudor church, and other objects of interest, all sum incredibly f,.ud nbmrdly</p>
        <p>Cs—one who con</p>
        <p>trade that of news-dealer—Lord Champ- iwpired the address of Colonel Effingham. Ete found that thearmy officer was well known at that establishment, and had no difficulty in obtain- ing the information he sought. lie then proceeded to Effingham's hotel. As he gained it, a lad rode out of the court-yard on a wet and panting roan horse, which seemed to have been ridden hard, and which showed signs of great fatigue, despite an evident brief rest. Lord Champney recognised both horse and rider</p>
        <p>at once. The lad—who was, of course, Lady Barbara's messenger, and who had arrived at the hotel twenty minutes since, and had safely delivered her lady- ship's note—mindful of the trust reposed in him, and of the injunction to secrecy, as well as of the promised golsien sovereign, endeavoured to slink out of the baron's sight, but his horse did not move quick enough. His lordship caught the horse by the bridle, and demanded the business of the young rider. "1 mom of an arrant, my lord," was the non- committal response. "What errand?" "Can't my leddy's seamstress want a reel o' thread nor nothing ?" asked the lad, suddenly. "1 has to mom every day for Miss Ada, that I do. I an't done nothing, my lord, to be scolded on." And the wise young lad commenced to whimper. His lordship released the bridle at once. "Go on," he said, quietly. "Obey your orders. ' I have nothing to say to you." He went into the hotel, the lad, rejoicing at his escape from the master he held greatly in awe, starting for home. The baron inquired of the clerk the number of Effingham's room, and carelessly asked if the colonel were in. "He was, half an hour since, my lord," replied the clerk, with a quick guess at the identity of his questioner. "He must be upstairs now. I will send a messenger to see, my lord." "No. I will go up myself. You think he is in 2" "He is generally in at this hour, my lord. He came in half an hour ago, with his black horse rather wet, and he'll hardly go out again till even- ing, my lord." Lord Champney loiteringly departed, and made his way to the upper floor, soon finding the number of Effingham's room.. He knocked on the door. No one answered. He knocked yet more loudly, and then turned the knob. The door was unlocked. He went in. The room was unoccupied. The window was open, and the sea-breeze filled the chamber with a delicious coolness. The bed stood in a recess, shut off by dimity cur- tains, and all about the room were evidences of the colonel's. occupancy. Riding whips of varions patterns, some with jewelled handles, littered the I mantel-piece. Swords, gilt spurs, shoulder-straps, strip of gold lace and metal buttons were mingled on the table with pipes of every description, jars of tobasco, boxes of cigars and empty bottles. Half across a chair and half on the floor, as if recently cast there, was an officer's coat. "It's easy to judge the man's character by his room," thought the baron, his lips curling. He looked yet more narrowly around him. And then he perceived on the floor, where it seemed to have fallen in a hasty departure, a note —the note the Lady Barbara had written to Effing- ham to secure her husband's safety or his life. Mechanically Lord Champney picked up the note• The envelope was torn across, as if the recipient had been overcome with eagerness on seeing it. The note fell out of its torn receptacle. Still mechanically Lord Champney unfolded the hasty scrawl, written in pencil on the leaf of a note-book. As the reader knows, the missive contained only an urgent entreaty of the colonel to keep out of Charnpney's sight, lest murder be done. The baron read it through, scarcely conscious of what he was doing. "Poor girl ?" he muttered, when he had finished it "Poor Barbara! How she loves him I She was afraid I would harm him ! " He felt suddenly strengthless, and sat down by the table, toying with a silver-mounted pistol that lay amid the rubbish. And then,. with the rapidity of light, his mood changed. He seemed to wake up out of a horrible trance that held his senses since the moment of perceiving the letter. "Poor girl I" he repeated, with an infinite scorn. "Vile traitress I should have said. She has betrayed me to that wretch; and the coward, in obedience to her command, has fled. But he won't keep out of sight long. He's got fire some- where in his blood, or he could not have fought so many duels. I'll bring that fire to the surface!" He arose and went to a smaller table, on which lay an open dispatch box. Upon this box lay a sheet of tinted paper, deli- cately scented, on which was written a date, and the words, "My own Barbara." - "Ali, he had begun to write to her when the lad came, and he chose to make his escape !" exclaimed Lord Champney; scowling darkly. "I shall exact a fearful revenge for all these things !" He little dreamed that the note had been left purposely on the floor, and the dispatch-box ar- ranged to catch his eye. Be little dreamed— noble, faulty, generous soul !—that he was the dupe and plaything of a crafty enemy who knew his weaknesses, and played upon them to further Wilma purposes—to widen the breach between the hus- band and wife, that he might in some way bring himself nearer the latter. Lord Champney turned the dispatch box upside down, and emptied out its contents. Then, darkly and grimly, he examined ti,ese slowly. They had been well prepared for his eyes. A bunch of withered violets, tied together with ribbon, and labelled in tiny letters, " From the L :dy of Saltair," fiest attracted the jealous hus- band's gaze. Next a photograph of the Lady Barbara, which Effingham had procured in town of a photograph at tist whom the baroness had employed—a nefa- rious mode of gaining possession of which Lord Champney was himself too true a gentleman to suspect his enemy—came to light. 'lbis picture was set in a jewelled haute, and on the card was written : " The picture of my darling." "his darling !" hissed Lord Champney. " We'll see!" _ _ - _</p>
        <p>_ He extracted the photograph, and tore it into a hundred tiny bits. Then he flung the jewelled frame on the ground, and crushed it under his boot heel. _ _</p>
        <p>rhere were other flowers, a couple of notes, pur- porting to have been written by the Lady Barbara, in which allusion was made to the "odious chains that bound her to one whom she hated, and whose jealousies poisoned her existence."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>-1.-ney may well • poison her existence." mut- tered the unhappy husband. "The fiend! The double-faced hypocrite She will have a harder time in the future, I can promise her. I'll be a Nemesis to her—the avenger of my own murdered happiness." Raving looked over the remaining contents of the box, and found them of no importance, the baron took up the sheet on which a letter appeared to have been begun to the baroness, and wrote in large letters upon it: " VILLAIN ! LIAR COWARD! You have run away to avoid a meeting with me! Meet me to-morrow at Cranny Beach, near Saltair, at noon, and give me satis- faction at the point of the sword, or I will brand you as a COWARD ,11 LIAR and a 'imam throughout Great Britain! sesd the story of your cowardice to the papers, to your club, to your regiment I And the first time we meet I'll horsewhip you and give you the kicking_ such a dog as you deserves._" _ _</p>
        <p>Lora Champy read this challenge over with a savage glee. "That will bring him out 1" he muttered. "He prides himself on his bravery! He'll abandon love and all to save his honour. Either he or I will die to-morrow, and I don't care which," added his lordship, recklessly. "But while I live no one shall ride over me roughshod I Yes, to-morrow the Lady Barbara Chatnpney will lose either her husband or her lover 1"</p>
        <p>He took up his challenge and went to the mantel•piece. Picking up a sword from the litter on the shelf, he pinned the flaming document to the wall with the point of ie ownstitand stalked out of the room.</p>
        <p>"To-morrow I" he said to himseh, ovagely, aa he went down the stairs. " To-merroter</p>
