        <p>THE TURKISH ATItoCITIES.</p>
        <p>MASSACRES AT VAN AND EGUIN. The "Daily News " Paris correspond.ut telegraphs : I am informed by one of my erm.nian corres- pondents that the disturnances at Van on the 23rd Sep- tember were followed by a terrible massacre there on the 2nd inst. rhe first news of this last massacre came through Russia, and particulars are lacking." The following letter from Kharput, written on Septem- ber 29, confirms the account already given of the horrors enacted only a month ago. It is (says the " Daily News") from an entirely trustworthy source:— "The news which I gave last week concerning Eguin was derived chiefiv from two candid Turks who were there at the time of the massacre. All the letters which I have seen agree in fixing the estimate at about 2,000 as the number of the killed. More women and children, in proportion, seem to have suffered this fate than in any previous massacre of which I know. Many of the dead were left in the streets for days asthe food for dogs, and large numbers were thrown into the Euphrates. They have been seen floating down the river forty or fifty miles below the city. In som cases whole families have been °la:iterated. Exact statistics. of course, can- not be given now,but it is feared that 2,000 is an under- estimate.. Tnis is a large proportion in an estimated population of between 5.000 and 6,000 Christians. The letters give the number of houses as 1,100, and of these ,it is said that only about 150 are left. This carnage if blood and fire lasted from Tuesday the 15th to Thursday the 17th. All the testimony concurs in showing that the massacre was official, and that it was wholly without reason. There was no disturbing element, except in the imagination of a few officials. They had alarmed the central government. The Vali and the military comman- der were in the telegraph office here most of the time from the beginning to the end, communicating with Egnin and Constantinople. As tar as I can learn, the people made no resistance whatever, and no Turks were killed, except possibly later, in i he division of the spoils. There were no Kurds in the place. The work was done by citizens and soliders. The massacre extended to several of the Eguin villages, but we have no details. The local Government is constant in its assurance that no further massacres will take place in this region, and a good deal of energy is shown in restraining the turbul- ent elements. Five people were killed in the Agbun vil- lages, one of them a priest, but the timely arrival of the eAldiers prevented a general massacre. After all that has happened, it is not surprising that the Christians are I unnerved by fear. The destitution of the ccming winter threatens to be almost as great as last year."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>HOW THE WORKING MAN DINES.</head>
        <p>HOW THE WORKING MAN DINES.</p>
        <p>A "Little Cough?'</p>
