      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>UATITAL " GHAT.</head>
        <p>UATITAL " GHAT.</p>
        <p>l!r.• Wit Istone has aftsin spoken. His letter, *lnch was read by the 'ishop of Rochester at the great Armenian demosstration,produced almost the Istie magical ,ffect tt,.at a speech would have had Ott rho packud audience. An electic thrill of eYMpatby rat ihringh the vast assembly, and a ?eland of applause was inOulged in, such as I have Seldom heard t-xcel!d. The letter marks,of course, conMderable expansion of the views expressed by Mr. Gladstone in his own speech at Liverpool. It lintis up, in a t-emarkably clear manner, the differ- ences between the two great bodies of opinion on th'e Armenian. question.</p>
        <p>The probability that Sir Julian Pounce-foto took Vat with hitm t he other day some new proposals for lidjusting elle Venezuelan difficulty is strengthened ,IIY the news from Washington. It appears that Mr. Miamberlain turned his American visit to good ac- tount, arid was able to suggest to Lerd Salisbury What he had ascertained would be aceptable to the Amcrican Government. An early announce- Ment of an agr-ement is,therefore, highly probable, isquite expected at Washington. The news Will be heartily welcomed by the kpatriots of the to interestod countries.</p>
        <p>Pour more generals have just left Spain for Cuba thi,s brings up the standing army of generals to ithoat thirty-si-ven. If too many cooks can spoil the broth, what will thirty-seven generals do in a taMpaign ? The insurgents, we may take it, will ,Ilot be one penny the worse. A journalist who &amp;lately returned o town from -Cuba did not bring ck a very good opinion ,Of ,General Vieyler, the panish Commander-in-Chief. He appears to be a °loodthirsty ruffian., with an absolute genius for uevising excuses to keep himself out of harm's bay,</p>
        <p>• On the top of the news that Dr. Jameson is seek- ,lnß consolation for his mishaps in the religion of sitiddha comes further intelligence that he and Sir Uohn Willouszbhy vill short2y be released. There ,18 more truth in the lattevreport than the farmer. Pr. Jameson is bearing he sedentary life very oadly,and his couspanion-in-arms is even worse off, for he is never suite out erf the doctor's hands. The ra atter has.l believe,already come before the 'Home Becretary,but it is understood that he does not feel Justified at present in advising any remission of the sentence. The Dowager Countess of tDudley Continues to take ,considerable interest in Dr. Jameson's vae4fare. The new ladies' paper, whose editresa.ought to know ,dll about these thinge,boldly prophesies a ineariage as soon as Holloway is un- locked.</p>
        <p>I understand that ,quite a number of telegrams were sent on Monday to Sir William Harcourt at lialwood, asking for inZormation about his alleged letter. fie,plainly replied that the letter was a forgery. Some persons still regard it as a very fir!iusingloax:; nut ,if the perpetrator is,caught, he Nsi.ll have a tad time el it. Not so many ears ago London 'paper .waOhoaxed on a very &lt;large scale, actually -paying something like .£2O. for what was thought to be priceleas information. The joker did zlot leave his name and aJdress, but a 'member of 11.1 e staff pan across him in the street one day, and, 111 'my m.mory -ervesitne, he did a month's ha, i la- our shortly afterwards.</p>
        <p>One small incident of the proceedings at Colches- 14r on Tuesday afternoon is worth noting. The hittrangement of the toast list brought Mr. Willis, lei to his feet Imost the moment that Lord ...."•08ebery sat down. The situation was an awk- Vird one. *Mr:Willis is strongly opposed to Lord ,coseberry's policy on the Armenian c iestion ; in- nted, it was statedlthst it was Mr. Willis who in- rarked the ,4peech at Edinburgh with cries of iiShame.." After -a moment's hesitation he was ;one the less outspoken and se promptly Qum sed the effect of Lord Rosebery's psech 'by repudiating its sentiment. 'Mr. Willis is 2eally the most Ipopular exponent of' Liberal feel- "lß and .principltis, and his open revolt made a %larked 'impression.</p>
        <p>_I bear that the Soy. Canon Mrrceoll purposes %king steps at an-early date to relieve himself of disabilities,,and so quality as ,g, Parliamen- CrY candidate. The Canon is,nf course, an enthus- ;Mt in the Armenian cause, and has4s large know- -1.6410 01 Kastern ffairs. On general politics he is, 140Weiver,a little undecided. Although usually rec- ‘oned a•Liberal.he did not scruple to support the _son of ;his old 'friend the Marquis 6f Bath, Lord Mward'Thynne. when the latter stood in the Tory terest at the recent by-election in the Frome drvision. For this he has not been forgiven by avient and attire Liberals. As is known, of i°' Maceoll enjoys the personal friendship both ti talisbury and Mr. Gladstone.</p>
        <p>Th -ere was no doubting the earne-tuess of the .Teeting held on behdif of the Armenians on Tues.. ItY night in the East-end. but Mr. Bean relieved 4‘B seriocen.iss by the relation of an incident at once itoteettue and pathetic. At a Salvation Array 111,, Beting recently, he .said, a fervent. -Salvationist rayed, with all the intensity of hix faith,that God 'Quid 44 break the Sultan's heart," with a view of tune to the conversion of the barbarous Abdul. t; e prayer was interrupted by .an additional peti- Ott from one of the devout company, cud it ran "Mg : "Oh, God I 'break his nPck." The vast tene,. of workingmen sent up a roar of appre- mktive laugshter at the story.</p>
        <p>he cab atrike is about to develop sovery curious Itlestion—mmely, whether a cabman is bound to Z!'re his fare inside, and set Lim down inside, a ttlWay terminus. As the Home Secretary has de- teed a terminus to be private property, lie leaders of the strike say thate a cabman is no I:re bound to firive his fare irte the private 4 °Party of a railway compan,7 than he is bound to tiliiVe it into a shed or the hall (A a mansion When lead Hrs of the strike, therefore,issue their order; 4,e passenger and his fare will simply be deposited D;teide the station, and will be left to get his lug- ON conveyed insi e the best way he can. At all the best termini, this will cause the pub- 14e, MI immense deal of inconvenience until the corn- have succeeded in grappling with this new ifa tk en tv. which they will easily do by augmenting Cir staff of porters. Unemployed men in myriads irp!!l be competing for the work, and the companies %Ili not allow themselves to be beaten by this new tre• But the point is, that the companis.s will ,leeutp the cabmen, and a judicial decision will lb..Airen on the question, which is both novel and</p>
        <p>-- Lhe story nbeut the Queen not liking type-written Umenta is rather curious. It is said that she htl,not read them so well as boldly written papers. q's Statement accords with some printing-house toPeriences, where type-written matter costs more t.,,e(lropose than ordinary writing,heeeuse composi- V eannet follow it so well and refix the ayes on ptetllaces last left to look at the "c.lse." Scientific kUlists have made no deliverance on type-written ter that I know of, though some papers are N4lred much too closely with letters and lines for 44 V reading. Type-writing would be considerably thleted if i were to be settled that the eyes were quarrassed or in3ured by it. Apparently same- of the kind occurs in the case of the Queen, 1)111f) has given instructions that no type-written Ars shall be laid before her.</p>
        <p>Ivb addition to all that has been already spent in tipZurou on providing a Sufficiency of accommoda- tot lunaties, the Asylums Committee or the 010,.4tY Council contemplate laying out a million 111 re during the next five years in building new lending old asylums. At present there are itie fewer beds than there are lunatics in London, Itlt, 41' the current average rate of t increase of kiNtriv thPre should be. when the additional accom- ,l balion is provided, 500 beds abc:vp the number 'ThAties At the end of last vu.ar there ware 1, - - u lunatic patients, and at the end of five years vtol;,,e may he expected to be 4,500 more. In other it4%;ll'. it will cost a million of ennital to provide 444 houseroom for these 4,500 sufferers, and to the ttht'en on this million must be act-,e(l. he annual NI of the maintenance of the buildings and tF, and the staff. The existing asylums are 15,;;N7 undFrmenned, as the day attendants have 141 "Tic 14 hours daily per week of six days, and itght attendants ten hours; and as such a con- f slavery cannot last under an exemplary goti ic hotly, there will soon be a considerable addi- . °the cost on this head also.</p>
