        <p>4</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>A becoming hat fat a young lady is of pink felt, with a crown of black velvet, and a black and pink bird on the left side, and black velvet bows appear at the back. A pretty toque for a young matron has the shape of buckram, perfectly plain, like an oblong skull-cap. Line it round with a strip of sarcenet or soft silk wide enough to meet ix the centre when drawn up with a thread. Stitch this round the edge, and leave it loose until the hat is finished. Bind the edge with narrow bias velvet. Now, for the crown, get a quarter of a yard of emerald-green satin, cut this in two and join it, making almost a square piece. Press the seam flat, and then embroider the piece with a light design in gold thread, studded with tiny jet or steel sequins or jewels. Line it with thin musiin. shape off the corners, and pleat it on to the shape, forming the crown. Now take three-eighths of a yard ot green miroir velvet, and tat a band, the width of the velvet being the length. Line wah book muslin, slip-stitching the edges on to the muslin. Gather at each end and in the centre. You will then have the folded band or corona' which forms the brim. The cuttings from the velvet will form a pretty bow for the back, and when this is fixed, adjust two bright petunia, pink, or red velvet flowers on each side; and a• black feather tuft and white osprey at the left side towards the front completes the chapeau. Last of all, draw up the lining with a silken thread and tie it securely.</p>
        <p>A nice oodt-ume &amp;as 'the akirt of brown aibcth, lined with turquoise velvet and with mink-tail fur. The vest !is df tttuquoise ••velvet held in by a ceintare of black satin •ribbon, which fastens at the side with a long, antique silver buckle. A particularly smart gown can be made in a dark-red silk and wool mixture:; the skirt should the per- fectly plain, and mounted on black linen, and finished with a black silk .bilayeuse. The 'vest is of cream-coloured silk, the revers of yellow guipure divided by a tiny band •of skunk or other dark brown fur. The belt is of black satin, with jet or paste buttons, and the collar and yoke are gibe of satin, the klatter finished with jet ornaments and pendants. If required as an outdoor toilette under a cape, this collar and vest could be made separately, end only putoon as required, but in any ease the high-satin belt is'quite distinct, and can be worn with other gowns. The bodice fastens down Use righteide,under thetfur border, buttif the vest</p>
        <p>is neparste, tfkis. can' hewn-He entire, ',avid to fasten at the back, and the bnilice may hoot: down the centre. The -.sleeves are tight fitting nearly to the top, nod there kis.a high, round pouff, ,defaich is put on after the sleeve is !nude. This .gown is also &amp;accent' ig in a deep bluiSh-green, having a shot blue and green effect, and trimmed ecru lace,,a primrose silk vest and bnawn fur. Allow threerds of six- inch lace, one and a-quarter fur, about hell an inch in width, half apard of soft silk for the vest, and three-quarters ef .a yard of black satin. Nrit,h two jet ornaments, and nee yard df two-inoh lace to finish the neck and waist, and thmee jet, buttons. To line the skirt, allow four and .a-balf ya.rds Of double- width linenette, and three and a-hall yards of can- vas horsehair for stiffening it, and the . aarmal two yards of bodice lining, .and &amp;are yard •oldaiszted silk for the balayeuse.</p>
        <p>Capes and coats have of late commanded con- siderable attention ; but now, however, ulsters demand some attention, for they will be very popular during tlie winter. One ulster is made of fine fawn box cloth, with lapels of brown velvet edged with narrow skunk. The Medici collar is faced with vel,,i4„ and edged with the fur, and the gauntlet cuffs are also of skinik. The back and sides are elose-iitting, the `illness at the back com- mencing just below tne waist, and spreading into handsome folds, and the, straight double-breasted front is finished at each side with a broad strap- ping, machine-stitched. Below the buttons it is fastened invisibly under the strapped band with safety hooks and eyes. Another handsome long travelling cloak is in soft brown vicuna, and has a novel and becoming painted cape and high zollar. Your loving JENNIN.</p>
