      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The Duchess of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha,better known Perhaps by her old title the Duchess of Edinburgh,who :ecently celebrated her birthday is at the age of forty- hree, the grandmother of several little children, for he has thought it well to allow her daughters to marry very much younger than Ow herself was wed. The Duchess who is a s:ster of the late Emperor of Russia and •iunt of the present Emperor, was twenty-one, when she became the wife of Prince Alfred. Duke of Edinburgh.</p>
        <p>A picture, in Sine of the New York papers just to hand, representing, or to be correct supposed to repre- sent the latest hat worn by the young Duchess of Marl- borough, is decidedly an imposing one. The writer of the article accompanying it described the structure at length. Fully a dozen plumes are used to decorate it, and the frame is a light openwork affair covered with folds, knots, and bows of violet velvet ! It is somewhat late in the season to indulge in " openwork " hats, the stoutest hearted among us %%mild scarcely like to venture upon such a form of headgear, even though we covered up the openwork with various forms of trimming, but doubtless the picture and the description thereof, is ctor• rectness itself. . . . .</p>
        <p>It is hoped that there will be great rejoicings at Blenheim shortly, and if it proves a Marquis of Bland. ford who is to be welcomed to this historic house, our Transatlantic cousins will indeed be able to lay claim to an" American lord." He will bring America nearer to the British aristocracy than it has ever been before. And to be born heir to a dukedom like Marlborough, a home like Blenheim, and a Vanderbilt fortune, is indeed to he barn with a golden spoon set with diamonds! When Mrs.Heary Wood was at the height of ber fame she issued a series of stories anonymously, to satisfy herself if she was trading on her old reputation, or if her fresh work pleased the world as much as her old. An- other writer has followed her example. For some time the stories of a new author "David Lyall " have been attracting considerable attention, and it now appears that the "new author" is no other than Miss Annie Swan, long known as a popular domestic Scottish novel- ' ist. Two or three years ago this lady married a young Scotch doctor, and is living in London where her hus- band practices his profession. With regard to the adoption of the decimal system for clocks and watches the actual originator of the appli- cation of this system to time, was a Frankfort watch- maker who nearly three years ago constructed two " decimal watches" in one of which the day Was divided into Ltzn "hours," and in the other twenty. In both instances the" hours" were divided into a handred " minutes " and the " minutes " into a hundred "seconds," just as in the case of the watches now in use by the Geographical Bureau of the French army. Speaking of clocks, Lord Grimthorpe has one of the most complete workshops in the world, regularly em- ploying several accomplished journeymen to carry out the rougher part of his experiments, and one of them once said that " Lord Grimthorpe could do anything with clocks but make them talk." Lord Marsharn too, who is the holder of more patents in industrial machin- ery than any other man in England, also used, in the workshops attached to his country seat,and in which he started work for years at five in the morning, to experi- ment in clockmaking. Madame Sara Bernhardt has given her name to the latest caprice in the line of lingerie. It is a nightdress, made in a fluffy voluminous style which is eminently suggestive of the flowing robes of the tragedienne. The gown has its fullness gathered at the waist in such a way as to give a short-waisted Empire effect, and the sleeves are very full. It is to be•seen now in many of the lead- ing London shops, built of Indian lawn, china silk, or flannel. By the way it does not appear to be generally known that Madame Bernhardt is a total abstainer. She has ni.ver taken wine or any intoxicants and to this she largely attributes the remarkable preservation of her youthful appearance, mental vigour, and power. She drinks only a little tea or coffee, and confines her bever- ages mostly to milk, and water. Warnings have just been issued by the leading medical societies of Paris, Berlin and Vienna, against the evils of excessive coffee drinking. It seems that the symptoms of coffee drunkenness are so similar to ordin- ary inebriety that physicians constantly diagnose coffee poisoning as alcoholic disturbance :—lnsc Dania, nausea, lack of appetite and depression of spirits are among the symptoms of coffee-poisoning. and the effort on the part of the confirmed coffee drinker to become a total ab- stainer is similar in result to that which follows the abandonment of alcoholic liquors, the nervous system being terribly wrenched. This is all, doubtless:; per- fectly true, but taken in moderation like everything else, coffee is not likely to do us any harm. Squareness, is a feature of the latest thing. Collars whether of the flat or the up-standing variety are all finished sqf squarely, reifers are square, and square tabs finish off the basques of coats. All the rounded methods seem to be despised,even the rounding off of little tailor- built coats in front is now superceded by uncompromis- ing squareness. The new invention in the way of imitation chinchilla, is a distinct success, especially in the form of narrow bands for trimming. To those of as who admire the real article, but not the price of it, this item of Mews will be a welcome one. It should be tried against the com- plexion however, if it be destined to trim the collar or revers, as it is by no means all women who can wear chinahilla or its counterfeit presentment successftfily.</p>
        <p>..... _ It appears that at Rheims and Fissites, artificial silk is shortly to be manufactured. The silk is called "•Char- donnet" after the name of its inventor., and it is asserted that the onswito operandi is somewhat similar to that of the silkworm itself. Whether the fabric will,prove as satisfactory as the genuine article remains to be seen.</p>
        <p>_ At the present time Bradford is balding its own most comfortably with fancy woollen dress materials mixed with silk, and it will be satisfactory to patriotic persons to know that the home produce can challenge foreign competition. If only British people wouldlbuy •British materials, what a delightful turn of thetide would rssulL for workers in Bradford looms, at West of England cloths and serges, and in Welsh and Scotch and 'lrish manufactures. What a flood of comfort and prosperity would pour into thousands of poor homes which other- wise have nothing but a winter of hardships, followed by a spring of nipping cold, to look forward to. And there is not one atom of Felf-sacrifice involved, for the fabrics are just as dainty, pretty and good, durable and cheap, as tkcee which pour into our markets from abroad. _ _</p>
        <p>In every family especially where there are little folks, small burns ocoxr sometimes,which if not treated poperly may result in a slow healing sore that is most trouble- some. The ,main thing to remember is, that tbe burn Amid be shut away from the air. Anything that , shuts out the air is good but some applications aretet- ter than others. if possible the burn shsuld'be covered with a thick smearing of vaeeline, and a thin layer of wadding; failing this a mixture of unsalted lard and flour is a homely and very exc6llent remedy that is generally at hand. Potatoes baked h thair skins should hare a piece cut off the ends before takine., in order that the steam may escape. When boiled they may be prepared in the same way, and the skin •ret.noved just before serving. Pota- toes are ,ungyeestionably dryer and aner in vour when boiled in this way tLan when pared before cooking. Great care however is necessary to prevent their being cold when eaten, as tlafy grow cold rapidly after the skins are removed and of all things potatoes should be hot when serwod.</p>
        <p>"MIDGE."</p>
      </div>
