        <p>too much tea and eat summer. The tannic a excess of flesh food DR. TIBBLES' VI-CC Malt, and Hops, has the the nourishment of the b tive force possessed by neii ful food beverage. Sold e Is. 6d. tins; or on mex a postcard will do— DR, Suffolk House, C a dainty sample tin will b sent post free,</p>
        <p>WA.RRIN(iTON LITER A.RY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the above society for the session 1896-97 was held on Monday evening, in the Mus/um Lecture Room, when the attendance of both ladis and gentlemen, was larger than usual. Mr.J.Ryrner Young is the president, but as he had to read a paper the chair for the evening was taken by the late President (Mr. William Owen). The EION.SECRETARY (Mr. J. A.Edelsten) made several announcements, in the course of which he expressed a hope that the membership of the soc?etv would be largely increased during the wint,r months. Their pro- graMMe, strong as it was, was almost entirely owing to non-members of the society.</p>
        <p>• +. • • •• • • 0 • es • •+•- +-° •+• • • + +•+•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ROMAN ALTAR _FOUND AT WILDERSPOOL.</head>
        <p>THE ROMAN ALTAR _FOUND AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>Mr. OWEN said he would call upon Mr. "fnomas May to make some observations with reference to the Roman altar recently discovered at Wilderspool, and which was being exhibited in the room that evening (Applause.) Mr. MAY said: The spot where this altar was found is just Within the new borough boundary at Wilderspool, and I am glad to be able to congratulate the people of Warrington on the acqifisitien of their new territory, consecrated by the discovery of this sired relic of anti- quity. You will recollect that Earl Grosvenor, father of the present Duke of Westminster, on a 'Roman altar being found in the vicinity of Eaton Hail, erected a temple in his grounds on purpose to receive it. The locality near Warrington is on a summit of the Roman road, overlooking the country round about, and which was probably the site of a temple close by the trench in which the altar now shown was buried. What is, in my opinion, mere interesting and important is the discovery of absolute and indub- itable evidence of the existence of the station at Wildersnool down to the middle of the fourth century. Those who are acquainted with the writings of Whitaker, Ormerod, Beament, Kendrick, and the more recent baok of W. Thompson Watk•n, on "Roman Chrshire," will understand what this signifies. It proves that the station was in continuous existence a century and a half longer than was previously supposed by these authorities ; and it renders the probability much greater that this was the station referred to by the choro• grapher, Ravennas, as Veratino. In the most ancient records the spelling of the name of our town was Werynton. It is possible.therefere, that the connection between the Roman station and Warrington has hren continuous from the time of the Roman Conquest. I again congratulate the people of Warrington on the re- covery of their ancient inheritance and the title deeds to so noble an antiquity. (Appianse.) THE PAPER.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT, who was greeted with applause, then proceeded to read his paper, which was entitled "From Alchyrny to Chymistry," and was historically sketched in a very able manner. There were also some excellent lantern illustrations. Mr. Young iemarked at the outset that the ancients appeared ta have made but little pro- gresa in any of the natural sciences. They divided all human knowledge into three parts, logic, physics and ethics—mental, natural, and moral philosophy. The cynics renounced all science, seeking freedom from any object in life. Socrates regarded observation as un- necessary, since truth could be attained by thought— knowledge obtained from external sources is worthless— there is nothing to be learned from fields and trees," said he. On that principle a philosopher was said to have put out his eyeseio that his mind might be uninflu- enced by external objects,and so left to pure contempla- tion. In singular contrast with the plaint of poor Galileo,just before his death proh dolor : " The sight of my right eye.that eye whose labours,l dare say it, have had such glorious results, is for ever lost." Si it hap- pens that a schoolboy of twelve nowadays knows more about fire, water, and earth than the greatest philoso- phers of antiquity. In the days of Aristotle the investi- gation of nature was in every possible way discouraged ; at one period it was considered impious to explain the manifestations of the gods. There were riots in Athens because some adventurous philosopher had dared to speak of the thunderbolt of Zeus—Lord of the Air—as merely common fire, produced by the collision of clouds. For a very long time, therefore, any investigation of nature was impossible fer religious reasons—men were to worship nature. men were to be filled with awe and wonder, but not to inquire too closely into natural phenomena : 2,000 years later, the Salamanca doctors who questioned Columbus, and the inquisitors of the Sacred College who examined Galileo, upheld the same old ioctrinee, thnuah the old gods had passed away long before. Even in modern times there was a martyrdom of science for such as ventured too far from the common-Place and orthodox ; they were not so old as to have forgotten how Darwin,by his writings and demonstrations,brought upon himself all the ridicule and satire that fanaticism could suggest, and yet he sup- posed that at the present moment there were few edu- cated men who would have the temerity to challenge the general principle of evolution in the animal kingdom, or to contradict the assertion of Galileo that the earth did move. The science oh chemistry did not owe its exist- ' ence to any one people, or to any sudden process of de- velopment, but was the outceme of a prolonged i,rocess of evolution. The time when the foundation-stone was laid was too remote even for conjecture, the basis of the edifice was sunk deep in Eastern soil, for there was ample proof that the Chinese had some know- ledge of alchytny centuries besore the Christian era. The theory which effirmed that the world was com- posed of four elements, earth, air, fire and watee„ was probably the oldest physical theory of which they had any record. It certainly existed before the 15'h century !Pee., and was adopted in Egypt and Greece at a very early date. After a reference to the seven metale, and to the origin of Alchymv, to Roger Bacon " one of the most distinguished and philosophical of the alchymists," to Raymond Lulil,and others, Mr. Young proceeded : What manner of men were the alchyrn- ists,? and how did they preserve and trans- mit the wonderful delusions of their creed As to the men themselves, their character was two-fold. There was the alchymist proper, the true entlausiast,the ardent persevering worker, who believed heart and soul that gold could be made, and that by long search and close study the philosopher's stone was attainable ; and there was the knavieh slchymist, the man who had wits enough to perceive that the search was futile and impu- dence enough to fool credulous people and wheedle their fortunes out of them on pretence of reurning it a hundred fold in the shape of a recipe for changing lead into gold. From the fourteenth to the sixteenth century supernat- ural beliefs exerted a powerful influence on the people, and during this period of appalling darkness many of the great alchytnists dabbled in magic as well as astrol- ogy, and had a general idea that in order to obtain the intervention of Satan in human affairs it was necessary to enter into a compact with him. Those who went to this length, and became exponents of the black art.were initiated with much solemnity. The oath to the demon had to be pronounced in the centre of a circle traced upon the ground, heavy perfumes, with burning incense, lighted tapers and smoking brazier, entered largely into the ritual ; belladonna and opium were always used as , ingredients of the incense in order to pror'utie a state of semi-stupor, and influence the imagination. The per- fumes employed had each a spacial significance, and were offered to some planet to form a link with the earth. A mixture of saffron, amber and musk, together with the brain of an eagle, and the blood of a cock, were offered to the sun; white poppies and camphor burnt in the head of a frog, with the eyes of a bull and the blood of a goose., were the correct delicacies to propitiate the moon ; whilst Mars had to be content with brimstone mixed, not with treacle, hot wiris the hrsin of a cow, some blood of a Hack cat, and a little hellebore by way of seasoning. One can readily imagine the terrible odour that would he caused by the cremation of such dainties as these, and as the dense volume of smoke diffused and enveloped the scared, half-choked half-stu pi fled devotee, he would see, or imagine he saw, as many writhing forms of fiends and demons as would last him for the rest of his natural life. It may he said that these alchymists were in the main moved by no very noble ambition ; their chief object was to find a way to make gold—a desire indeed not yet extinct by any means. In modern times we simply differ as to method. Sem. have tried te find wealth in Ship Canal shares and, like the alchyinist of old, wete doomed to dieappuintment ; others have found a verit- able gold mine in such concerns as Brunner, Mond and Co., and in stock exchange speculation many have won—many more have lost. Well, se we know, the alchymists never achieved the great hope of their lives, hut in their search for it they achieved much that was far better ; they desired, as Sheridan puts it—</p>
        <p>Not to show occurrences that Happen every day but things just so Strange that though they Never did, they might happen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <p>nuch flesh food, especially in in tea lowers the spirits, and sates against working energy. lA, prepared from Kola, Cocoa, :oshing properties of fine teas, Dcoas, and a tonic and recupera- Not a medicine, but a wonder- vhere in 6d. packets and 9d. and ing this paper and writing— BBLES' VI-COCOA, Ltd., on St., London, E.C.,</p>
        <p>• AO • • •••• • • S • 0 • • , So</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>13LEs 'IIB</head>
        <p>13LEs 'IIB</p>
        <p>-Cocoa</p>
        <p>They looked for gold and found sulphuric acid ; they sought for the elixir that should confer perennial youth, and in their failures laid the foundations of a mighty science. Alchymy is in very truth dead, but, Phoenix • like, from its ashes has arisen modern chymistry. The cold realities with which the old dream,ql were so disap- pointed were, in the truest and best sense, blessings in disguise, though they knew it not. Mr. YOUNG was warmly applauded at the conclusion of his lecture, which throughout was listened to with considerable interest. Toe CHAIRMAN said their best thanks were due to the President, and he would give hitnielf the pleas re of proposing the formal resolution of thanks on behalf of the meeting. _ _ _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. RORERT DAVIES seconded, and -aid he had enjiyed the paper almost more than any paper he had heard for a long time. When he was young he used to read the writings of the alchytnists, and tried to learn the reason w ich prompted them to mike so many experiments. They could not help feeling sympathy for them whilst they were working in ignorance amongst the secrets of nature. The telephone and other inventions of the pre- sent time were far greater than that of transmuting metals, as they enabled them to talk to their relatives at the other side of the world. It would have been impos- nible for anyone to have more cleverly condensed such a vast amount of information than Mr. Young had done. Mr. CHARLES Warrirsupported the motion, and re- ferred to Mr. Edelsten's remark that the majority of the papers were being contributed by non-members of the society. He thought that alter the excellent paper which had been read to them that evening by Mr. Young it was a pity that more local talent had not been brought into requisition. Alluding to the alchymists, he said they started in the wrong place, but their motive must be respected, and they certainly led to the wonderful discoveries which adorned the Victorian ora. (Applause.) The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND briefly supported the motion, and referred to the rem trkable fact that the alchymists,unlike many others in the middle ages,almost completely escaped from the hands of the Inquisition. He believed that that was largely due to the tact that the real Churchmen were themselves alchymists. The paper which Mr. Young had read to them must have cost him much trouble and research. (Hear, hear). 'The motion was then put to the meeting and heartily agreed to, and Mr. Young having returned thanks the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>POST OFFICE ItOMA.NCE.</head>
        <p>A POST OFFICE ItOMA.NCE.</p>
        <p>A" North Wales Observer" reporter investigated a re- markable occurrence at Newmarket Post office, near Rhyl. A tall rosy-cheoked young woman received him, and on his enquiring for Miss Savage, said that was her name.</p>
        <p>She told the reporter that about midsummer of 1895, she was taken so ill that she could hardly walk ten yards,nor could she even go upstairs without stop- ping many times,her complaint being poorness of blood. She could not Bleep nor take her food, and for many months was in this state of ill-health. A doctor pre- scribed for her, but she found little or no relief. A lady friend advised her to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills,as they had proved so beneficial in countless similar cases, and Miss Savage obtained a box, and took the Pills regularly after meals. To her surprise and deliglat,before the box was half empty she felt better. Her appetite quickly came back, and she was able to go about the house ; in fact,she was quite recovered. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, she said, deserved every praise, and she was happy to state that they had saved her from death's door, The whole village, said Miss Savage's mother, who was present, " can te3tify that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have wrought a wonderful cure in my daughter's case, and a neighbour of mine has a daughter who derived wonderful btmefit by taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills on the advice of her doctor. This recognition of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills by the medical faculty is important. The Pills are famous for enriching and purifying the blood ; they cure bloodlessness, rheumatism, scrofula, and restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health. They are also a splendid nerve and spinal tonic, and thus have cured many cases of paralysis, locomotor ataxy, neuralgia, St. Vitus dance,and nervous headache. They are now obtainable of all chemists, and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46. Holborn-viaduct, Lon- don, at 21. 91. a box, or six for 13. 91., but are genuine only withfull name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Daring a o week ended Friday, Oct6ber 16, 120 sea- going cargo vevsels, with an aggregate net registered tonnage of 36,566 tons, entered and sailed from the port oi Manchester, as comp tred with 118 vessels of 36,944 ton' in the preceding seven days. Tnirty-one vessels of 18,277 tons arrived from or cleared for forei n ports, and 89 vessels of 18,289 tons entered or sailed coastwise. Two cargoes of cotton, &amp;?.., arrived from Texas and Georgia; five of timber, wood pulp, and general mer- chandise from the Baltic ; two general cargoes from Rouen, two from Rotterdam, one each from Paris, Bou- logne, Treport, Ghent,and Antwerp, and Hamburg; and a cargo of green and dried fruit from Spain. The Dub- lin steamer landed 191 head of cattle, nine horses and 80 sheep at Trafford Wharf on Friday. Two steamers left for Rotterdam, two for Rouen, one for Paris, one for Treport, one for Antwerp, one for Hamburg, and one for Copenhagen, all with miscellaneous cargoes. A cargo of pitch was exported to Cette, and five sailing ships left the canal for New York, Sydney (Cape Breton), Char- lotte Town (Prince El ward Island), Riisoer, and Chris- tiania respectively. There are at present no fewer than twenty-four steamers, each of over 1,000 tons net regis- ter, bound and fixed for Manchester with cotton, &amp;c.. from America and Egypt, fourteen steamers and eigh- teen sailing ve,sels with wood pulp, timber, logvrood, from the Baltic, Canada,the United S".ates,and West lodies ; and several steamers with fruit from the Medi- teranean,ore from Spain, and produce from Australia. 'THE SEPTEMBER TRAFFIC RETURNS.</p>
        <p>Alti.ough the volume of merch indise carried by vessels navigating the Snip Canal last month shows a slight falling off as compared with August. and a somewhat greater falling off as compared with July, a comparison of the returns with those for the corresponding period of last year will satisfy the most impatient critic that the trade of the port is growing at a really remarkable pace. Taking the returns month by month, experience shows that July is normally one of the best months of the year owing to certain seasonal trades being then at their height, wherev; August and September usually show a failing off from the midsummer high-water :nark. This may readily be seen from the following table : Total Weight of Traffic (Tons). Increase. 1895. 1896. Per cent. ,Enly August September... 121 398</p>
        <p>Total ...... 371.807 504,732 36.7 Total Receipts 1395. 1896. Increase. £ £ Pr cent. jalv .. ....... 12.504 ' August 12,591 Septembe:... 11,615</p>
        <p>Total Taking the first three-quarters of the current year into cPinparison with the corresponding period of last year an increase of 36 per cent. is shown in the weight of cargo passing along the canal, and an increase of 30 per cent.in the receipts. If this rate of increase can be con- tin red the revenue of the waterwlv will be quadrupled in five years, a circumstance which, though not imme- diately helpful in present embarrassments, shows that the ultimate solvency ni the undertaking may not be so remote inrime as is commonly supposed.</p>
      </div>
