      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>THE WAR SCIRE OF 1573. FRANCE'S DEBT OF GRATITUDE TO QUEEN VICTORIA.</p>
        <p>The Paris correspondent. of the " Daily News " gives the tollowing resume of, and comment on, an article in M. Clemenceau's journal on the war scare of 1875, and the service rendered to France on that occasion by the Ent,lish Queen :—" Do you know," M. Clemenceau asks his readers, " that Queen Victoria interfered in 1875, just like the Czar, to prevent Germany from declaring war on France ?" Of course his readers do not know, any more than the readers Of any other French piper. It is a good deal the fashion here to deny England any justice or generosity in order more freely to form hostile combinations against her. These qualities are supposed to be peculiar to natives of this country or to her northern ally. Ignorance and misconception of Eng- land's attitude are like fanaticism in that they resent enlightenment. Suggestion and suppression are the means by which the French public have become " in- sular " as Englishmen never were towards their neigh- bours. M.Clemonceau is nothing if not a plucky man ,aud it requtres no mean courage on his part to remind his countrymen that if France is still alive to talk of Egypt and the Transvaal, it is due to Queen Victoria as much as to the Emperor Alexander, and more so. This point of history is established by the letter from Prince Bis- marck to the Emperor William dated Varzin, August 13, 1875, and already published. "This letter," says the editor of the " Justice," " afford's evidence of Queen Victoria's action in direct opposition to the original de- signs of the blood-and. iron Chancellor. He shows it in such bad temper that the whole letter, so aggressive as to be insolent towards the Queen, is as much as a con- fession of his treachery. The document has been cleverly worded with an eye to future publication, and it throws such a flood of light that no interested denials can weaken it." As regards the late Czar's interference in favour of France, the evidence, as M. Cleisienceau re- marks, is of the vaguest sort. The important passage of M. Clemenceates article is an acknowledgement ot the service rendered to France by Queen Victoria and the British Government. What a pity no French paper will ever quote it. "As for the attitude of Queen Victoria and the British Government in this affair " (he writes), " I believe there is not a Frenchman who can escape the duty of recognising it as just as praiseworthy as the attitude for wnich our papers have 90 often thanked the Czar. In fact, we have evi- dence about the conduct of the British Government at that time which is entirely lacking with regard to the action of St. Petersburg. Now, we know that the Queen's Ministers, being approached unofficially—offici- ally would have been a little too strong—by Count Munster on the attempt on France,prepared without any other warning—so sure were they of Bismarck's inten- tions—to invite the Powers to use joint pressure on Germany to prevent this cruel breach of the peace. That Prince Bismarck thought it an unfriendly step (vide his letter) we cannot be surprised. The Emperor William must have been disagreeably surprised when, having met Queen Victoria's first letter expressing her anxiety with reassuring answer his Imperial denial merely brought a retort from his Royal correspondent that she could offer to prove her assertions. Mr. L. J. Maxse having complained in the "National Review" that no French paper has yet condescended to quote erince Bismarck's letter, M. Cliimenceau says he is also astonished. It is true France has many causes of disagreement with Ragland, but that is no excuse for refusing to acknowledge the good behaviour, and even the kindness, of the British Government in 187 g. "For my part." he writes. "I would look upon it as a pledge for better feelings in future on both sides. The Queen was advised by Lord Derby, who was Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, to write to the Emperor William. In doing this she felt she exposed her eldest daughter to the ruthless animosity of Prince Bismarck. Still, as it was a great public duty, she ran the risk. She was helped by the Empress Augusta, but not by the Crown Prince, who did not want to incur a snub.'</p>
        <p>Prince Bismarck never forgave the Q wan orEmpress, and the Crown Princess was made to feel his wrath. I knew M. Gavard, who was Chargé d'Affaires for the French Repuplic after the death of his chief, the Comte de Jarman. He said that Queen Victoria never more completely waived a great family and personal interest —that of her eldest daughtcr—than in March,lB7s. Lord Darby affected to make light of Prince Bismarck's sud- denly expressed fear that the military power of France was seam becoming a danger that must be retnoved,but Gavard had been so urgently instructed to induce Lord Derby to act that he did act,both through the Queen and through Russia. Prince Bismarck was so enraged at the Emperor Alexander ll.coming forward that he helped to throw Russia into the war of 1877 to force her to mind her own business. The Schnae bele affair was put a stop to by the account given of it by the "Daily News" special correspondent, a service spontaneotsly admitted by M. Sniffler is the "Republique Francaise " some time after, and by M. GriSvy also. That account was trans- lated by the Empress -Augusta herself and laid before the old Emperor. Things had gone so far towards the mobilisation of the German army that troops were bil- leted in the classrooms of universities near the Rhine. M. Spuller, who was an extremely. honest man, acknow- ledged to Lord Lytton the services rendered by the Queen in 1875 and by Lord Derby in helping to bring tne Emperor Alexander to the same side. The time was propitious for applying to him. His only daughter had six months previously married into the English Royal Family, and he was most anxious to cultivate friendly relations with her adopted family and nation. It was not so much out of friendliness to France, but out of re- gard for the Queen, that he stood against his wont in Prince Bismarck's way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ARMENIAN AGITATION.</head>
        <p>THE ARMENIAN AGITATION.</p>
        <p>GREkT MEE PING IN LONDON.-LETTERS FROM</p>
        <p>MR. GLA.DSTONE.</p>
        <p>, A great meeting Convened as a" national protest against the barbarous rule of the Sultan," was held in the St. James's Hall, London, on Monday night. The Bishop of Rochester (Dr. Talbot) presided, and a large and representative gathering completely filled the hall. A number of letters of apology for absence were read. Mr. Gladstone wrote that he hoped the weight and tone of the meeting might worthily crown the proceedings of the last two months, which thus far had been without a parallel during his political life, especially as regarded tke union of the religious and representative bodies in the direct voice of the nation. The great object at the moment was to strengthen the hands of Lord Salisbury. He personally objected in the strongest manner to abridging his Lordship's discretion by laying down this and that as things which he ought not to do. To say that our enforcement of our treaty rights to stop systematic massacre, together with effectual security against our abusing them for selfish purposes, would provoke hostilities from one or more Powers was, in his judgment, a wild paradox, with no suppert from reason or from history. To advertise beforehand, in the ears of the Great Assassin, that our action would in all circumstances be cut down to what the most backward of the six Powers might think sufficient appeared to him, after the experience we had had, to be an abandonment alike of duty and of prudenc3. A national movement subjected at the outset to such a conditien must, in his opinion be not only barren of results but probably mischievous. Be had had rather special opportunities of knowing of what materials the present Sultan, with all his seeming obstinacy, was made. From deplorable and disgraceful causes he triumphed for the moment, but for the moment only. In the fane of civilised man- kind he had recorded the final condemnation, first and foremost of himself, and not less effectually of those who had been his willing, brutal, and sanguinary tools. The first resolution expressed indignation and abhorrence at the outrages committed on the defenceless subjects ef the Saltan;and assured the Government of support in what- ever steps they might take to bring to a speedy termina- tion the reign of terror in the Ottoman dominions'. Another embodied an appeal to the signatory Powere to the Berlin Treaty to use every passible means to bring about an immediate cessation of organised massacre and plunder in the Ottoman dominions, and the execution of definite aud permanent reforms. Both resolutions were carried unanimously. •</p>
        <p>PECULIAR FACTORY ACT PItOSECUTION.</p>
        <p>On Friday morning at the Warrington Borounh Police Court, before Alderman Harrison, Councillor Roberts, and Dr. Gornall,Rebecca Brittle, Refuge-square, Lilford- steeet, was charged with unlawfully making use of a false certificate of birth to obtain employment for her child.—She admitted the offence., Mr. Rogers, Factory Inspector, prosecuted and said that the informat on was laid under a -ection of the Factory and Workshops Act which stated " that any person who wilfully connives at the making use of a certificate as applying to any person to whom it does not apply, then that person so making use ef it is liable to a penalty of £20." The facts were that Mrs. Brittle. the defendant, had a daughter born on the 3rd of April, 1882. She gave the name of Florence to the girl who died on the Ist October of the game year. Another child was born on the Ist August, 1883, and to her also the same name was given, so that defendant was able to obtain two certificates of birth in the name of Florence Brittle. When Florence the younger, reached the age of 13 her mother obtained emnloyment for her in the fac- tory of Mr. Bolton, fustian cutter, and produced her elder daughter's birth certificate to obtain employment for the younger. The latter only being 13 Vests of age could net be employed full time unlefs she had reached a certain standard of educational efficiency, which she had not reached. Defendant, after taking her da ghter to Mr. Bolton told him she was 14 years old, producing the certificate of the girl Florence, who died, as proof. He (Mr. Rogers) instructed his deputy to inform Mrs. Brittle that she had committed a serious off ,nee ad she admitted that she had used her elder daughter's certific- ate to obtain employment for the yfainger one. She could not plead poverty as her husband was in good eir)l3loY. He (Mr. Rogers) Jid not, however, ask the bench to deal severely with the case, as he thought the advertislng of the proceedings would be sufficient, if a small penalty were inflicted.—The magistrates adopted the etlageAion and fined defendant 2s. 6 1. and 103. costs,</p>
        <p>Mr. Rogers said he had received every aßgistance in the case from the Local School Authnrities,who put it in his hands, and he was grateful for that assistance.</p>
      </div>
