        <p>IN the political world the ' The Armenian Armenian question still holds Question. the field, to the almost entire exclusion of other subjects. It is still the chiel theme of members of Parliament and other pollticians when they address public gatherings in anY part of the country, and it continues to exereiSe the minds of diplomatists over the greater part of' Europe. So far as can be seen little or no pro- gress has been made towards a settlement. It cannot be said that even the " beginning of the end" is yet in view. Only one thing appears toZbe certain, and that is, that the Sultan continues his nefarious work with a steady pertierteity that is worthy of a better cause. Unless all the re- ports that come from the East are utterly men' dacious the Armenians are still being persecuted out of existence. The most sensational znetb°d of destruction—that of wholesale massacre—has been partially discarded, though even this method is still, it would seem, adoped with shocking re- sults in some ef the remoter parts of Asiatic !' Turkey. But the Sultan and his mereilese satellites have found that there are other ways of getting rid of Armenians besides smashing their skulls with bludgeons or stabbing them to detail with bayonets. Large numbers of these helpleso people are arrested by Turkish policemen et soldiers at their homes or in the streets. Ther are taken to Turkish prisons and their friend°. never see them again. The ranks of the At' menians are also depleted by slow starvattozi." Employers who could be influenced by either the persuasion or threats of the Turkish authoritis°' have discharged their Armenian servants wile thus lose their only opportunities of earning ° livelihood. In these and other ways the SWOP' is gradually accomplishing the end which lie' clearly ihas in view—that of exterminating this particular ChristiSu nation. Under these circriev' stances there is naturally a strong desire among' the people of the country to hear what is being done by the Government to carry out the rasri_; date which has been given them by the nation' agitation. Is Lord ,SALISBURY, as the represents' tive of the Government, doing anything or noth' ing ? Does he hold the opinion which seems t° be held by some of his supporters that. this cone' try is too weak to even attempt to do anythia effective? Or, is he conducting negotiation! which may lead to satisfactory results Questions similar to these are frequently asked,' there is no authoritative answer. Lord 551,15; BURY'S diplomatic methods are the very reverse c"' those followed by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN in connection with the Transvaal. His actions are hidden 1/8: hind an impenetrable veil of secrecy. Most peoPI: have been eispOsed to form the conclusion 00 this portentous silence is due to the fact tb° Lord SALISBURY is preparing some grand diplorae: tic stroke which will bring the Sultan to his senses: It is sincerely to be hoped that this is an accurst', area explanation. At the same time it is to be fe— that there is much room for doubt. Lord Rssle- BURY has explained in a letter written to a corree., be pondent by one of his private secretaries, that is not in a position to speak freely, otherwise bee: would have an opportunity of 'removing s°°. misapprehensions " which appear to prevail. Tilatl9' reply is almost worse than no repfY t all. It is tantalisingly enigmatical. Whil are the misapprehensions which Lord sA•l'lro BURY would like to remove? There isit:f wide-spread belief, or hope, that Lord Sslasllljsee. will in the end succeed in doing something a' of • titre for the Armenian Christian. Is this the misapprehensions that he wishes to reinolo wi,ll be sorry news if our PRIME breaks his long silence with the declaratiou !I; he has done nothing and can do nothing to end to the dreadful orgies of the Great At all events if anything is to be done it sllow done quickly. If xelief is delayed for 110, months longer there may be no Arreenirto relieve. That will be a fine upshot surelY "peace with honour" which Lord lisitc and Lord SALISRURY brought back from thig one condition of which was the protection bY country of these very Armenians. One thitiblovt certain. In spite of all the attempts te- f cold water upon the agitation the people ° jos,' country still show the same resolute deterZ the end • Ili tion to use all available means to put, an , atrocities. The meeting in St. jartios</p>
