        <p>undon, on Monday, was probably the most lllgnificant that has been held in the whole course Of the agitation. It was attended by represen- tative men holding varied religious and political °Pinions and hailing from all parts of the United Kingdom. The Chairman of the London County Council presided, and the Mayors 'X numerous towns were present, including even the Lord Mayor of Belfast. Eloquent speeches Were made by, among others, the Bailor of IaRREFORD, Dr. CLIFFORD, and the Rev. Dr. C. A. NRRY, and the resolutions were endorsed with Unabated enthusiasm. With such evidenee as Ibis in front of him Lord SALISBURY, whatever key be his suceess or non-success, can not corn- Plain that he has not had a united country behind</p>
        <p>11A.T is the meaning of the un- Trafalgar usual enthusiasm with which Day. "Trafalgar Day" has been cele- Prated this year? is it due to the marked growth of interest in the Navy during the last few years, or does it indicate a revival Of the Jingo spirit? We shall not attempt to answer this question, but whatever may be the explanation, there is no disputing the fact that Trafalgar Day was very warmly celebrated on Wednesday. With the aid of two venturesome steeple-jacks from these northern counties the nelson column in London was handsomely decor- ated from top to bottom, and from morning till eight the Square in which the column stands was crowded with sight-seers. The memory of NELsow Was on the same day honoured in various towns iZI the provinces and even in the distant colonies. 30me objections have been made to this celebra- tion en the ground that it may be a cause of offence to France and Spain whose combined fleets Were almost annihilated on the 21st October, 1805, by the invincible admiral. The French have, however, taken the matter very philosophic- ally, and as for the Spaniards they are too much occupied with their revolted colonies of Cuba and the Phillipines to pay any attention to what la taking place in this country. If the policy of Commemorating events which recall the inter- national strife of the past be conceded, it will be generally admitted that there is no English hero Whose memory better deserves celebration than that of NELSON, nor is there any victory which better deserves to be held in national remem- brance than Trafalgar. That great sea-fight was the means of frustrating the invasion of this coun- try by the formidable army which NAPOLEON had gathered together at Boulogne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WA.RRINGTONIA.NS IN AME RICA.</head>
        <p>WA.RRINGTONIA.NS IN AME RICA.</p>
        <p>The following extract from "The Middleboro' (Mass.) kiazette " will be read with interest by the many friends tA the Rev. J. Eliscn, son of Couticillor Luke Ellison, of Latchford. The Rev: J. Ellison is pastor of the Baptist Church, Carver, Mass.U.S.A. The extract is as follows: "A strong physique and vigo ous mind, a large SYMpathetic heart, directed by true Christian conscien- Vousness,are just as necessary for the pastor ef a country !Ilurch as for the pastors in more populous districts. It 111, a ludicrous mistake to suppose that anything and any °ody will do for the country people of a country church. lld especially so,when arJund that church gather such etidid opportunities for aggressive Christian work ainong the unchurched classes of our State, as gather .oace a year around the churches of Carver. For lour or five weeks in the fall season, the unemployed znen women and children for miles meet on the bogs to Plek'the cranberry. Away from home, church, society, libraries, newspapers, lodging in tents awl in bog huts along the lonely pines of Carver, are colonies of French, Portuguese, Italian, Irish, Swede and American—all of them ready to see the preacher's face, to hear his voice ili speech or song, and to receive from his hands the story Md message of the Christ in Testament ol tract. Have e .otraotluoctehhalPrestors of Carver appreciated the oppoltunitv °l' Have the pastors of Middleboro or Ply- horse velltured out of their study to borrow or hire a th. and drive down to sing er preach to these busy m'c'nge of mixed people about the sally true way of life? e have heard of one alone, who, with a French speak- -1,...ng layman, has gone on this holy mission. The soul of brotber and paeter, Rev. Joseph Edison of the Carver Baptist :buret), has been on fire with zeal, %ad his hands full of labour during he last few weeks. By means of the song " Toll rail the old, old story" sung to 100 Frenchmen, women, and children one day, he found a Mr. Blucher. a Chris- tAan Frenchman of Franklin, who volunteered to help cam ._ in his work among the French. The other day we the p t•tor's diary lying on his desk, and after read- kle last Sunday's entry,as:.ed permission to copy it. It .Letide as follows: Sept. 27,h. Left home 650 a.m. k_lstributed literature in men's colony on Vaughan's °/. Called for Mr. Boucher and together we drove to bottom of Swan Holt bog. Held a service for French tti7 of 38 people, Bang three times, distrilsuted French nets. Second colony of French, excellent order and 7terest. Sang four times, told of safety and peace in Laahs, and gave reading matter. Next a camp of men, lave tracts and words of explanation. Drove on to erican camp—invited in-4 songs, address, much in- nest, gave " Union Signals" and tracts. At 9.30 `Paclacd Perkins and Paine's French colony. Went from ,ror to door with literature and pictores,sang 4 times to ()ling people in one house, ordered off from one Zdoor by an old man in anger, etc. N-xt to A. T. bUrtleff's and Jetinson's—both French colonies, then h_onle for morning worship. Preached on " Nothing 1 tttomon or unclean." Instructed boys' class,had dinner, en drove to West Carver. Gave Bible reading at 3, en on to Rocky Meadow bog. First an Indian camp. tl"eatered tent, found Indian woman sick in bed. Sang her 3 times, then knelt by her bed, and prayed for rt recovery. Next to a large colony of French and lotrie Irish. Lots of young people. all eager to hear sing and speak, and to receive literature. Last .101 ed at one other French camp—found women and `9ildren, gave reading matter. Sent tracts to two other 14t„olps across the bog, and returned for evening service "8. Fini.hed day's work at 8 p.m. Travelled la Ziles. Very weary but happy.' Surely a work of this lid needs to be encouraged. If (leery local church was :centre of such activity, not by pastor alone, but by Rtra missionary helpers from a home missionary ciPty, what a vast amount of good work might be :one each year. By proper arrangement and system :large field might be covered, and much done towards 'lying the problem of h ,w to reach the unchurched."</p>
        <p>THE LATE MAJOR PICKNIERE. •4kon Sunday evening a service was held in the LuaPel of the Blue Coat school in memory of the or`4te Major Pickmere. The prayers and hymns were an appropriate character, and the anthem sung MI TO Ss • Like 48 a father pitieth his own children." 41:10 subject for examination Was the Apostles' iteed. At the conclusion of the service the Dead ch in "Saul" wa4 ably rendered. "A VISIT TO SKYE." t This was the subject of a very interesting paper tttld by Mr. Charb-s vt !lite, before the members of Ite Warrington Field Club on Friday evening last ee,IL The lecture dealt chi..fly with the togical aspects of the island, and the lecturer I,„"ed to his aid a number of slides .•,repared by Dr. t1231-)est Andorson,Nliss E. White, Mr. Abrahams, of to'swick, and others, which illult,ated the points %Itched upon. A hearty vote of thanks was ac- to Dr. White for his able and interesting per, which was listened to with vr-itt attention. SUDDEN DEATH OF A CHILD On inquest was conducted by Mr. F. A. Jones, telltY coroner, in the Parade room at 1.-- tei olice Station on M • -ilay afternoon 4 ative to the death of a chil•-!, tit weeks old, 144bIztu'-d Tnotnas Harrison who lied idenly on Sun- rnorni,pg. Tie fir •t, with le.l was Mrs. tejrison, wife of George Thomas flarrison, of 26, .4 ward-street, forge labour,- r, w!1() said' toe de- e Tilomas Harrison was li,r on. Ha was six t)':kB Old and had been in good h. lith since birth. retired to bed on Saturday nii and the de- kie,ll was then all right. He slcpt in the same bed 11114-'tness and her husband. About seven o'clock 141, fed the deceased, and ar 8-20 the deceased toiriTl her arm dead. ;.7die cafll i Dr. Braids who uer that, the child had die from convulsions. Viitgeant llowbottom said teas called in at thikritY minutes to eleven on .-•ipiclay morning. The had ev,yry eppearan.!e if having died from lekittli,iorH. The jury ret urn,d a verdict that de- ed died from natural cu ,1110, and fillAbS &amp;BLAND, Iron- -4xer,warringtoll. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
