      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TUE NEW THEATRE FOR RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>TUE NEW THEATRE FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday. the tenders for the theatre proposed to be erected in Lowland-road by Mr. Caddick, were under consideration. Ultimately it w.,s decided to provisionally accept the one snbmitted by Mr. William Farrell,builder, Runcorn. It is expected that the work will be com- menced without delay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODI3T BAND OF HOPE</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODI3T BAND OF HOPE</p>
        <p>On Thursday a lautern lecture entitled "Cash Three " was given by Mr. Richardson, in the schoolroom, Green way-road, Runcom Mr. Sayle occupied the chair. The lecturer explained his subj,Tt very clearly and concluded with some local views and comic sketches. Mr. fox- worth manipulated the lantern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>lIIINCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>lIIINCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A social gathering of the members of this society was held on Friday evening in the Bethesda Lecture Hall. An interesting programme of songs,instrumental solos, etc., was gone through by several members, after which re- freshments were dispensed by the ladies. The room had been tastefully decorated for the occasion. The proceed- ings throughout were immensely enjoyed by the iarge audience.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Simpson. pastor of the Bethesda Congre- gational Church, gave the first of a series of lectures on Sunday evening in the above church, taking for his sub- ject "What is ended." Miss Clara Taylor, R.A.M., sang "0 rest in the Lord." Mr. Wesley Cooke was very successful in the interpretation of" the geavenly Song." Mr. C. Le Couteur presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>SHORT TIME IN WIDNES CHEMICAL WORKS. The decision of the directors of the United Alkali Company to put the mechanics, labourers, Sze., at their works in Widnes on five days a week, came into opera- tion on Saturday. They ceased operations on Friday night until Monday morning. Those engaged in the process departments also have their hours of labour cur- tailed, consequent on the accumulation of heavy stocks and the severe depression in the chemical trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LOST BARQUE. FOUND BY A RUNCORN CAPTAIN</head>
        <p>A LOST BARQUE. FOUND BY A RUNCORN CAPTAIN</p>
        <p>A letter was received at Runcorn on Tuesday morn- ing from Captain George Hardley, of the es. Cecil,iying at Bangor, and belonging to Messrs. Brundrit and Hayes, High-st., Runcorn, stating that the large Nor- wegian barque, timber laden, which went ashore on Duchman banks, and which afterwards drifted out to sea has been picked up by him (hardley) between Pen- mon and Beaumaris. He tugged,: her upTto Bangor where be reported the matter to the authorities. No other information is yet to hand. Mr. Llardley resides in York-st., Runcorn. RUNCORN CYCLIC CLUB.</p>
        <p>A social gathering in connection with the above club took place on olonday evening,at the headquarters, Mr. Riley's café, Bridge-street. The chief feature of the evening was the presentation of the prizes won during the past season, which ceremony was performed by Mr. Mundy. The recipients were :--Twenty-five miles handicap,fastest time, gold medal,G. Whinyates ; 1, gold medal,S. Webster ; 2, silver medal with gold centre, W. Wray; 3, silver medal,C. Charlesworth. Ten mile race : I,gold medal,W. Wray ; 2,silver medal with gold centre, R, Withington ;3, silver medal, C. Peacock. Several at- tendance prizes were also given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN BIBLE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BIBLE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Runcorn branch of the Foreign Bible Society, was held on Tuesday evening in the Primitive Methoiist Hall, Greenwav-road, Runcorn. Mr. Harrison occupied the chair, and he was supported by Vas Revs. Dr. Preston, A. M. Monk Jones, T. Brack- enbury, J. Spafford and W. George. Dr. Preston read the financial statement, prepared by the treasurer (Mr. J. Simpson), which showed a slight falling-off in the income during the past year. Mr. Jones spoke at length on the Society's workings, its aims, its constitution, and its character. He showed how it aided the several mis- sionary societies. On the motion of the Rev. J. Spaf- ford, seconded by the Rev. T. Brackenbury, a vote of thanks was accorded to the deputation, and on the pro- position of the Rev. A.M. Jones,seconded by the Rev.W. George a similar vote wae accorded to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>THEI LOST PLYMOUTH STEAMEII.--4RRIVAL AT WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>The s.s.Romala of Plyinouth,laden with china-clay ar- rived at Weston Point on Thursday morning having en- dured some terriblelweather.The Romala with her captain Mr. Crowther of Plymouth, left that port about a fort- night ago. She reached Bangor in good time. After leaving the Welsh port high seas were running, and she experienced the full force of the gale, and she was sub- sequently drifted out many miles from land. Nothing was heard far some days, and it was feared that she had gone down with all hanJs. But a telegram was received at Weston Point saying that Romala was safe in the harbour at Ramsey, after a fierce battle with the seas. She arrived at Weston Point docks yester- day morning. Great anxiety prevailed at the Seamen's Mission,Runcorn,as to the fate of Mr. Crowther, captain of the Romala, as he is well-known amongst the sailors in this town. But now when it will be known that he has arrived safe and none the worse for his trying ex- perience, the sorrow will be at once removed. Mr. Crowther is to give a service on ounday at the Runcorn Seamen's Mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN PREZ LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PREZ LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Runcorn Free Library, war; held on Tuesday evening in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall. Mr. S. Pope occupied the chair, and there were also present,the Rev. J. Simpson, Messrs. W. Hankinson, Ft. G. Giice. W. J. Cunningham, and J. D. Jones,Ltbrarian :—Mr. Jones presented his report which showed that the balance in hairi was £l7 us. 9d. The issues of books for September were as fol- lows: Lending department: theology 40; philosophy, 5 ; science, 82 ; history tz3., 118 ; poetry, 15; fiction, 1,888 ; miscellaneous 143; French literature 5 ; music, 28 ; total 2,102; increase en corresponding month last year 294: Reference department : Miscellaneous 35. Total 35 ; decrease on corresponding month last year 1 : It was proposed and secorded that a vote of thanks be passed to Dr. F. J. Bowman for his lecture on" Three famous books," and also to Mr. J. W. Lightburn J.F., for presiding. The Committee recommended that 80 volumes be sent to the binders. Amoni the gifts which had been received during the month was—one from Sir J T. Brunner M.P., on " Public Education in Cheshire 1885," by himself and J. L. Hamorrd.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S BIBLN CLAS3 , RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The first opening meeting of the session in connection with the above class was held on Quaday afternoon, Mr. Kniveton presided. The interesting feature of the meeting was the presentation to Mr. William Cooke, A.R.C.0., late organist of the class, of four handsome volumes. Bich of the volumes contained the following inscription :—" St. Paul's Sible Class. Runcorn. Pre- sented to Mr. William Cooke, A.R.C.0., 1896."—Mr. Crosby spoke of the pleasure it gave them to know of Mr. Cooke's success in his recent examination, and said how sorry the c:ass were to lose his services. Mr. Cooke had during his three years' term of office worked hard to make the musical part of the service a success, and they all knew how he had succeeded. Mr.Kn iveton in making the presentation on behalf of the cla,s, wished Mr. Cooke success in his new post, as organist of St. Paul's Church, Widnes. Mr. Cooke, in responding, thanked the members for their valuable and handsome present. Be said be could not find words to express his thanks. Be was very sorry that ke was obliged to sever his connection with the class, but he should always remember with pleasure his connection and association therewith. A musical pro- gramme was gone through and the meeting brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ST. EDWARD'S CATHOLIC CHURCH, RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ST. EDWARD'S CATHOLIC CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The annual appeal on behalf of the day schools attached to the above mission, was made on Sunday in the church, Irwell-lane. In the morning high mass was celebrated by the pastor, the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi. The sermon was preached by the Rev. Father Cross, of Liver- pool, who dealt at great length with the character and virtues of the Saint to whom the sacred edifice was dedi- cated. The choir creditably rendered Guonod'a Grand Mass. The principals were Miss McClew. soprano ; Miss A. Richardson' contralto ; Mr. Joseph M. Bate, tenor; and Mr. PAer J. Bate, basso. Mr. W. Grounds presided at the organ. The instrumental accompaniments were beautifully given by Mr. Horace Handley' orchestra, whilst Mr. J. W. Dockrey, choirmaster, conducted. In the evening the Rosary was recited, and Father Cross again preached. The rev, preacher chiefly dwelt on the schools and asked all present to support them liberally, and so enable their worthy pastor to keep up the high state 'of efficiency which the schoo: had now attained, The choir rendered selections from Gounod's work. There were large con- gregations at both services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DEATH OF A WELL KNOWN RUNCORN HESTIA/4T.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A WELL KNOWN RUNCORN HESTIA/4T.</p>
        <p>The death of Mr. Peter R,gby, 48 Egerton-st., Run- corn, took place on Sunday evening at his residence after a very brief illness. Deceased for many years had been engaged at Mathieson's Works, Widnes, and was well respected by the workmen of that firm. His illness commenced on Friday evening alter returning from his work at about 6 o'clock p.m. Be then appeared in his usual health and rrirtook of tea with his daughter. At the tea table he was .luddenly taken ill. Medical aid was sum- moned, but despite the attentions of the three medical gentlemen (Dm McLennan, McK.ozie and Wright) in attendance he expiieci at abut 6.30 p.m. on Sunday evening. Mr. Rivby had cloNely identified himself with Wesleyanism in tile town, and for 15 years had been a teacher at Brunswick Sunday school. He had also been a member of Mr. T. &amp;mister's Bible Class of the Vlresley Guild„and of the Itnpr wement Class. Deceased was a staunch teetotaller, and for many years had belonged to the Sons of Tomperance. His remains were interred on Tue'sday afternoon in the Runnorn Cemetery. The funeral cortege headed by a orocession comp mod by gentlemen belongirig to the Wesleyan body of the town, started from the deceased's late residence and wended its way up Greenway-road o the Cemetery. A short ser- vice was held in the church by the Rev. T. Brackenbury. At the grave-side the Rev. T. Bracken bury recited the funeral service, whilst the Rev. W. George sang a hymn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Ti 3 E FX A MINER FRIIIAY, OCTOBER 23 1896.</head>
        <p>Ti 3 E FX A MINER FRIIIAY, OCTOBER 23 1896.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>James Dickens, 6, Hankey-street, Runcorn, met with an accident whilst playing football on the ground off Perciya;'s-lane. He collided with a member of the op- posing team, and sustained a slight concussion of the brain. He was attended by Dr. Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RIINC•/RN SCHOONER IN COLLISION.</head>
        <p>A RIINC•/RN SCHOONER IN COLLISION.</p>
        <p>The steamer Prince Ja Ja, from Carnarvon, arrived at Liverpool on Wednesday, and reported having collided on the previn us day, off Great Orme's Head, with the schooner Ellesmere, belonging to Messrs. Brundrett and Hayes, High•street, Runcorn. The Ja Ja, sustained no damage, but the Ellesmere was dam ged on the star- board quarter. She commenced to leak a little and was at once towed to Beaumaris, and beached for repairs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>SAD BURNING CASE AT RUNCORN</head>
        <p>SAD BURNING CASE AT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning shortly before 12 o'clock. Mary Ann Payne, daughter of Mr. J. Payne,of 50, Irwell-lane, Rnneorn, suetained very serious inpries from burns. On the morning named,Mrs. Payne left the house and heard some screaming and then saw her daughter enveloped in flames run out of the house. A youth named James Hill living in the same locality was attracted by the screams and be on seeing the girl pulled off his coat and wrapped it around her, and thus extinguished the flames. Dr. McKenzie was quickly upon the scene, and he attended to the injured girl, whom he found suffering from severe burns on the face neck, arms, and chest. Her case is considered critical. _ _ _</p>
        <p>SIR J. T. BRUNNER, M.P., AND HER MAJESTY'S LONG REIGN.</p>
        <p>Tne Rev. Dr. Preston, Vicar of Holy Trinity Church, Runcorn, recently wrote to Sir J. T. Brunner, M.P.. pointing out that nothing having been done in Runcorn of a permanent character to publicly commemorate the jubilee of the Queen, it would be a fitting memorial of the longest reign in English history if a readingroem were erected in the town. Sir John has replied express- ing approval of the suggestion, and stating his willing- nes i to subscribe half the sum that can be raised locally towards the object.</p>
        <p>BILLIARD HANDICAP. A billiard handicap was played at the Huncern Liberal Club, on Wednesday evening between a local twelve.and a team from the -Chester and Counties Liberal Club, Chester. The home men commeneud well and won easily by 297. At the conclusion of the game Mr. T. Lea, High-street, provided tea. The following are the indivi- dual scores :</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CHESTER W Wright °apt. 65 B Griffith lOO R Owen lOO J M tyors 11 flames Brown H Wright lOO Corporai Pollitt 9l J Green 9l J 'opper lOO W J Carter W Copley 10) W Aldie B7 R Green T Back J Crowley lOO W Fiela 3l TRiley ......... ••,,...,.• •• ..... . 3.q. T Mid?. ••••t••••it ft.'', 61</p>
        <p>Tatal 1142</p>
        <p>Total. a.... 845</p>
        <p>Rt/PICORN PRIZB CHOIR CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The opening concert of the 12th session under the aus- pices of the above choir, took place on Wednesday even- ing in the Public Hall, Runcorn. There was a large atten- dance. The concert was opened by the audience singing the National anthem, in honour of Her Majesty's long reign. The members of the choir then sang "The comrade's song of hope" and during the evening also contributed Cowen's " Boat song," " The sea bath its pearls and "The river floweth strong." The way in which they acquitted themselves gave every satisfaction. The principal aristes engaged for the evening were, Madame Eugenie Morgan (from the Crys- tal Palace, London), Miss Annie Layton (from the Royal Albert Hall, London), Mr. Charles glliscM (late principal tenor, Royal Carl Rosa Opera Company), and Mr. W. H. Burgon (late principal bass, Royal Carl Rosa Opera Co.) The principals all took their parts well and were all encored. The concert concluded with selec- tions from Gounod's popular opera" Faust" the cast being: Margaret, Madame Morgan ; Siebel, Miss Lay- ton; Faust, Mr. EAison ; and Mephistopheles, Mr. Bur- gon. This portion of the concert proved equally as suc- cessful as the first part, the artistes ably performing their alloted tasks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD scaous.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD scaous.</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY MR. E. M. SNEYD KYNNERSLEY.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening Mr. E. M. Sneyd Kynnersley, H.M. Inspector of Schools gave a lecture on Belgium and Hol- land, illustrated by lime-light views. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. Pritchard (chairman of the School Board), there were also present the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. F. J. Norman J. P., Mr. C. Timmins, Mr. G. Moss (clerk) as well as the lecturer. The Chairman said the countries that they were ex- pecting to hear about that night, were not so remote as some of those described by the lecturer on his previous visits. Belgium and Holland were very accessible, and were places of interest, being noted for picture galleries, museums, &amp;c. He was very pleased as he was sure these present were, to see Mr. Kynnersley once more amongst them. He (Mr. Kynnersiey) took a great interest in the schools of the tesve, not only as an inspector, but as one ever ready with his counsel and advice to assist managers and teachers from time to time. The proceeds of the lecture were to be devoted to the scholars' Prize Fund. The Prize Fund had been a very material assistance, for it had resulted in regular attendance, as well as diligence at lessons. Altogether Mr. Kennersley's lectures had been the means of raising over £2O. In 1894 a portion of the sum obtained was spent in providing free breakfasts for the poorest children in time of the distress. The Prize fund had done great work in getting the scholars to at- tend regularly, and enobled the students to become pro- ficient. (Applause.) Mr. Kynnerslev, who was greeted with a loud burst of applause, delivered a very interesting lecture on " Bel- gium and Holland." He described very clearly his travels through the two countries. He spoke of the people, their habits and customs, and dwelt at great length on the public buildings he had personally inspected. Mr. Thomas Bowyer manipulated the lantern. - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the lecturer. He said Mr. livnnersley's lecture that night had been intensely interesting, and made one very much desirous of paying a personal visit to the countries brought under their notice. The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi seconded the motion, remark- ing that the lecture had been most interesting, and one, which he thought they would all derive great benefit from.</p>
        <p>The proposition was carried with applause. Mr. Kyn- nersley, responded,and proposed,a vote of thanks to the chairman. Mr. Moss seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FRODSRAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSRAM.</p>
        <p>METHODIST FREE CHURCH.--A meeting of the ladies attached to the above church was held on Wednesday evening. A public tea was afterwards provided in the schoolroom. An entertainment consisting of songs, re- citations, and readings also took place. Mrs. Janes oc- cupied the chair. LECTURE ON THE THANSVAAL.—.I public meeting was held in the Trinity Chapel, Frodsham, on Wednesday evening to hear an address from the Rev. Thomas H. Weinman (who has recently returned from the Trans- vaal) en the missions in South Africa. Addresses were also given by the Revs. J.R. Hargreaves and J.K. Kelly. Mr. R E. Garnett occupied the chair. . _ _</p>
        <p>YOUTHFUL THIEVES AT FRODSHAII.—At the Frodsham Police Court on Wednesday, before Mr. Thomas and Dr. Steele, three youths,named John Booth, John Tollitt,and Regain Davies,were charged with breaking and entering the jam factory of Messrs. Kydd and Kydd,and stealing, ,therefrom 18s. lad. in cash, between six o'clock on Fri- day night and six o'clock on Saturday morning.—Mr.H. G. Linaker, a member of the firm, said he secured the factory up on Friday night and left 18s. 104 d. in the office. At six o'cloek on Saturday morning when he went to open he found that the place had been broken into, the leek having been spliced open, and the money missing. Alfred Neil said he knew the defendants, he saw them together on Friday night. They paid for him two bottles of pop. Booth bought something else in his presence. They appeared to have plenty of money. PC. Price proved apprehend- , ing Tollitt and Davies, and Sergeant Clays proved !arresting Booth. The last witness charged them with the offence, and they all admitted it, but said it was only 12s. 33 that they took. The charge was reduced to one of feloniously stealing, in order that the. case might be dealt with there. After consideration the magistrates fined Booth and Tollitt lbs. including costs, each, and Davies 103. including costs. FOOTBALL MATCH.—A football match under association rules, was played on Wednesday, on the Frodsham ground,between the Shop-assistants and Connah's Quay. The home team showed superiority and won easily by 4 goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN BezAAß.—The result of this bazaar, which was held on Wednesday and Thursday last, for the raising of funds for tke repair and decoration of the Halton Trinity Wesleyan Church and school,has resulted in the handsome sum of £246 beine ohtained, namely £l5B us, taken on Wednesday and £B7 93. on the cecnnd day. This is far in excess of what the promoters antici- pated, for it was generally thought that they would do very well if they reached £l5O. A Nnw Poon RATE.—A new poor rate for the parish of Halt ,n has been signed by Mr. J. Handley, J.P., and Mr. S. Tayllr, J.P., for is. 31. in the £, estimated to realise £375 03. 61 A psor rate has also been signed by the same justices. for Stockham at is. in the £, estim- ated to realise £4B 93. 4J. Each rate is the same as the last half-year.</p>
        <p>It is stated that a national strike is threatened among the telephone men owing to a notice issued in Yorkshire and the northern districts extending the hours to fifty- three from fifty, without extra remuneration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF BOWLING PRIZES.</p>
        <p>There have been many pleasant gatherings at the Warrington Liberal Club during the last year or two but none productive of more enjoyment and fun than that of last Saturday night when the prizes won in the annual club bowling main were presented. About 80 bowlwrs sat down to a substantial meat tea in the read- ing room at 6 o'clock, and showed their appreciation of Mr. E. J. Mitchell's catering by the readiness with which they partook of the viands before them. A second sit. ting was necessitated at 7 p.m. before all were disposed of.</p>
        <p>Rather more than an hour later a smoking concert was held in the large committee room, which was crowded to the door. Mr. A. H. Crosfield (president of the club), toek the chair and was supported by Councillors Pier- point, Shaw-Green, Tinnion, and Dr. Gornall, and Mr. J. H. vleming. The CHAIRMAN proposed the health of the Queen and Royal Family. He added that the toast was one that was received with euthusiasm at all assemblies in the kingdom, of every description, and very properly so as there had never been a mere conscientious and hard- working sovereign in any country, and the evidences of the Queen's assitthity in her onerous duties in spite of increasing years, must move their constant admiration. (Applause.) The toast was then honoured. Mr. T. PEMBERTON read letters of apology for absence from Councillor Plumpton, Mr. T. Birchall, Mr. W. Briggs, Councillor F. W. Monks, Mr. T. Morris, and Mr. J. W. Hayward.</p>
        <p>A duot by Mr. R. H. Pearson and Mr. W. H. Payton on the violin and piano was then given, both performers acquitting themselves with their usual efficiency. Mr. R. Anderton sang "Off to Philadelphia," with charac- tertist vigour, and being encored he gave "All have a drink with me." _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN then asked Mr. J. H. Fleming to distri- bute the priz-s he had contributed. Mr. FIRMING in doing so remarked on the satisfaction it was to see so many present OR that occasion.lt showed that the game of bowls was one much appreciated at the club. He then handed the prizes won in the first main to the winners. They were as follows :-1, half dozen prom's, carver, and steel, Thomas Gibbon ; 2,dinner ser- vice, 54 nieces, William Warburton ; 3, Gladstone bag. Joseph Bennion ; 4, bowls and jack, J. S. Parkes ; 5, ccpper kettle, James Sankey ; 6, umbrella, F. Jones ; 7, brushes, B. Shearer ; 8, dolly tub, T. Critchley ; 9, pipe, George Strange ; 10, calico, John Cant ; 11, stout, C. H. Hibhert ; 12, shirt, J. Appleton ; 13, whi4ky, S. Dun- bobbin ; 14, whisky, John Pollitt ; 15, mirror, C.Lyon ; 16, tea, James Howarth ; 17, handkerchief. Middleton ; 18, seed and currant bread, R. Anderton ; 19, pie, J. W. Evans ; 20. braces, A.Jones ; 21, bread board and knife, T. Lowe ; 22, braces, J. Jennings ; 23,walkin! stick,Jeff nennion ; 24, walking stick,George Taylor ; 25, pie, H, Caintith.</p>
        <p>Mr. Delaney contributed a humorous and pointed re- citation, and Mr. Greenhalgh a song "Comrades ever since we were boys." Both were well received. The pr-zes of the annual main,which Mr.Crosfield had presented,were now distributed hy him. They were an exceedingly handsome and valuable lot,and the winners of them were :-1, marble clock with bronzes,T Locker ; 2, silver-plated tea urn, James Henry ; 3, marble time- piece with bronza ornament, William Bibby ; 4, cruet stand, M. Knowles ; 5, silver-plated tie'-carvers, John Willett; 6,cruet stand, J. E.Warren; Nana dish, T.Applet- ton ; 6, Masonic clock, Middleton ; 9, matchbox. J. Savage ; 10, butter dish, J. Critchley ; 11, breakfast cruet. A. Jones ; 12, watch, W. Warburton ; 13, clock, John Holmes ; 14, pair spoons, J. W. Evans ; 15, silver albert, Longmore ; brooch, H. Daintith. Mr. CRO.FIELD, bef,sre making the presentations said he believed this was the first time he had distributed any bowling prizes th,,re. Perhaps they thought it incuna bent upon the President to say something in favour of the game but he could not really promise any disquisi- tion upon bowls. He was bound to admit that his edu- cation in the game went back to a fairly early date, considering his years, certainly over a generation, be- cause when he lived at Lymrn one of the treats he looked forward to was to go with his father to a neigh- bour's and carry the "footer." (Laughter.) He recol- lected an amusing incident happening at that time. A gentleman same 'o Lymm from the other side of the "ditch "and said he understood the game. He was asked to join in and "open the ball." Somebody bowled the " jack " down and then gave him a bowl. As the gentleman said he understood the rules of the game they did not presume to coach him. He had a shot at the "jack," narrowly missed it, and the bowl flew up an embankment, damaged several rhododendron brushes, and wrought considerable havoc in the kitchen garden. (Laughter.) Speakirg of himself, Mr. Crosfigld said he was afraid attractions of a different kind had prejudiced his chances of reaching any degree of proficiency in the pursuit of bowls. If he spoke on the game it would be like the small boy giving tis elderly relative lessons. (Laughter.) He had heard that ic would be much better if he told them grime travellers' tales of his visit to Switzerland. (Hear, hear.) He could not do that, however, as the time was running short. There was also a better reason for him not launching out on Switzerland and that was that while there he had the luck to spend several days in company with the leading authority on mauntaineering of every kind in the world, Mr. Edward Whimper. Opplause.) The latter was not only competent as regarded the theory of climbing but he had done a great deal at the 'practice of it, for he was one of the first to attain the bite noire of climbers b ascending the Matterhorn. (Appl (use ) It had struck the speaker that it would be better if they could get some specialist like Mr. Woimper to undertake the job of lecturing on mountaineering, so he sounded him on the subject and had had a note from him saving he would be glad to come down towards the end of Nov- ember or December. (Applause.) He (Mr. Crosfield) had not heard Mr. Whimper on the platform but he was a competent lecturer, and he knew he had the finest pos- sible set of slides. (Applause.) Next door to getting up at 4 a.m. and seeing the sun rise they would have as adequate an illustration as possible, he hoped, in the Parr Hall. (Loud applause.) He was pleased to see the popularity ot the game of bowls and other recreations in Warrington. (Applause.) Mr. J. SANKEY (captain of the bowling team) then rose to perform a duty which was not down on the pro- gramme in the Chairman's hands. Producing a solid silver cigarette case and match-hex, with Mr. Crosfield's monogram on them, he said he had great pleasure in pre- senting that small but valuable present t their presi- dent, Mr. Crosfield. (Applause.) In handing it over to him he hoped Mr. Crosfield would live long, and that his heart would he " a bumper of comfort" as long as he lived. (Applause.) Mr. Jeff. 131INNION said he was sure that Mr. Crosfield well deserved the little present they had made him ; not because of its value but because of the kindly feeling the bowlers of the club held him in. (Applause.) When he heard that Mr. Crosfield was made President of the green he felt gratifiod that such was the case. (Applause.) They were only too pleased to have him as president and he hoped the present would alway remind Mr. Crosfield of the bowlers of the Warrington Liberal Club. Op- plause,) - _ _</p>
        <p>- Mr. C.ROSIMILD said he had no idea that such a presenta- tion was going to be made, and really he had such a character as an inveterate pot-hunter—though this was not a pot—that he almost regretted they had bestowed upon him such a present. As president of the bowling green his duties were so light he bad no claim upon them of the kind. Still he valued it very much because it showed a cordiality of feeling which he hoped would always exist. (Applause.) It was only the other day that Lord Rosebery warned them that they were in dan- ger of playing too much at cricket and football,and that they were apt to forget the more serious duties of life. There might he ground for the warning, but, after all, the day was 24 hours long, and there was time for all these things. He thought it was Bulwer Lytton who said that" Five hours sleep was enough for any man over 50." (A Voice: "That won't do," and laughter.) "Six hours for a woman and seven for a fool." (Laughter.) The audience apparently demurred. He did not sub- scribe to the novelist's opinion himself, as the rest was too short. Still there was a big balance left for recrea- tive and other duties. Each bad his work to do. He never would be a preacher of Utopian follies, but, given the nine or ten hours day, or whatever it might be, which some of them had to put in, and then a margin was left for recreation, be it bowls cr golf. They were all the better for recreation, and no man could play well if he was constantly fuddling. It was Wordsworth who said that one of the most sacrilegous things a mats could do was to sacrifice his health, and they could lay the balm to their souls that when they were playing bowls they were keeping up their strength to enable them to do their work from day to day. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Messrs. PEARSON and PAYTON treated the audience to another duet and the proceedings soon afterwards ter- minated.</p>
        <p>ROBBERIES IeROM WARRINGTON TRADESMEN AT W IDN ES.</p>
        <p>On Monday at Widnes Police Court, before the Mayor (Mr. Sadler) and Mr. J. Mercer, Mary Isa- bella Cappel was charged with steeling three pairs of stockings, value 24.7 d., the property of Alfred Walker,Warrington ; a frock, &amp;c., value is. 5d., be- longing to Elizabeth Rothwell,4B,School-brow,War- rington ; and also three pinafores, value 2s 6d., be- longing to Thomas Edward Leigh, of school-brow, Warrington. The complainants all have stalls in the market. On Saturday night the prisoner was seen by Mr. Lei,,211 examining some pinafores, and he noticed that she placed one under her shawl. He informed Constable Moseley,who took her into cus- tody,and found the stockings produced in a peram- bulator under the child which it contained. In each case the prisoner was fined 104.</p>
        <p>Mrs POSSONDY'S HAIR RESTORKR, a great discovrery, a Most Refreshing Wash, Marvellous Results, Innumeiable Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. In great demand by Professionals, makes the Hair Grow, prev.nts -Bald- ness, Invigorating, restores hair to its naturalcolour, gives healthy and youthful appearance. Most astonish- ing results ;2s 6d Bottle, post tree.—A ddt tlisMis. Pox- SONnr, 25, Lime-st., Liverpool—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NATIONAL UNION OF TEACHERS</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL UNION OF TEACHERS</p>
        <p>MEETING OF THE WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon a general meeting of the War- rington and District Branch of the National Union of Teachers was held in the Heath Side Infant School, Mr. John Wright (head-master of Daresbury Board school) presided. The attendance was not large. After the confirmation of the minutes of the previous meeting which were read by Mr. T. Edden (hon. secre- tary), the chairman expressed his surprise as well as sorrow to see such a small attendance,and said he hoped ' that for the future the members would endeavour to awaken an interest in the work of the association for the benefit of the profession generally. Thanks, how- ever, t 3 the efforts of several members of the committee, he was pleased to be able to report to the meeting that the membership was now 70, but he saw no reason why it should not be at least 120. In order to create greater interest in the meeting, the committee decided to ask the executive to send down one of their representatives. Mr. Jones had come amongst them that afternoon, and by doing so had sacrificed his Saturday holiday to give I those present a lecture on the benevolent funds con- nected with the union. No doubt he would think they were most uncharitable as they did very little towards the benevolent work of the union. However, he was pleased to be able to tell them that the committee at Warrington had decided to hold a concert in Parr Hall, by which it was hoped a substantial sum would be real- ized. (Applause.) Mr. T. H. JONES, of London (who is a member of the executive and supervisor for the district),then proceeded to deliver an address, but before speaking on the " Work of the Benevolent Funds," said there were elle or two other matters to which he should like to call the atten- tion of the meeting. He contended that what they wanted was liberty to teach—that liberty they had not yet obtained. It had been said that the price of liberty was perpetual vigilanee, and he thought that there was more need for vigilance on the part of the teacher now than at any period of his history. They were on the eve of an educational crisis, and how they got out of it depended in a great measure upon the unionism or co-operation in their ranks. To non-members he asked them did they intend facing the crisis in their own strength or with 34,000 or 35 000 odd persons at their back ? In January the Government would introduce their Bill to deal with the intolerable strain in Voluntary schools. Millions of children and thousands of teachers were languishing for the need of further support. Taking the whole of the voluntary schools of the coun- try,and comparing them with the Board schools, it had been foetid that in order to bring the Voluntary schools up to the standard—" level r.p must be their cry— they would require ae least over 10s. per head on the average atten44uce in their schoolE; but the Government in their last Bill offered them 41. Then there were teachers who were in daily dread of dismissal, and were subjected to indignities in order to keep their post, and so tong as that state of things existed the teachers could not do their best IN the schools. Allusion was made to the low salaries of some of the teachers. It was said that it was absurd to expect the Qovernmerit toifv:erfere in a matter of that kind. But why hot when they in. terfered with the s ilaries of poor law officials of which he gave several instances. On the question of teachers werking on a Sunday,he said that he saw no objection to him or her doing so, provided their health was good, but the work should not be made a condition of appoint meet. (Ilear,hear, and applause.) Some remarks were made about the superannuation fund, and the speaker said it was an exemplification of the old proverb that hope deferred made the heart sick. They had had platitudes to any extent, but, unfortunately, when the winter nights set in platitudes would not keep the wolf from the door. In the fate of the unanimous voice in the House of Commons that teachers should be super- annuated, the speaker observed that the Chancell r of the Exchequer had not seen his way to put aside £35,000, that being the amount required for the first year for the superannuation of teachers, although so much money was spent on war ships. However, the teachers of the country out of the scanty means at their disposal had started a fund to enable them to do what they conld for their fellow teachers, and a benevolent and widow and orphan fund had been established. The mana e'en= . -ss was in the hands of the gititscrit cu,and !act; yeartio le a sem than £i,200 was paid out. The committee met eace a week; 15s. a week was the maximum sum, and £25 a year. A large number of details were given as to the working of the funds, and the whole of the address was attentively listened to. The CIIAIRMLN said he was sure they were all very much obliged to Mr. Jones for his able address and he limped they would go home more enthusiastic for the welfare of the union than ever.</p>
        <p>Mr. SINGLE ens (Newton-le-Willows) proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Jones for his interesting lecture, and this was seconded by Mr. CANN, and carried. Mr. JONES replied, and said the best return that could be made to him would be to obtain an increase in the membership of the association. The non-members shonl Ibe induced to join at once, because unity was strength. (Bear, hear.) .</p>
        <p>Some discussion of a general character took place in the coarse of which reference was made to the forthcom- ing concert, and Mr. FINNEY, who expressed a hope that it would be a great financial success,said there would be two questions which, he thought, were more important at the. present time—namely, the questions et superannuation arid security of tenure. It was the especial duty of the young people connected with the profession to endeavour to secure these benefits and they could only do so by bringing pressure to bear on the education autherities.lf they were disunite( and not in the movement heart and soul tkey might depend upon it they would never get what they wanted. Unless superannuation was obtained, they would be all sixes and sevens in tits future.lt .oras.in his opinion, a great shame that superannuation had not been obtained already. If they obtained it, and a fair amount too, the result would be that they would feel more confidence in their situations, they would have more happiness and contentment, and feel that they had something to look forward to. And if they got security of tenure that would be an additional reason why they should love the profession more than ever. (Applause.) A vote of thanks was then accorded to alr• Wright for presiding, and the meeting was brought to a con- clusion.</p>
        <p>After the meeting the teachers sat down to a good tea, which was pv.sided over by the Misses Finney and Brown. Games, songs, and dances were afterwards en- joyed by the company. Mr. Wright sang splendidly the song, " Never mind the rest."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SIH.P CANAL.</head>
        <p>SIH.P CANAL.</p>
        <p>THELWALL PARISH COUNCIL AND THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the members of the Diet- wall Parish Council was held on Monday evening, in the Parish Schools, Tnelwall. The Rev. T. Pym Williamson presiden,and there were present:—Messrs. W. H.Bleckly, W. Houghton, G. Lambert, W. Tinsley, H. Roebuck, A. Robbins, and W. B. Cook (clerk).—The Clerk read the letter acco npanied by the resolution which the Council had drawn up at its last meeting having reference to the state of the Manchester Ship Canal in that neigh- bourhood, and which had been forwarded to the County Council :—" 20th July, 1896. I am desired by the Parish Council of Thelwall to call your attention to the tollowing resolution which was proposed by Coun- cillor W. H. Bleckly, and seconded by Councillor H. Roebuck, and carried unanimously: 'That a memorial be forwarded from this Parish Council drawing attention to the uffensive and insanitary condition of the Manchester Ship Canal, and pray- ing that the Council will use every available means to have the nuisance abated with the least pos- sible delay.'" It was then proposed by Councillor Roebuck, and seconded by Councillor Kite, and also carried unanimously. " That a copy of the above reso- lution be also forwarded to the District Council."—The following letter had been received in reply :—"Cheshire County Council, Chester, 22nd July, 1896. Dear Sir,— I have to acknowledge the receipt of your letter of the 20th inst., together with the resolution passed by the Thelwall Parish Council, drawing the attention of the Public Health Committee of this Council to theiffensiva and insanitary condition of the Manchester Ship (~trial, and praying that this Council will use every available means to have the nuisance abated with the least possible delay. The Public Health Committee of this Council, at its meeting on Friday last, the 17th inst.,had its attention drawn to the state of the Manchester Ship Canal, and also to the nuisance arising from the condi- tion of the old river bed, in the parish of. Appleton,and I enclose copy of a resolution which they passed thereon,a copy of which hap been transmitted to the Clerk of the River Mersey and Irwell Watershed Joint Committee and also to the Town Clerk of Warrington. I may also state that the Town Council of Warrington have been rrquested to remove the filth, which is now in the old river bed referred to, and which to a very large extent occasions the nuisance complained ot.—Yours, tee., REGINALD POTTS."—The following is a copy of the reso- ution :—" Resolved upon the motion of the Chairman of the 17ounty Council, that this Public Health Committee, consisting of the whole of the members of the Council, is of opinion that a very serious nuisance dargerous to the public health ot the district is occasioned by the filthy condition of the water in the disused portion of the river &amp;Jersey at Warrington, and also in the Manchester Ship Oaual, consequent on the pollution of that, part of the Mar Mersey within the jurisdiction of the Rivers Mersey Irwell Watershed J jut Cemmittee, and expresses the hope that the Joint Committee will vigorously put into operation and without delay the powers they possess to prevent the pollution complained of, as by so doing the nuisance referred to will be materially reduced, if not entirely abated." Mr. Bleckly remarked that it was a very suitably reply. The remarks in the letter applied more to the old river and the bed of the river at. Warrington than they did to them, and the Ship Canal was included in it, and they might let the matter rest for some time. He thought everything was being done that could be done at present —The Chairman said he thought the agitation in Man- chester with regard to the sewerage scheme was doing something.—The subject then dzopped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>A correspondent of the " DAily Chronicle" gives a de- tailed account of the deliberate killing of an engineer mechanic in Carlsruke by a German lieutenant. The mechanic accidentally knocked against the officer's chair in a restaurant,and as he did not immediately apologise, the lieutenant, crying out that his honour had gone, closed the exits,and,findin,g the mechanic in a barAryard, slew him with his sword,declaring his honour was saved. This murder is the direct result of the Emperor's policy in courting and fomenting the military spirit. People have been murdered by sentries in Berlin for what, at the most, would have brought about a five-shilling fine in London, and the ridiculous, the grotesque, height to which soldierly arrogance has risen in Germany has now cost another life, that of an humble producer sacrificed to the idiotic sense of " honour " of a person who in England would be regarded as a criminal or a lunatic.</p>
        <p>Bulawayo, after its recent spell of palpitating excite- ment, seems to be again coming round to the common- place. Chiefs who were recently watching an oppor- tunity to swoop down on the township are now walking about the streets, while disbanded volunteers are casting about for a living, and speadators are resuming opera- tions. Gigantic building schemes are on the tapis, and one alone is said to involve an expenditure of over £200,000. Complaint is being made that with the re- sumed activity of transport, drink is arriving in quan- tities out of all proportion to food supplies. 17 tmine prices prevail; board at ordinary hotels costs £2O a month, and Boer goats in poor condition fetch 60s. to 803. in open market.</p>
        <p>The Nome Office return of suits instituted in 1893-4 in the High Court for divorce and for judicial separation, shows that there were 213 petitions presented for judicial separation, and 1,063 tor divorce or dissolution of mar- riage. The suits for judicial separation numbered sixty- three, forty-nine of which were successful. For divorce the suits numbered 808, in 743 of which decrees nisi were granted ; 407 causes never came to a hearing. The Queen's Prioctor showed cause in thirty-three eases, four of which were unsuccessful. The decrees in the remain- ing twenty-nine were reversed.</p>
        <p>A lady travelling by " dandy " the other week from Chael, the Maharana of Dholeoore's summer residence, to Simla, wnilst resting on the way under a shady nook was (says the "Times of India") attacked by a swarm of hornets and stung severely on the neck, face, and hands. The lady, in great pain and thoroughly frightened, ran down the khud leading to a dangerous precipice, and would inevitably have gone over the edge had it net been for the plucky intervention of a passing coolie, who on the very edge caught the lady in his arms and held her there, though the hornets which were fol- lowing in swarms kept on stinging the brave fellow. That her rescuer was rewarded by the lady for his timely and plucky service goes without saying.</p>
        <p>Writing in the "Figaro." M. Maurice Leudet gives rathAr an Interesting, though brief, account ofri'e Irish colony in Paris. He estimates that it comprivs about a thousand naembers, scattered over every division of the social area. In the highest spheres of fashion, without reckoning such thoroughly naturalised families as the klacMahons, we come upon the Comte O'Connell and others of his sect, Arthur O'Connor, the Standishes, O'Callaghans, O'Keenans, MacDermotts, Mac Sweeneys, all prosperous and moving in the best sets. The clerical element centres round the Irish Seminary near the Pantheon, founded by Louis XlV.,and presided over to- day by Canon Curtin, who is attached to the parish of the Madeleine. In the working-classes Irishrnen are numerons enough. All the compositors of the " New York Herald" belong to that nationality. By no means the least interesting personage in the group is Miss Maud Gonne, whose eloquence pleads unceasingly on behalf Jf the distressful cuuntry, though she found time and enerey to bestow some of her active sympathy on Le Bray' General and his cause.</p>
        <p>A remarkable tribute was paid to the memory of Mr. Du Maurier at one of the variety entertainments in New York tit; ,-;;;;* prived of his death. The orchestra was instructed to play:: isen 1;64," the song with which, it will be remembered,Trilby astonished the world. The news was soon whispered throghout the hall that Du Maurier was dead, and every man in the audience took off his hat. The plaee became parfectly quiet, and when the orchestra had finished the song was encored. In obedience to this demand, the air was payed again. A young woman with a pure seprano voice sang the words, and was so laudly applauded that the song had to be repeated five times more.</p>
        <p>Chicago has just beaten all previous "Divorce re- cords," and can now with truth advertise in the well- known formula." Divorces while you wait." On Oct 2nd thelfollowing new record was:established by John B.liet- cham,a wealthy and well-known club man :-2,10 p.m.. hill for divorce filed ; 3 10 p.m., case called for hearing ; 3.43 p.m., decree granted; 3.47, that decree filed ; 4.15, alimony of 150,000J015. paid over to the divorced woman.</p>
        <p>Some weeks ago a great commotion was excited in Nottingham by the discovery of a real Waterloo hero in the workhouse— a kind of institution in which "heroes" who happen to be mere privates are usually found in England. John Stacey, the veteran referred to, had in- duced the Guardians to believe that be was 99 years old and in his early youth had fought at Waterloo. It is creditable to the hearts if not to the beads of the Guardians that upon this simple statement ef the wily Stacey they opened the " house" and the public purse liberally, and sought to make the closing days of the " hero's" life pleasant to him. But the publication of kis story had unpleasant results. The records of het War Office showed that Stacey was born five years after the battle of Waterloo. 'ibis would seem te dispose of his claim to having assisted the Iron Duke and that mythical hero " Bill Adams " to put Bonaparte to flight. Whatever may be Stacey's claim to be regarded as a hero, there can be no doubt that he has played " the old soldier " over the Nottingham Guardians most success- fully.</p>
        <p>Wynyard.where Lord Londonderry is entertaining the Prince of Wales,might,says the " St. Jamei's Gazqte," be regarded as a most unlucky place, for it has twico been destroyed by fire. Lord Londonderry's grand- father pulled down the old manor-house of the Vane- Tempests, and expended £200,000 in building a huge Grecian palace. This was burnt in 1841. Lord London- derry's view of the disaster was recorded in his journal : —" Here" (he was then abroad) " I learnt that Al- mighty God, for reasons only known to Himself, had been pleased to destroy by fire my house at Wynyard,in the county of Durham." The second Louse wis des- troyed in 1861. When 1881 drew near *great precautions were observed, for it was feared that the cycle of fire might come round again and the third house be des- troyed by fire, like its predecessors. The omen, however, was not fulfilled.</p>
        <p>The Emperor of Russia, says the " Westminster Gazette," offered the Order of St. Andrew to Lord Salis- bury during his visit to Balmoral, but it was declined in accordance with the rule which forbids a British Minis- ter to accept any foreign decoration. Except Royalties the only British knight of the Order of St. Andrew has been the Duke of Wellington, who received this order from the Emperor Alexander I. a shorz time after the battle of Waterloo. The St. Andrew is really a valuable gift, as the insignia include a star which the Emperor presents in brilliants.</p>
        <p>On the alignment of the great Siberian Railway there is a break which cannot be bridged. This is Lake Baikal, and in carrying out a contract for a steamer specially designed for this service the Elswick firm re- cently accomplished an engineering feat. The vessel, which is to carry the trains across, is of 4,200 tons, and measures 290 ft., by 571 t., with a draught of 19Mt. It has screws at stem and stern. Armstrong's received the order early this year, and delivered the ship in sec- tions maiLed, and: ready for dispatch, in six months. A peculiarity of the contract was that the sections had to be made as small as possible,for on the latter part of the route to the lake they were to be conveyed on sledges. The "Engineer " describes the whole enterprise as " without precedent." We doubt if even one of the new Ci,m.man shipbuilding concerns would have been equal to it.</p>
        <p>- ---'ol'--.</p>
        <p>A PROSPECTUS</p>
        <p>The well-known furnishing firm of Messrs. Wors- leys, of Manchester, Bradford, and London, is to be formed into a limited company to be known as Worsleys, Limited,and the prospectus will be found in our adl ertising colums. The company is to have a share capital of £75,000, divided into 3,750 seven per cent preference shares of £lO each and 3,750 ordinary shares of £lO each. The share list is now open and will close tomorrow (Saturday). We un- derstand that shares have been largely taken up,</p>
        <p>EMI BILLIARD TABLE Wanted, lift, by 4ft. 61n. or thereabout; must be cheap, and in fair condition.— Address A. ELLtaox, Man at the Wheel, Church-street. Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LANCASHIRE HUSSARS _DINNER,</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE HUSSARS _DINNER,</p>
        <p>The annual dinner to the " (.; " troop of Lanca- shire Hussars (Newton) took place at the Pied Bull hotel, Newton, on Saturday evening. Nearly all the members of tne troop were present, and the dinner being of a substantial character was much enjoyed. Mr. J. E. Pardey presided over the after proceeding, which were of a social character, songs being given by members of the troop.</p>
        <p>I. 0. G. T. TEMPERANCE MISSION LODGE. The usual weekly session of this lodge was held on Wednesday. Sis. Shipman, of London, G. V. T. visited the lodge and spoke in encouraging term: to the members, urging them to go and hear Bro. Malins speak in Wycliffa Hall. Bros. Jenkins, of Latchford,and 'Willis, of Earlestown, also spoke. Bro. O'Brien proposed and Bro. Hall seconded that the lodge tender their most hearty thanks to Sis. Shipman, and this was carried unaninously. After the usual vote of thanks to the visitors, the lodge was closed in the usual way by Bro. Sullivan, C. T.</p>
