      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>MORE CHILD NEGLECT.-ANOTHER CHANCE GIVEN</p>
        <p>Before Al lerman Harrison, (chairman), Or. Gornall, and Councillor Roh,rts, ot the Warrington Borough Police Court on Friday Richard and Mary Newton, 216 Hind-street, were charged with having on the 3rd Octo- ber, and other dates during the past 6 months, neglected their children Fanny, Ev-ma, Thchard, Esther, Philip, and Ada.—lnspee'or M'Ateer said the defendants were the parents of eight children ranging in age from 18 years to 12 months but they were only charged with neglecting the 6 children produced. On the 3rd of this month he visited the defendant's house in company with P.C. Tamer. They found there the eldest girl. Alice, aged 18, ad Philip aged 4. This child was insufficiently clothed and had a large number of vermin on it. In the front kitchen there was nothing but in the back kitchen there was a small table and a broken chair. In a corner there was also the remains of an old sofa with something upon it like a sack of chaff. Upstairs, in the front room they found a broken-down bedstead and a filthy mattress but no bed coveting. The back room contained one mattrebs which lay on the floor rotten. He (the Inspec- tor) called again on the same night in company with Dr. Bowden and P.C. Sankey. They found both defend- ants there and two of the children. IF called again on the 6th inst.and found the female defendant ,who ,it all the blame On her husband. She had been cautioned by him p six or seven months ago but she had gone from bid to worse. He went to St. Mary's school on the 6.h inst.and saw to of the children there. The schoolmistress said they were so dirty she did not like putting them amongst the ethers.—P.C.'s Turner and Sankey corroborated the former remarking that he never saw a worse case in his life. The condition of the house made him feel it.—Dr. Bowden deposed that he visited the defendant's house at 9 p.m. on the 3rd October. He examined one child. It was very dirty and there were a great many marks of flea bites on the child's body. There was a very dirty bed underdeath the child and no bed-clothing except what appeared to be a jacket and skirt. The other beds in the house were also filthy and the rooms foul.—T wn neighbours of defendants' proved that sufficient food was purchased for the children but could say nothing of the neglect.—A married daughter of defendants said Mrs. Newton was always a good mother to the chilAren. —The magistrates agreed to give the defendants another chance if they promised to abstain from drink. They gave the required promise and the case was adjourned for a month. Mr. Taylor, P.C.M., promised to look after the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH FOLIC': COURT. FR mi.Y.—D.forA Alderman Harrison (in the chair), Dr</p>
        <p>Gornall. and Coonefflor Roberts</p>
        <p>BEGGIvG ER(43I I SHOPKEEPER—John Dixln last from Whines. was charzed with begging in Turner-street at 6.15 on Thursday night - P.C. Bell at ted he followed the de- fendant into a shop in Turner-street and heard him ask the woman in charge for a copper. She refused him and witness then took defendant in custody. He was drunk.—Defendant was committed for 14 days' h rd labour —Charles Carter. last Irons Bi kanhead, aged 16. was charged with a similar oNence In Gladstone-street.—P.C. Cooper proved the case and de- fendant was discharged with a caution. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY.—WiIIIaM UOIAII W4B granted temporary to sell at 14, St. James-street In lieu of Margaret Hughes. MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor. Alderman Pairclough (in the chair), Alderman Harrison. Mr. J. W. Hayward, and Mr. S. Wright. WINDOW BREAKING.—Frank Hares, 6, Hollow-yard. was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Bank- street, and with committing damage to a window, the prenerty of John Matthews.—P C. Edwards proved the case, and defendant WP 3 sent to prison for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>BEGGING IN THE STREET.—Blizabeth Grimshaw, no arldres. was charged with begging in Bridge-stre-t on Sun- day night.—P.O. F Cooper stated that he saw defendant go up to three young ladies and receive a penny. She was in drink.—She was sent to prison or 14 days with hard labour. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Dennis Ryan, tanner's labourer, 16, Union-street,wa charged with having used obgeme language in Dolman's-lane on the 10th October.—P.o F. Cooper stated that defenrlan persisted in using obscene language in thq presence of a young laiy and others.—Defendant was fined IN.-including costs.</p>
        <p>TUKSDAY.—Before Mr. E. J. Guest (chairman) and Coun- cillor Henry Roberts. A SoN Asesurirs HUI MOTHER. —Janl63 Sallee, a young man was ch,lrged with having assaulted Mary Sance, his mother on the 11th August.—The Chief Constable informed the Bench that the case was an adjourned one from the pr vious day in consequence of the prisoner's mother, who issued the summons, not appearing to proseelte. She now appeared.—The complainant stated that on the date named the prisoner her son, hit her on the side of the head and knocked her down. He was drunk at the time —The prisoner said did It accidentally. He did not strike her with an intent to commit any injury.—The Chi 4 Constatde said it was the prisoner's 27th appearance, and read out the various charges which had been preferred against him.— The prisoner said it was very hard to have those brou tilt up against him. He had suffered for them —The magistrates committed him to prison for two months with hard labour. —The prisoner said he ;t RS innocently as a child. AN .AU9TRALIIN DRUNK. Fanny Collier. of no tixed address, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Sankey-street the previous rught.—P.C. Taylor proved the case. The defendant said she was going to M,nehester. where a gentleman would ad- vance her the capital to go back to Melbourne where she had lived for a number of years.—The Bench discharged her on her promising to leave the town.</p>
        <p>SdnOOL CABE.4 —The following cases were deal with : Patrick Donoghue, 1 Refuge-square, lAbourer. 2s ; Jo;eph/ Pot:Rev, 23 I-Icicle-street. forgemn, ss. ; Richard Tranter, 2, Warburton-street, forgeman. ss. ; John Kelns, 16 Silver-st I,llyourer. adj ,urned ; ,tary Hagan. 9 Stanley-street. dress- maker. adjourned. Mr. Jones, school attendance ofilmr prosecuted.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclouzh (in the chair)</p>
        <p>and Mr. John Geddes.</p>
        <p>BEGGING. Thomas Feeney was charged with b'g dng in Irlam-street at a quarter to 6 on Tuesday night.—P.o Woods proved the ease and added that defendant was drunk when apprehended.—The Chief Constable stated th It, complaints had been received about defend int begging from door to door and b-i e v-•ry cheeky when refused alms.—He wee committy-d for 7 (lays.</p>
        <p>THUllll)4.l%—Before lir S. Chandl,v (in the chair),</p>
        <p>Su't. n. and Col. Ridlwav</p>
        <p>EARLY INTO'X-U7,ATED.—Martill Griffin, Mich ,e 1 lfennesey, and John Lawrenson were charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Bank-street, at 9 45011 Wednesday morning.—p.e. Ryla erlepoieo to finding the defendants all on the ground fighting in Bank-st7eet. They were drunk.—A penalty of 14 days In gaol imposed on each. A QUEER Way OF iEEING A NEIGHBOUR.-Blin Pritchard, a woman who has -teen better times, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Howley-lane at 12 30 that morni g; she was further charged with breakinK a window, the prop-rty of John Walton.— She pleaded guilty.—P.C. Evans said that from a complaint he received he went to the back yardof John Walton, 103. Church-street, and saw three squares of glass broken. Afterwards he foiled the defendant drunk. She a iii" I have broken them and I dont eare." she lived next door to complainant. —Defendant told the Bench that she broke the window because she wanted to see Mrs. Waltma and she would not come to the door. She promis, d to reform and the cae was adjourned for a month to give her a chance of doing so.</p>
        <p>To THE DEAF.—A Gentleman who cured himself of Deaf ness and Noises in the Head after fourteen years' suffering will gladly send full particulars of the remedy post free.— Address F. CLIFTON, Amberley House, Strand, London. W.O</p>
        <p>it' AthZINCil ON C 011:: Y PErcYSESSiONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. - Before Mr. George Comber (chairman), _ \lr. J. White and Mr. William Heughton. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>THE LATE MAJOR PICKMERE.—Before commencing the business of the Court, the Chairman referred to the loss the bench had en teined through the death of Major Pickmere. Mr. Pickrnere lied not been a member of the bench very long, but in the neighboarlylod of Warrington he had been known for maw; years, and all would join in this expression of re,ret at hie death —Mr. If. Gre-nail (magistrates' clerk), said that on behalf of the offiAals of the Court he endoraed all that had been said. During the period he had been magistrate Major Pickmere had been a most useful ann. always willing and wishful to do his magisterial duties. —Mr. A. Browne. on behalf of the members of the her, also referred to the late gentleman. Mr. Pickmere, he said, had endeared himself to alt, He was the best of friends to the nramatic Scietv, end one of the best frier.ds to young fellows starting in life. A SLEEPY DRIVER FINED. —John Sumner was fined 10e. and costs for being asleep in charge of a lurry and two horses la Sankey on the 2nd Octob:tr. P.C. Thorley proved the case.</p>
        <p>DRUNK IN CEIARGE.—Ch tries Simpson was lined ss. includ- ing costs for being drunk in charge of a horse and cart in Croft on tip., 20th inst. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY. —Robert Robinson Heath was granted permission to sell at the " Swan Inn," Wiuwick, until next transfer day.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. 11.2nwri.Y.—Before Messrs. J. Handley, and T. Rigby.</p>
        <p>LICENSING.— Henry Shepherd made a application for per- mission to sell at the flutters vaults, Brunswick-street.— Grant ed. 2</p>
        <p>DR NICENNEM -Leanord Maddocks, boatman, Mill-brow and John Burges3 Wellington-street, were each tined be. for being drunk in Egerton-street, on the 14th inst. A NUISANCE.—Mr Percival. nuisance inspector. applied for an order against James Collier. Weston to abate a anis- e.' ce in connection with Some cottages in Granville-street by removing their existing privies, and replacing them with water closets. The application was granted. TURN ,P LOVERS —Walter Mather wad William Henry Cooke, two boys residing in Surrey-street, were each ordered to pay the costs 43. 6d for :tea ing turnips growing i a field belonging to George Acton Halton,on the 7th inst.—The pro- secutor said he raw the boys break through the fence and pluck up several turnips. He had experienced a great deal of trouble with boys coming to his fields and damaging th ' crops. Ile did not wish to press the case, but hoped it woul be a warning to them and ethers. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASES.—The following persons were I fined for sending their children irregularly to school : Emma Moore, Robert-street; Edwin Roberts. Wellington-street ; Jt.reph Findlow. Sutherland-street; lit rnard Flanigan, Al- cock-rtre t ; and Wm. Johnson, Surrey-street.--An order wa I made in the case of William Ellis, Surrey-street. Mr. Thos I Bowyer, School Board Officer, prosecuted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>44TH A PPEARANCE.—EIIen Gandy, a well-known character was charged with being inc Table in Mersey-street on Mon- day night. Sup, rintendent Okell said the prisoner was arrested on Monday night by P.C. Buckley. On Tuesday ruo ning she was not in a fit state to appear before the magis- trates, as she was then very drunk. It was her 44th appear- ance. A fine of 10s. and costs or 14 days in default was im- posed. THEFT OF A SOVEREIGN. William Henry Tonkin was charged with stealing a sovereign from the pockets of Bertie Matthews, in the Sailor's H me, on n nday, the 19th inst. —Prisoner was observed with his hand in the . r .socutor's pockets. When prosecutor got up he at once missed the sovereign. luformatian was given to the p and Detec- tive Gregory apprehended Tonkin the same night in a publin house in Liverpool. He was charged with the offence, which he didn't deny.—A fine of 30s. and costs or 14 days with hard labour in default, was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>HEALING PROPERTIES OF P IJRIE lED PETROLEUM.</head>
        <p>HEALING PROPERTIES OF P IJRIE lED PETROLEUM.</p>
        <p>Everyone knows of the healing effect of petroleum (vaseiline, petroleum jellies, etc.) when applied locally to the skin for inflammation, bruises, etc. Vet, few, how- ever, stop to think that it can have an equally healing effect when taken internally upon inflammations of the mucous membrane (the internal skin, so to speak) lin- ing the throat, lungs, stomach, and intestines. Such, however, is the case, and when properly purified for in- ternal use it exerts not; only a soothing and healing effect, but an anti-septic or anti-fermentative action as well, preventing fermentation and aiding digestion and assimilat;on, while at the same time it possesses a food value equal to that of cod-liver oil. More- over it acts directly through the blood, and being anti- septic, tends to destroy the germs of disease. Angler's Peiroleuni Emulsion consists of .a highly purified taste- less petroleum oil combineci with hypop:rstahites. tlt is pleasant to talc( and agrees with the most delicate stomach. If; will cure the worst cough, heal any inflam- mation of throat, lungs, stomach, and intestines, and do more to prevent and cure consumption than any other remedy. It is a food, as well as a medicine, and aids digestion, increases weight and strength, and builds up the system generally. It is far superior to cod-liver oil in the treatment of all chest affections and wasting dis- eases. Prescribed by the medical profession and sold by all leading chemists, 2 91. and 4s. 6d. A sample bottle sent free on receipt of 3d. to cover postage. D.scriptive pamphlets post free. Aagier Chemical Co., 32, Snow 13i11, London, E.C.</p>
