      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ANOTHER CASE OF WOUNDING AT</head>
        <p>ANOTHER CASE OF WOUNDING AT</p>
        <p>GOLBORNE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court on Friday befor Mr. J. Tinsley, James Carlon and Patrick Carney, o Golborne, were charged on remand from Monday with• having on the 10th instant unlawfully wounded Thomas Cooper at Golborne. Mr. Percy Davies, solicitor, Warrington, represented the prisoners. Thomas Cooper, miner, 17 Harvey-lane, Golborne, the complainant, said that on the 10th inst. at .11 p.m. he was going up Heath-lane towards home when Carlon, Carney,and another man rushed at him. Carney was the only one witness knew at the time. The prisoners lifted him up and then threw him down,and Carney com menced kicking him. He kicked him on the top and left side of the head. Carlon got on top of nim (complain- ant), and c)rnmenced biting his left ear. He bit it several times and complainant bled a good deal from t e top of his head and near his left eye and ear. P.C. Gough came up and Carney ran awy. The constable pulled Carlon off complainant who was then taken into Mrs. Goulding's house, close by, Dr. Dobb attending him. He (complainant) never exchanged a word with any •f the three men before the night of the assault and never had any quarrel with them. By Mr: Davies: He did not rush at Carlon and strike him with a stick. He did not hold Carlon. He never struck a blow with any stick. Though he had had a little drink he was perfectly sober. Emma G mlding deposed to seeing the prisoners and another man kick Cooper and knock him down. Carney kicked complainant three times on the head, and Carlon was lying on top of complainant biting his left ear. She tried to pull Carlon off but could not. P.C. Gough pulled him off when he came up. Cooper remained with wit- -1:1048 until Sunday night as he was to ill to go.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Davies: Carlon was not trying to get away but was struggling to bite. Joseph Hibbert also deposed to seeing Carney kick cpmplainant and Carlon bite him as described. James Dobb, physician and surgeon said that he ex- amined Cooper at Mrs. Goulding's house after the as- sault. There was a wmund on the top of his forehead and one on the head. 133 th were cut the bone. There were teeth marks on the left ear and the top and bottom lobes were bitten throu•zh. The wounds had bled very much and might have been mused by kicks. By Mr. Davies : He was sure fr-)m the shape of the wounds that they were caused by kicks. P.C. Gough said that on the night in question he saw a crowd and going towards it met Carney running away. Further on he found Cooper and Carlon on the ground. Carlon was biting Cooper's ear, and witness pulled him off. He locked Carlon up. From information after- wards received he apprehended Carney at his residence and charged him. To the charge Carney replied " I knocked him down right enough, but he struck me with a stick first." Prisoners were committed to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions to take their trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>AWFUL TRAGEDY NEAR CORK.</head>
        <p>AWFUL TRAGEDY NEAR CORK.</p>
        <p>MURDER OF A WIFE AND TWO CHILDREN. SURRENDER OF THE HUSBAND.</p>
        <p>Cn Saturday night. at Passage West, six miles from Cork, a man named Edward Foley, aged thirty-five, pre- sented himself at the police barracks with an open razor and hands covered with blood. He said he had murdered his wife and two children, and that it WAS his wife's fault, as the last child was not his. Foley was secured, and the police, directed by the prisoner, proceeded to the gate lodge of Mr. Flynn, J.P., of Florsehead, few hundred yards away. -Lying across the kitchen floor they found the dead body of the woman, her throat cut from ear to ear. Close to her was a live months' old infant, with its head nearly severed from its body. In another room another child, a boy of fur years, viva. quite dead, his sfiull having been battered in. A hatchet, smeared with blood, was found in the yard,and it is thought that with this the boy was murdered. Foley had for some time lived on bad terms with his wife, of whom he made no secret that he was jealous.</p>
        <p>A Birmingham firm has, it is reported, received an order from the Russian Government to strike brorzt, money to the value of 800,000 roubles.</p>
