      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>The above match played on the Wilderspooi ground on Saturday, was a fixture between the present tail of the League and the recent tail. The homesters were in the latter position, being at the time third from the bottom, and victory for either side meant a rise in the table. This alone was sufficient incentive to good play and both sides had practically their full team on. Sal- ford were represented by H. Mauwaring, full-back ; G. Fawcett, W. Crocker, J. Frame, and A. Pearson, three- quarters ; Ivor Grey and Ben Griffiths, ha &amp;backs ; J. Jones, R. Moss, H. Woodllead, S. E. Hanson, G. Heath, G. Fisher, J. Lions, and H. Davies, forwards. The Warrington team was, ileesom, full-back, Radford Barber, Cross, an 4 Lawless, three-quarters ; Bate and Houghton, half-backs; Nevins, Swift, Hili, Burrows, Barrett, Thornton, Robey,and rdeacock, forwards. Bur- ton was the only absentee. There were about 6,000 spectators on the field and a strong breen blew from goal to goal towards the railway. Mr. Bruckshavr was the referee, _ _ -</p>
        <p>J. Jones started for the " Reds " and put the ball in touch. From the scrimmage, Salford got possession and were starting to follow up when Barber returned the oval into the visitors' territory. Bate then got 103Sql- sion and attempted to run round the scrimmage but Grey was toe quick for him. Further ground was gained by 'kicking and Warrington were getting dangerous when Grey relieved by a streng punt. Houghton got away from the scrimmage and alter running smartly transferred to Bate who was collared. Again the visi- tors came up with a rush but a mark by Heesom stayed their progress though it was no advantage to his side. A pass from Grey was missed by Griffiths and before tete latter could lecover, Radford kicked the leather in touch at the corner. Temporary relief was given byj Manwar- ing, but from his kick a mark was aim at made in front or the goal. Another scrimmage was, however held and a plucky save by Nevins kept play in that quarter. Off- side play by Warrington caused them to be penalised and the " Reds" reached the quarter-fig. Bate and Houghton still obtained possession and soon had the threequarter line running finely. Oae of Bete's swinging passes travelled right along until Radford was reached, and the latter filed. Immediately after, the same player retrieved himself by taking a pass from Bate and transferring neatly to Barber whose kick found touch right at the corner fiac.l. Here there was some very lively work and it took the Salford men all their time to keep the home forwards off the line. Thornton took a free-kick and missed the goal, but play was still in favour of Warrington though at the opposite side of the field. More scrimmaging followed, and Bate securing adopted his tactics of last week by passing out to the centre, Cross,and the latter dropped a magnificent goal. This reverse tickled the visitors up and it was not long before they paid the Warrington half a visit. Grey and Griffiths got the ball away, but Lawless intercepted a pass, and Radford panted the leather far up the field. By a very smart "follow ' he prevented Grey returning and forced him into touch. Frame made a useful burst and Warrington had to de- fend. Heesom was tackled with the ball and th'ngs looked warm for Warrineton. Houghton and Cross however made exchanges which transferred play to the Salford line once more. Manwaring was forced to kick dead and a five yards scrimmage followed. Here the opportunity of scoring was lost through Houghton get- ting off-side, and Salford getting out of their difficulties by a free kick. Griffith e then got smartly away and made a fine run which was well supported. Just as he was passing to Grey, Barber appeared on the scene and upset both Griffiths and his calculations effectively. Houghton assumed the aggressive by a long kick and made for the line but was tackled. Radford and Barber then looked like getting away but the latter was brought down. The homesters were still press- ing when Woodhead got hold of the leather and breaking away made a splendid run eluding both Heesom and Lawless. In the scuffle, however, he lost the ball, and Lawless kicked in touch. The two Welsh halves were next prominent by some good passing, Griffiths evading Lawless and being only just tackled in time by Barber. Again Grey set the ball travelling and was becoming dangerous when Bate dropped him by the feet. At this period the homesters were having a warm time of it and the forw3rd:l fieen/ed t).9 !Nine ground. Salford for Ced a, minor and a big kick and I spurt by Lawless was welcomed. Bate initiated more passing but Radford By a wild pass almost let the Visi- tors through. Pearson made a dashing rue and kick but Lawless was speedy enough to overhaul hint and knock him in touch. A flying kick sent Radford and 'Barber at top speed after the ball. Manwaring secured but Barber upset him and kicked in touch near the cor- ner. Grey and Griffiths were again instrumental in openine the passing but Warrington gave no ahance and half time arrived with the homesters leading by a drooped goal to nil. Hill restarted and Griffiths made a peer return. Swift intercepted a pass from Grey and kicked in touch. A free kick was given Warrington but Cross failed to (nake good use of it. Salford also had the same chance given them but nothing resulted. Bate made a long throw from touch and Cross taking the ball kicked well up to the corner. A scrimmage formed and "Smack " secur- ing passed rapidly to Radford, who made no mistake in crossing the line. Nevins took the kick from a difficult angle and mide a good attempt at goal. Nay now was very exciting both teams working hard. The home for- wards freshened up while the visiting backs made them- selves dangerous. A kick by Barber was re- turned well by Fawcett. A moment later Barber took a pass and made for the line at a great rate, Cross keeping well up. At the right moment the former pressed to Cross who attempted to drop but the oval went dead. One of the visiting forwards did good work by a run and kick from a line out hut a well-judged return by Houghton altered the complexion of matters. A splendid bout of passing along the Warrington line gave Barber another chance and he made a brilliant effort and transferred to Cross onee more. The latter was tackled and the ball rolled over the line. Manwaring proved a steady custodian and one of his long kicks sent Warrington to the right about. Grey passed to Frame but the latter made a terrible mull and Bate picking the ball from his feet rae over the line in fine style. Fleesom took the kick from an easy place and failed. Time soon after arrived with Warrington victors of a fine game by one dropped goal, two tries (10 points) to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LANCASHIRE v. CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE v. CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>This match, under the auspices of the Northern Union, took placl at Stockport. before 8,000 people. Lancashire won the toss, and W. Faulkner kicked cif for Cheshire against the wind. J. Faulkner was at once prominent, hut Berry returned finely, and danger was averted. Valentine next got possession, and, evading both Robin- son and Saville, got a try for Lancashire at the corner. Coop failed to convert. A free kick against Lancashire proved dangerous,but Messenger eventually felieved,and Chapman getting possession gave the ball to Valentine, who enabled Berry ta complete the try. Coop took the place kick, and converted the point. Half-time : Lanca- shire, one goal and one try ; Cheshire, nil. On resuming Cheshire attacked, and the opposing citadel was threatened with danger. Warder, from a mark, failed at goal, and then Valentine gained some ground with a good run. Robinson appeared likely to gain Cheshire a try,but Chapman caught him a yard from the goal line. A few minutes afterwards Chapman was prominent with a splendid run and kick. He and Saville raced for pos- session, but the Stockport man eventually proved the speediest,and kicked the ball dead. Result . Lancashire one goal and one try ; Cheshire,nil. Tea.ma :--Cheshire: Saville (Stockport), back ; Robinson (Stockport), Sands (Stockport), Butterworth (Runcorn), and Warder (Run- corn). threequarter backs; Allen (Stockport) and J. Faulkner (Runcorn), half backs; W. Faulkner (Run- corn), Mottershead and Gar•ide (Stockport). Walker (Runcorn), Longtherpe (Stockport), Langley (Runcorn), Farmer (Runcorn), and Moss (Dukinfield). forwards. Lancashire : Coon (Leigh). back ; Hol:nes (More- cambe), S. Lees (Oldham). Chapman (Broughton Ran- gers), and Valentine (Swinton), three-quarter backs ; Berry ( fyldeeley), and Messenger (Broughton Rangers), half backs; Briers (St. Helens), Bonsor (Oldham), Clem- inson (Broughton Rangers). Brown (Wigan). Worthing- ton (Tyldesley), Simpson (Rnchdale Hornets). Edwards (Oldham),and Robinson (Broughton Rangers)s forwards. Referee, Mr. H. Bairstow. Hunelet. THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>THE YORKSHIRE TEAM FOR THE LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE MATCHES.—On Wednesday night, after the County trial match at Walt field, a meeting of the York- shire Committee of the Northern Union was held, when the following team were selected to meet Lancashire and uheghire in the forthcoming matches :—Lorimer, Mao- ni“gharn, full back ; Copper, Bradford ; Goldthorpe, Hunslet ; Firth, Halifax ; and Hombrecht. BramleY three quarter backs; Sunderland, Maneingham ; and Rigg,flalifax, half backs ; Kitson,Balifax ; ciefm. Man- ningham ; Sutcliffe, Fluddersfii-ld ; Walton, Wakefield ; Sneed, Castleford ; Suirden, Brighouse ; Conley, Leeds Parish Church ; and Robertson,Bradford.forwards, Rr- seves : Chorley,Efalifax ; full back ; Brook, Brighouse; Brown, Winningham ; Boothroyd, Huddersfield ; and Lempriere, Hull,thre,.-quarter backs • Wood, Brighouse, half hack ; Walsh, Fliinslet ;Doukin,BUll ; and Debney, Fludderafield, forwarrla,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CA RD</head>
        <p>A CA RD</p>
        <p>ErNOWN as ev. Joseph Mimes' Mexican Prescription 11,- is the only original guaranteed Remedy for all those wh,) suffer from nervous weakness, exhausted vitality. kidney or bladder diseases, gravel, prostate, spinal weak' ness, brain fag and kindred complaints. It has stood the test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed stamped nvelope to Jos. lior.stEs Remedy Co. Bloomsbury Man cions, Bloomsbury Square, London, and get full particulars of this groat specific free of charge. Mention whether for male or female, Beware of fraucbslent imitators.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night, a meeting of the committee of the Lancashire section of the Northern Union was held at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Manchester ; Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes, presiding, The chief business was the granting of permits and transfers, the principal of which were as follow Widnes, Batey, of Tyneiale ; to Saltord,W. Crocker and Gres- (Nlorriston. South Wales),and Griffiths (Penygraig) ; to Broughton Rangers, Mitchell, of Elland; to Oldham, Marsland, of Failsworth ; to Warrington, Cooper of Barnsley ; to Leigh, Hosker, of Rlackley ; to Sc. Helens, Holland (Hull Kingston Rovers), Lewis (Lianeny), and Williams (Llanelly Rovers) ; to Swinton, F. Crocker (Morriston), Evans (Cardiff), and Mulligan (Dublin). It havini, been reported to the committee that a hostile demonstration took place at the close of the Swinton v. Oldham match three weeks ago, it was decided that the referee be asked to give his version of what took place. Stockport and Swinton Were fined 53. each for a late start at Stockport on October 3rd.</p>
        <p>RuscoßN 4' A " v. ROCKSAVAGE.—Owing to the county fixture between Cheshire and Lancashire a hasty ar- rangement was made with the newly-formed Rock- savage club. A very pleasant game was the result. The more experienced Runcornians gained an easy victory, although playing a couple of men short. The final result was—Runcorn two goals five tries to one try.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. S. EIRLENS " A."—Played at Warrington on Saturday. The first half was very even, not a point being scored, but on turning round the junior 4 had all the best of the game, tries being 3c6ored by DAkin, Buckley, and Rutter, Sidtiall improv- ing the last two, and as Allen dropped a goal for St. Helens the game resulted in a win for the home team by 13 points to four.</p>
        <p>WARRINCITON Loco's v. WIDNES Sr. MARY'S.—This match was played at Warrington in fine but dull weather. The Locos won the toss and for some time after the commencemlnt the play was very even, the homesters having slightly the best of matters. Eventu• ally they asserted their superiority and from a scrim- mage in the visitor's twenty-live R tybold dropped a net goal. Following this success the Loco's played up strongly and Appleton scored at the corner, Mason con- verting. Another try was scored shortly after by Ray- bold but this was not improved up.4n. The whistle sounded for half time with the homesters leading by 2 goals 1 try to nil. In the second half the home team had the best of matters and tries were scored by Powny, Dutton, and Bouldsworth. Mason converting two, the Locos ran out out winners by 4 goals (1 dropped) 2 tries (25 points) to nil. The Widnesians although beaten by so much played a good game ; the forwards were very little behind the home lo", and on several occasions got away very fincly.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. LIVERPOOL AND DIS rßic r CONII3INATION.</p>
        <p>ST. ELF/YIN'S v. GARSTON.—PIayed at Dalton Bank on Saturdar. The Saints with their unbeaten record expected two points out of the match, and were not dis- appointed. Garston won the toss and had the assistance of a good wind, but the homesters had the best of matters, yet could not score in the first half ; Garston scored in a bully, and crossed over with a lead of a goal. The re-start was sensational, J. Duckworth at once put- ting the Saints ma an equality, and from the. Garston had no show. Pendlebury was the next to score with a grand cross shot, and from a freee kick well put ii by Colley one of the Garston backs put through his own goal. Hunter made the score four with a terrific shot during a scrimmage in goal, and the final was—St. Elphin's, four goals ; Garston, one. The Ga.rstou goal- keeper deserves a word of praise for an excellent exhibi- tion between the sticks. St. Elphin's team : Goal, Robson : backs, A. Duckworth and C.)lley ; half backs, Jones, Thompson and hunter; forwards, J. Duckworth, Mckinbottorn, Dagdlle, Batton and Pendlebury.</p>
        <p>G IintENDALE V. ST. ELPHIN'S BRIGLIM.—This match was played ota the groun of the former team at Port Sunlight, Birkenhead, on Saturday. Hastry kicked off for the visitors, and after a few minutes' play Nichol- son shot a very difficult goal for the Saints. On play being recommenced Darby equalized for the homesters. After this, however, play went on very evenly for a short while, where Spendilow with a fine shot added a second goal for the Saints. Just before half-time ['Wry al,* added a third point for the visitors. Thus when half time was called the Saints led by three goals to one. On play being resumed the Saints soon forced a corner, from which Elastry headed a fine goal for them. The game was now all in favour of the visitors, and just before time was called Efastry added a fifth point to the score from a penaltY. The visitors ran out easy winners by five goals to one, LOIS! V. HROADItEATFt.—PIayed at Lymm. Broad- heath began with the wind at their backs, ansl soon were busy sending in smart shots at the Lymm goal, but the goalkeeper seemed to be always in the right place, so their efforts went without reward. Seeing that the Lymm players were managing to keep their powerful opponents at bay the homesters took heart, and were soon sem down at the other end, and after a splendid bit of scrimmaging in the Broadheath geal mouth, the ball was worked through. This proved to be the only goal scored up to half-time. Broadheath again went off with a dash, and in a few minutes scored a soft goal, thus equalizing. This might easily have been saved, but the Lymm captain, Hugh Bolt, had got kicked, and could not get at the ball with his accustomed speed, hence the (goal. In a few minutes, from a foul against Lymm, Williams for Broadheath placed the ball beautifulli, in goal mouth, which was equally well headed through, and so Broadheath were again in front. At this point Broadheath were certainly going very strong, but to the great surprise of the shouters the Lymm lads broke away, ran the length of the field and scored a goal almost before anyone had time to realise what had happened. Try as they would neither side could score again, and the game ended in a draw of two goals each. STOCKTON id KATIE V. DA RESET:TRY. —Played On the ground of the former on Saturday, the homesters win- ning after a hard game by six goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS CHARGE. AGAINST A WA.RRINGTON MAN.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Police Court, on Saturday, be- fore Mr. W. J. Stewart, stipendiary magistrate a power-fully built man, named Thomas Walsh, of Warrington, said to be a boxer, was charged with assaulting John W illiams, a retired tobacconist, re- siding in York-terrace, and robbing him of a gold chronometer, valued at £25. Detective-constqble Gibson stated that from information received and a description, he and Detective-constable Paterson arrested the prisoner in Clare-street on Friday at five o'clock, on the charge named, the offence hav- ing taken place in Scotland-place, Liverpool, at half-past two on Friday afternoon. Prisoner denied having committed the assault and robbery, bat said he was " with the men who took the watch," and admitted having pawned it. Prosecutor iadentified prisoner as the man who robbed him. When prisoner was searched a silver lever watch, No, 2,288, was found in his possession. A remand was ordered for seven days.</p>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT IN KNUTSFORD-ROAD.-A WARNING TO Boys.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, conducted an inquiry at the Parade Room, Irlam-street, on Mon- day afternoon, concerning the death of ahoy named Timothy Quinn, who died from the effects of injur- ies sustained by being run over by a Cart in Knuts- ford-road on Saturday.—Mary Quinn, wife of Thomas Quinn,3s, Old-road, Latchford, bricksetters' labourer, said the deceased was her son. He was seven years of age last birthday. Witness left her home at 5.30 a.m. on Saturday morning for the factory where she worked, and left the dec-ased, who was the youngest child, at the house. He was lying dead at the Infirmary when she next saw bun. —James Albert Campbell, a lad, seven years of age, said he was playing with the deceased on Saturday. Deceased was riding behind the Latchford 'bus, and when he dropped off he ran against the wheel of another cart and was knocked down.—Charles Hughes, Mill Farm, Stockton Heath, said he was in the 'bus in question and he saw the deceased go from behind the 'bus and come in contact with the wheel of a passing cart. He was knocked down and the wheel went over the deceased's neck. Wit- ness did not see him move a hand or foot after- wards. The driver was on the opposite side of the cart to that, at which the accident happened. Neither of the drivers of the vehicles were to blame. It was a common practice in Warrington for boys to ride behind vehicles, and he wondered that more accidents did not happen.—Tbe jury returned a verdict of " accidental death," and exonerated the drivers from all blame,</p>
        <p>VVAItitINGTON CRICKiA AND BOWLING</p>
        <p>CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL DINNER.</p>
        <p>At the Patten Arms Hotel, Warrington, on Saturday evening, the annual dinner in connection with the War- rington Cricket, Bowling,and Association Football Club was held,and was fairly well attended, In the unavoid- able absence of the President (Mr. W. H. Robinson) through illness.Mr.Thomas Mains, F.G.S.. presided,and the vice-chair was occupied by Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins. The Mayor (Alderman Fairclough) was amongst those present. After an excellent repast which did credit to the caterer,Mr. Kiernan, the Chairman submitted the toast the Queen. and in doing so referred to her long reign which,he said, had broken the record. The toast was drunk with musical honeurs,Mr. George Barton,the renowned tenor singer of St. Helens,singing God save the Queen."—The Chairman then submitted the toast of the Prince of Wales and the Royal Family. He was sure they all prayed that the Prince of Wales might be as worthy a son as his mother had been a worthy woman. The Queen had been a model parent and a model Queen. (Applause.) Whilst they hoped that the Prince of Wales might be as worthy as his mother,they trusted he would have long life and good health.—Mr. Jenkins and Mr. Barton then sang " God blesi the Prince of Wales."</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN referred to the absence of the president of the club. He said he received a letter from Dr. Sherratt on Thursday sta.tinP that it would be necessary to provide another chairman for that evening as Mr. Robinson could not possibly attend. He (Mr. Morris) had seen Mrs. Robinson who told him that her husband had passed thmugh a very anxious time. He was a, little better that night. They all hoped that the presi- dent of the club would have a speedy recovery to health. (Hear. hear. Mr. Barton next sang " The Sailor's Grave."</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in proposing the toast of the "Army and Navy and Reserve Forces" said it was rather a new de- parture on his part to propose such a toast and he did not believe he had given it before. They all knew what their navy was like, and they were all proud of it.(idear, hear.) Their bluejackets e ere always there when they were wanted (laughter) and especially was that the case at Zanz:bar. With regard to the reserve forces, if an enemy invaded our shores, they knew how Quarter-Master Sergeant Hutchinson and his men would tumble them into the sea. (Laughter.) He would couple the name of Mr. Jenkins with the toast. After the drinking of the toast, Mr. R. T. Faircleugh sang "The fair-faced young curate." In responding, Mr. Jenkins said he did so with a great deal of pleasure, but if there was one thing more than another just at that period of the life's hiatory of the country which should be remembered it was that they should be guarded in their speech as to what they could do, or to what they might do if they were threa- tened with invasion, or what possibly might happen if they fought. Mr. Jenkins then proceeded to relate seme of his experiences abroad, and mentioned the interesting fact that Mr. Charles Holland, a present member of the Warrington Cricket and Bo %ling Club, at one time, carried Sir Jobn Kirk and Dr. Livingstone through the breakers at Zanzibar. In conclusion, he said he was sure Englishmen would always conduct themselves as they had done in the past, but he hoped the occasion would not arise. Hear, hear. Mr. R. T. Fairiceonos proposed the toast of the president. He said some of them would be aware of the , services their president had rendered in the course of his lifetime to the Warrington Cricket Club. Be per- formed the duties of treasurer when the cluh played at Bank Hall. When the club was obliged to leave their ereund there owing to the purchase pf it by the town, they removed to Arpley. The bulk of the work caused by that removal fell upon Mr. Robinson their president, and there was hardly a sod of that ground but what he personally inspected. He believed they had spent double the money upon the ground as would have beught it out and out at the start. They had spent £3,000 upon it, and Mr. Robinson had had a great deal to do wi h the raising of that amount. He (Mr. Fairclough) was 'delighted when Mr. Robinson was appointed president for he had done everothing which was possible to make it a success, and had been greatly instrumental in rais- ing it to its present high position. " For he's a jolly good fellow" wee sung after the drinking of the toast. Mr. Jenkins then read a cleverly composed song of his own, entitled : "The Angler's Song."---The Chairman briefly responded to the toast on behalf of the President of the Club. Two solos were next sung in an admirable manner by Mr. Barton entitled " Let me like a soldier fall " and " The Anchor's Weighed." The next toast was that of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club,"and it was proposed by Mr. Chidlaw. He said be had teen associated :with the club for a good , many years, and it had always been his desire to see the club prosper. When he first became associated with I the club it was in a small way, and as an old Town Councillor used to say "in fdifficults." But by dent of perseverance they overcame their difficulties and had now a ground second to none in Lancashire. The com- mittee worked amicably together and he was proud to see the club in its present position. He could not speak too highly of the services Mr. Robinson had rendered to the club. They started, as they knew, at Bank Hall, and it was then suggested that they should have a bowl- ing green attached. A bowling green was mad, which became very productive, and was really the backbone of the club. (Hear,hear.) The members kept inereas- ing,and everyone was satisfied. He could only say that he was proud to see the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club in its present successful position. He coupled the name of Mr. Unsworth with the toast. (Applause.)— After Mr. Chidlaw had read a poem appropriate to the occasion, Mr. Bell sang a comic song. Mr. UNEWORTH in response to the toast thanked Mr. Chidlaw for the manner in which he had proposed it. He could bear out what had been said with refesence to the old members of the club having laid the foundation of its present prosperity. In looking over an old balance sheet of the club for the year 1877 he saw that the sub- scriptions then amounted to £65 17s. 7d.,last year they were £4OO. (Applause.) So far as last season's records were concerned he thought they had every reason to be proud. The Cricket Club had done better last year than perhaps they had ever done before. (Applause.) Mr. Callon having sung" Ailsa Mine," the CHAIRMAN referred to a testimonial they were about to present to Mr. Chidlaw. He assured them that in collecting the money for the testimonial, the committee had had very little difficulty in doing so and all were agreed that Mr. Chidlaw had done useful service for the welfare of the Cricket Club. The amount collected was between £l2 and £l3, and in the name of the subscribers he asked Mr. Chidlaw to accept the articles which had been aent on to him. (Applause.) Mr. G. PILLING said the Testimonial Committee con- sisted' of the President, Mr. Morris, Mr. Jenkin, Mr. Crosfield, and himself. They met several times, to con- sider what form the testimonial should take. Mr. Chid- law was written to, and he replied saying that be was refurnishing his house and made a suggestion of a hat stand and arm chair. He hoped Mr. Chidlaw, his wife and family would long live to enjoy the hat stand, and also the arm chair. (Applause.) Mr. CHIDLAW in acknowledgleg the gifts said it was a pleasing feeling to him to know that although he was at Wrexham, he had not been forgotten by the members of the Warrington Cricket Club.' He thanked them very much for the kind manner in which they had appreciated his endeavours on behalf of the club. (Applause.) Mr. HOWARD sang" Oft in the Stilly Night" and Mt. Kenyon afterwards recited a piece of poetry of his own composition. KTERNAN then proposed the toast of the " Officers of the Club." He referred in praiseworthy terms to the services of the officers of the club,stating that they were all worthy men, and he was net at all surprised that the club had progressed as it had done under their guidance. He trusted that they would continue to do their duty. (Applause.) Mr. CHAS. PRICE having given a capital rendering of "They all love Jack." Mr. Gm. PILLING in responding thanked Mr. Kiernan for the manner in which he had proposed the toast. He felt that the officers of the Warrington Cricket and Bowl- ing Club were RS an efficient set as any he had ever met in connection with any other association. Mr. UNSWORTH also reponded, and said it had been customary on similar occasions to give the results of the matches of the Cricket Club. The past year had been an exceptionally good one. They had played twenty-one matches, won ten, lost six and drawn five. (Hear, hear.) He thought that was one of the best records that the Warrington Cricket Club had ever had. They had played the very best matches it was possible to get matehei with, and he thought next year the club would be even stronger than last year. They would have Dr. Smith, who was a hest in himself,back again—(epplause) —and they would also have their two professionals again who had done so well during the past year, and who could be relied upon. (Applause.) He thought they would go one better next year than the past. (Hear, hear and applause.) Mr. BIRTLES said he had a toast to propose of great importance, namely, the health of their worthy Mayor. (Hear, hear.) On that occasion they would drink his health with very great pleasure, not enly because he was the mayor of an important town, but also because he was a member of the bowling club. They met there on common ground. His Worship played bowls and com- peted and won a prize. They had all been very jovial and hearty that night, and would drink his health not because he was the chief magistrate of the borough, but because he was a bowler. (Applause.) The toasts was drunk with musical lienoure after which Mr. S. G. Jarman, of Wrexham, sang a solo.</p>
        <p>CLEAR COMPLEXION, LUXURIANT HAIR, Spotless arms, soft white hands, and shapely nails are produced by CUTICURA SOAP, the purest, sweetest,and most effec- tive skin purifier and beautifier of this or any age. It is so because it strikes at the cause of minor cutaneous affections, viz.: THE CLOGGED, IRRITATED, INFLAMED, SLUGGISH, OR OVERWORKED PORES. CUTICURA SOAP de- rives its remarkable purifying and beautifying pror erties from CUTICURA the great skin cure,but so delicately are they blended with the purest of toilet and nursery soap stocks that the result •is incomparably superior to all other skin and complexion soaps.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in responding, said he thanked Mr. Birtlea for the way he had prapo;ed his health, and the corn. pany for the manner in which they had received it. Be had been under the impression that when be came to the dinner that night his position as Mayor would be left behind. He was very sorry that their President was not with them, and he had come there to support him in the chair. Some of his (the Mayor's) happiest days had been spent on the cricket field with Mr. Robinson. He could not help referring to Mr. George Barton. He was the oldest cricketer he had played cricket with, and used ta play for St. Helms. There was very keen rivalry in those days, and he was as good a cricketer as he was a singer. He remembered him singing thee" ll,Jath of Nal. son"thirty-five years ago. He then recalled some pleasant recollections of Bank Hall and Colonel Wilson Patten. He (the Mayor had now come to the old man's game bowls (laughter) but he hoped he would be able to plaY bowls for many years to come. (Applaus ..) Mr. DINWIDDIR proposed the health ot Mr. Morris,who suitably replied. Mr Brayshaw fulfilled Vie duties of accompanist during the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>STOCKTON lIENTH WARDS SEPARATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE STOCKTON lIENTH WARDS SEPARATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>PARISH MEE LING.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a parish meeting was held in St. Thomas' schoolroom, Stockton Heath,for the purpose of giving the ratepayers a chance to discuss the proposed separation of the north and south wards and their tor- mation into separate parishea. Mr. F. Pearson presided. and there were also present Messrs. John Ellison (clerk). Ashton Hayes, J. Warburton, J. Whitley, C. W Devene port, J. Hallow, H. Isherwood, the Rev. S. P. Grey,and others. Mr. England, jun., before the business commenced. asked whether the meeting was a legal one. He did not wish to object for one moment on that ground,but it had been said in the village that seven days' notice was re' quired before the meetinE coutd be held. The Clerk said that as far as the meeting was nue- cerned it could be held after three (lees' notice, but be said seven days' notice had been given. The Chairman then said that the meeting was called for the puepose of considering the proposal to divide the township of Appleton into two parishes. They would observe that a notice was issued calling an inquiry that ' day for the purpose of considering the advisability of the proposal, and the Parish Council at the last meeting thought it was better to take the parish into their con- fidence as to what steps should be taken in the matter. If the two wards were separated,ae they were aware, it would result in an increase of rates as far as Stockton Heath was concerned, and the lessening of the rates for Appleton Ward. It appeared to him that there were several courses to adopt at that meeting. One was to protest against the separation, another to apply for tal adjournment of the inquiry, and then another course that could be adopted was to OP' ply for the powers of an urban authoritl. If the latter course were adopted he believed more officials would be required than at present. A further suggestion, and the most impertent was that thef should empower a legal representative to represent then; at the legal inquiry and to put their case as clearly ant` distinctly as he poseibly could. He thought the matter should be clearly gone into and that the expenditure they had incurred in improvements should, in case tbe separation was granted, be borne by the South Ward 10 its proper proportion. (Applause ) There was also e funded debt for Appleton as a whole and he took it- thst the County Council would put that right, hut they oegbt to empower someone to see that it was done properly. themselves. He mentioeed that Warrington had agree.l to take away the sewage, and Arpleton Parish Council were under an agreement with the Warrington Counell that in some future time they would bear the same Pre' portion of the expense. He thought that the Appletoe • Ward should bear their fair share of the expense also. Should the wards be separated it would result in a cog' siderable reduction of the rateable value. The north side would he taken away in November by Warrington at the extension of the borough, which would cause the rateable value to be £B.OOO instead of as at present £15.900. It was for toe meeting to consider what v 706 to be donle at the enquiry the day after (00 Wed nesday). Mr. Isherwocd thought the best course would be tcfl pass a resolution to protest against any separation the wards, and to empower a small committee to irr struct a solicitor to be present at the enquiry. Mr.Englencl, jun., spoke about the rateable value. fill wanted to know where the representatives of the Soot.. Ward were. They were not there at the meeting,ret was their proposal to separate the wards,and they sboul° be present to state their case. If the wards were selle: rated he thought they should do all they could to hal' all the liabilities properly adjusted. rhO Mr. Davenport endorsed what had been said. separation would relult in a considerable increase of t'r rates. Be wanted to know where the representatives2t the South Ward were. The whole affair had been broug" about by tnem.and he considered it was a "hole and el; ner " business from bf-ginnina to end. Everything be been done in private until the last minute,and there we: scarcely any time for the No.th Ward to do anything 1 the matter. They had only 24 hours to instruct a leg3j gentleman to take the matter in hand. HA 911pn°5% the separation would mean an increase of or rd. lot the £. The people of Appleton had derived great benreo, from the sewage, lighting, and emptying of the ashPl't, The lighting came to the boundary, as well as throuere the village and up to London Bridge. 111,,etie was a very great improvement in the road.. t`he main ones having been taken over bY County Council, and they were ercend none in this part of the county. (Hear, hear.) The eatrno of Appleton was far in excess of the are. of St.'" ;tea Heath but at the same time owing to the rapiditr which Stockton Heath wes being built upon it wee rre almrst as much in rateable value as Appleton' old thought they ought to strongly oppose the separation p, he ey•.*s surprised at the way the mattc-r had been gottger They must try and get proper compensation. An° thine v.-ae that Appletnn farmers had the ntivilten selling in Steckton Heath markets. They in S'oe ole Heath had to put up with all this humbug while " Appleton peep! • had the benefit all rourd. ended Mr. Warburton propeired and Mr. Whitler seen.' oth " that thie meeting of the ratepar or of Stockton 4—„,elee Ward protest avainst the division of the Parish ef ton into two distinct parishes."—The resolution</p>
        <p>carried unanimously. m On the 'motion of Mr. nouchier, seconded tic° Clarke. Messrs. Pearson. Davenport, and arhurb /to were appointed a committee to engage a solicitlr look after their interests at the enquiry. the' Mr. Clarke then proposed that any elcrwpflea - might he put to shout he charged to thr Parish tile ot Appleton.—Mr. England seconded, and l'one" The Council would get them charged on the rate motion was passed. t" A vote of thanks to tha Cl.airm,n conclude business.</p>
