      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>THE REV. J. MCNEILL AT WA RRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Friday night last the Rev. J. McNeills mission, at Warrington was brought to a conclusion and every available seat in the hall was occupied. On the platform accompanying the Rev. J. McNeill there were the Revs. John Yonge, J. Warnock, E. T. Reed, C. Finch, P. J. Byrne, W. Ptts, W. G. Triggs, Jones, G. Harbottle, C. Andrew ; Mr. Osborne (secretary of the Young Mens' Christain Association), Mr. John Fair- dough, J.P. and Mr. E. J. Guest, .J.P. After prayer by the Rev. P. J. Byrne, the 11.-v. J. MeNELE.r. said he wished to speak on behalf of an associa tion. The Warrington Young Men's Christian Associa- tion had rendered yeoman service in that mission, and he wished to urge that Warrington Association on young men. They had a thoroughly consecrated, converted, spiritually minded, zealous secretary in Mr. Osborne an.] he trusted that the association would be a success in this thriving and bustling town of Warrington. He (Mr. McNeill) wished to thank the united committee for their services in connection wits that mission, as they had done everything they could to help to win the people of Warrington to the side of Christ. Referring to the assistance he had received from the clergy, he said it would be unfair to single out a church that had not been with them. Although certain men had been missed, the people had been with them. (Applause.) He (Mr. McNeill) had no warmer suppor- ters than the evangelical iriends of the Church of Eng- land. The rev, gentleman proceeded to eulogise the services of the choir and its leader (Mr. Brough) and then thanked the stewards, and also his host and hostess (Mr. and Mrs. John Fairclough, J.P.), who he said at Latch- ford Grange had devoted themselves to his personal comfort. (Applause.) Mr. E. J. cuxsT. J.P., said he had a duty to perform that night nich was in one sense a pleasure,in another sense a pain. It was only about fourteen days since they had heartily welcomed Mr. McNeill to that town, but now they had to say" good-bye" to him and thank him for the magnificent service which he had ren- dered to them. He had great expectations when he came there and those expectations, he thought, had been more than realisei. (Applause.) Mr.lllcNeill was to most of them a perfect strangeribut he had endeared himself to every heart. He would' l and a preacher who had Parker had said that preset. Mr. IV</p>
        <p>3 an</p>
        <p>I was a born preacher. H qunnce, culture, great natural dramatic p,)wer with a learning easy of control, an i a deep insight into human nature, and a humour thtt had delighted them. They recognised him as a gift from God. Mr. McNeill knew that none of these powers were his but that they had been given to him for their goad. They had had a great uplifting and they must use their renewed strength in the service of God. There were some who said that those services were merely services full of excitement, and that when the missionary had gone they would fall into the original condition of langour or indifference. This should not be, and it would not be, if they were determined so. (Rear, hear.) In conclusion he ex- pressed the gratitude of all to Mr. McNeill for his services.</p>
        <p>natt</p>
        <p>:al !o</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE said Mr. Guest had spoken everything that needed to be said, and yet the sugges- tion made by the committee was that Mr. Guest should speak on behalf of the laity, and he (Mr. Yonge) on behalf of the ministers in reference to Mr. McNeill's presence and the work amongst them. He took it hat everybody who had been to those services wanted to thank Mr. McNeill for what he had done. They were thankful to God for the blessing on that mission which He had vouchsafed. But while they were grateful to Him, He would not wish them to let their preacher go with Yut a word of gratitude spoken to him. He was sure he spoke for his brother ministers when he said they heartily and intensely thanked him for the words it had been their privilege to listen to from him. He hoped they would all preach better after having heard Mr. McNeill. (Laughter.) He thought the Grand Old Book would live anew to them. Mr. McNeill had a won- derful faculty for making old stories quite new and he (Mr. Yonge) was sure as they listened to his sermons they had seen old st(iries in a new dress. He was sure the inflefence of those services waiuld not be lost upon their churches. (Ap- plause.) Warrington never saw sights like the present at religious gatherings in its history. Cert.inlv not within the four walls of a building, because they had not had in the town such a large building before. He regretted that the generous donor of that hall through absence from home had not been able to see the sights that hail had pre- sented night after night. He (Mr. Yonge) felt it would have been a matter of great joy and thankfulness to Mr. Parr to think that through his generous gift meetings like those had been possible. They were hoping that they would see the fruits of that mission and that they would reap a harvest from the labours of Mr. McNeill. , (Applause.) • The Rev. J. McNsatr., in acknowledging the thanks which had been expressed, said it had been a blessed time in Warrington. He paid a tribute to the labours of I the Rev. E. T. Reed,to whose exertions, he said, it was 'owing that he (Mr. McNeill) visited Warrington at all. the rev, gentleman then proceeded with his sermon. It may be well to note here Mr. McNeill's further lengagements for the winter season,so that his many friends may follow his movements with an intelli- Igent interest :—October 18 to 30, Middlesbrough ; November 8 to 20, Bradford; November 22 to December 4, Blackburn; December 6 to 13, Pres- ton ; January 10 to 31, Manchester; February 7 to 19, York; March 7 to 28, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CONCERT AT THE ROYAL COURT</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT THE ROYAL COURT</p>
        <p>THEATRE..</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon a concert of more than or dinary interest was given in the Royal Court Theatre, before an audience which, though fairly numerous,was,owing to the exigencies of the hour, chiefly confined to the reserved portions of the building. The concert party comprised Mesdames Rate Cove and Alice Gomez as solo vocalists, the famous Mons. Hollman as 'cellist, and the equally renownel Meister Glee Singers: It will be gathered that, with such an imposing array of musical talent, a rare treat was enjoyed by those privileged to attend the concert. Madame Gomez was,we un- derstand, suffering from c Id. but her singing was none the less effective. To the new song "Greeting and Farewell" her powerful contralto voice and finished style lent a charm which is hardly inherent in either the words or music, and in this as in her other items Madame Gomez was accorded a hearty reception by the audience. Madame Kate Cove's most successful efforts were in the songs " this Green Lane "and " The Rose Garden," in which the tender sentiments and the appropriate musical set- ting of each received a most artistic rendering. Mons.Holl man's technique is almost beyond reproach, and his performance of the 'cello solos set down in the programme was greeted by such a storm of applause as may be construed into a hearty wel- come on.his reappearance after an absence of six or seven years. The concert marked, we believe, the debut of the Mei*ter Glee Singers in Warrington and, although their concerted singing scarcely touched their high-water mark of finish,much of it must have come as a delightful surprise to many frequenters of our local concerts. Judging from the vociferous ap- plause which followed the close of such gems of vocal harmony as " Twilight" and " Forsaken "it may he expected that their second visit on the occasion of the Musical Society's Concert in the early part of next year will be looked forward to with pleasurable anticipations. Mr. William Forn- ington's song" Doris Darling" though common- place in character, was well sung. A much worthier song however was "The Capture of Bacchus" which Mr. Webster Norcross essayed with solid and well-deserved success.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Harrop, a Harrogate solicitor, was found dead on ruesday in the river Slack, near Boulogne, under mysterious circumstances. He had proceeded from Goole to Boulogne for his health, and had sustained an injury while on the boat.</p>
      </div>
