      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>THE MA.NC aESTER SEWAGE DISPUSA.T. SCHEME.</p>
        <p>ITS EFFECTS UPON WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The new scheme of the Manchester Corporation for the treatment of their sewage is likely to be a subject which will be brought prominently before the public of Warrington in so much as it will vi- tally affect the interests of the town if carried out in its proposed form. In pointing out in what respects the scheme is likely to injure Warrington it will be well in the first place to state briefly what the scheme of the Manchester Corporation is. The chief feature of it is the conveyance of the effluent from filters at Davyhulme to the tidal waters of the Mersey. The point recommended by Mr. T. de Courcy Meade, the Manchester city surveyor, for the admission of the effluent to the tidal water is near to Randall's Smices, which, as our readers may be aware, are used in connection with the Manchester Ship Canal, and are situated at an angle of the river Mersey be- tween Fidler's Ferry and Widnes. The effluent would be conveyed to this point in a conduit, fol- lowing approximately the line of the Ship Canalon the south side, and would be syphoned under the Canal, and then enter the river. The suggested con- duit would be about 151 miles in length, and it is intended that it should have a discharging capacity of 70 million gallons per day This will carry off, after treatment at the tanks a Davyhulme, all the dry weather sewage from Manchester, together with the street washings due to raintalls, or, in other words 98 per cent. of the total amount of sewage reaching the sewage works will be carried off by the conduit. The remaining fraction would enter the Canal only during the periods of very heavy rainfall. It is stated that toe , cost of this scheme would not exceed £258,000, and , by it the Manchester city surveyor is optimistic lenough to believe that the sewage problems of that enterprising city would be solved once for all.</p>
        <p>Now the dry-weather flow of sewage—as it is called—is estimated at about 40 gallons per head per 24 hours, and on this calculation the sewage flow from Manchester would. be about 26 million gallons, or 4,160,000 cubic feet. The result of the, abstraction of this water lrom the river Mersey—as will be the case by the new scheme—will be that one-third of the present flow of the river above Warrington will be taken away entirely. That is from Manchester alone, but if as has been suggested, Salford should join in the scheme the abstraction will be considerably more, and Warrington will be situated in an even worse position than they would be if Manchester only participated in it. It will need no great amount of foresigtt to see that this will be a great drawback to the navigation of the river. The abstraction of the upland-waters of such a river as the Mersey will not only decrease its scouting power, but will also make it the more liable to silt up, and it is im- possible here to contemplate the extent navigation , will be interfered with. The importance of the river to Warrington as a navigable stream cannot be too highly appreciated, and must be maintained. I No one can tell into whose hands the Ship Canal may ultimately fall, and it therefore behovesl the Corporation to keep the river as the whip- handle for everybody. It may be argued that the Ship Canal Company are obliged to dredge the river, and maintain a depth of eight feet, but still the Warrington Corporation have a right to maintain that the water from the upper reaches shall not be abstracted, more especially if the contention of the Manchester people is true, that the effluent will enter in a purified condition, If the tfiluent which is to enter the river is to be practically clear, then the question arises " " Why take it in a culvert to Randall's Creek,and rob the river at IVarrington of the benefit of it? Even in the event of this volume of effluent being put out at Randall's Creek.there is every reason to believe that tile sewage—however small a percentage—will oscillate backwards and forwards and never get clear out to sea. Another point which should not be lost sight of is, that the abstraction of the water from the upper reaches of the river will cause a larger volume of salt water to come up the river by the tidal flow. This will be the case owing to the fact that there will be less water in the river to keep back the tide. The increase of salt water in the river may not at first sight; seem a serious matter, but it must be remembered that many works, especially at Bank Quay, use the river water. Some of it is used in the raising of steam, and the addition of salt which will take place, will be a serious matter, and may in some cases make it practically useless. These are but a few of the many difficulties which present themselves to Warrington by the new scheme, and which will require the serious consideration of the Council.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY 'REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. During last week, which was the 41st, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington 39 23 Latchford</p>
        <p>Total 46</p>
        <p>The excess of deaths over births was 21. The death rate from all causes was equal to 22 3 per 1,000, against 31.8 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 14 9 the corresponding week of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 1.6 per .:,000, against 2•5 the average rate of the four previous weeks,and against 18.2 the correspeuding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 9 ;6 6 3 25</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from whooping cough, 1 from diarrhcel, 1 from Influeoz3, 1 from phthisis, 2 from tuberculosis, 10 from lung disease, 5 -from brain disease, 1 from heart disease.</p>
        <p>- The folliwing cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—erys,pelas, 1 case, scarlet fever, 8 cases. Total number of patients in hospital on Oct. 10th,-14 JOHN EL GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
