        <p>7Letters to tin 011ttor,</p>
        <p>A CORRECTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Kindly oblige me by inserting the following in next Saturday's issue :—Mr. Alderman Chandley's re- signation was read at the Council meeting held is the 31st October, 1893 ; also on the 9:h November, 1893, at which latter meeting it was formally accepted and his successor appointed.—Yours obediently, J. HARRISON.</p>
        <p>TIP:: RESIGNATION OF ALDERMAN HOLIES.— UNFOUNDED STATEMENTS. Sir,—Having noticed that statements have been cir- culated to the effect that my father resigned his position as an alderman of the borough in consequence of pres- sure having been brought to bear upon him by members of the Liberal Party, I be to say that such statements are absolutely without foundation.—Yours respectfully, C. JACKSON HOLMES. Market Gate, Oct. 15, 1896.</p>
        <p>THE RESIGNATION OF ALDERMAN HOLNIES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Kindly grant me space to deny that the Liberal Party has in any way brought about the retirement of Alderman Holmes from the Town Council. It was the wish of my Executive that he should be allowed to retain his seat as long as it pleased him to do so. Might it not be rightly assumed that, to enable the seat to be appro- priated, the pressure has come from the Party which has broken a rule of its own making, namely the " Preced- ent "of selecting the senior coancillor to the Aldermanic vacancy.—Yours truly, VV. PIEItPOINT, Chairman of Executive, Warrington Liberal Association. Hill Top, Latchford, October 16, 1896.</p>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS-USEFUL INFORNIATION WANTED.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Th' time's fast appro:then wen we'st ha to don aar harmor agean, to feight an win hsck thoose lost provinces as'th enemy stole last November• I want as yo too questions :—Fostly : Is it true as Mester Brews- ter's gooin't penshun off his presunt supporters, ith Taan Caancil, providen they getten thrown sat this next elec- tion. Secondly : Flavin e yew th talent as they'n sent to'th Caancil this last three years, is it treu as awe futur kandedates fort Taan Caancil, has fort bring a surtifikit furt show as they'n pass'd th' forth standard. Answur th' above questians, then ise't know wot fort do, as im a light amhishus mesel. Tha•dtin yo in expectashutn, 1 xemane, sur, yoars respeekfully.</p>
        <p>TONI O'DICKS,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>FIRE EXTINGUISHING ARRANGENIENTS OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE FIRE EXTINGUISHING ARRANGENIENTS OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you please oblige me by inserting few lines which will be of importance to property owners and ratepayers ? The present arrangements for fire ex- tinguishing and flushing purposes are inadequate for present neeAs. The average number of fires annually for the past 20 years ending 1894 has been one. There were two last year and four already this year. In the fire extinguishing department we have four engines, two manual and two steam. Up to last week we had 12 firemen and a superintendent fireman and stoker, making a total of fifteen. Since then six more have been appointed, making a total of 21. In my ,opinion it is useless to appoint more men when there is ,not sufficient water for the large engine in any otreet ,in the borough—it is not men we want it :is water. When the fire took place at Jubilee Buildings the taps in the neighbouring houses only just dribbled, showing that the domestic supply had been seriously in- terfered with. Mr. Bailey, an expert appointed by the Town Council, recommends that the canvas dam (which is very unwieldy) cl hold have side inlets. How is it that this portion of his recommendation has been neglected ? In the future I shall endeavour to suggest plans whereby the present arrangements will be improved.— Yours, &amp;c., WILLIIM DIGGLE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MARRIAGE LAWS.</head>
        <p>THE MARRIAGE LAWS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Sursum Corda seems to look on marriage with the eyes of religious prejudice. Re writes," either Mr. Kenyon is a Christian or he is not a Christian." What has my being a Christian or not being a Christian to do with the laws of marriage. lam thankful to think that many live happily together notwithstanding these laws ; yet others who are very uncomfortable are practically compelled ,o live together unless they can prove certain brms of unfaithfulness which often exist when legal proof cannot be obtained,and thus instead of promoting truthfulness and pure affection the marriage laws foster unfaithfulness and vice. Mrs. Grundy and her followers may not mind this,for they are only led by ap- pearances and disrezard these eAlleArS of tne soul, but men of thought and feeling cannot endure them. They require truthfulness, sincerity, reality. When married persons no longer regard each others welfare they ought not to live together. I should not have replied to Sursum Corda's letter, had I not been requested to do so. It is but little that we can individually do to dis- perse the darkness and misery of human errors. We can only cast forth in faith our little ray of light and pray that God may finish the work sublime.—Yours truly, S. KENYON. Warrington, October 16th, 1896.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the discussion on this question, into which I have no wish to eater further than to point out a few things overlooked by your correspondents, no one has stated distinctly what is the law of England on this sub- ject. Judging from the letters of "Sursum Corda," one might imagine that we were a nation of Jews bound by the Mesalcal law diluted by Christianity. Si, if we tol- lowed his lead we must circumeise our sons and burn our witches. Now the law of England forbids either of these, except the first in case we are Jews, and entirely ignores the authority af Moses in the matter by declaring that marriage must, first of all, be a legal funatien—by registratien—any so called religious ceremony being entirely optional with the contracting parties—to be added or left out at their will. Now, I presume, " Sur- sum Corda " is a good Catholic. So, to him, the Catholic church's marriage ceremonies are imporative but Her Majesty reigns over Mahomodans,flindoos,and Parsees— people of all denominations and of none. Hence regis- tration only is made compulsory. And, if the law can bind, so it may unloose, and this beneficially where no encumbrances exist. Personally, while deprecating too easy divorcement, I do 01 jot to the marriage laws acting as the line on which the Kilkenny Cats were hung. By the way, " Sursum Corda " must be an Irishman— bless him—who else would Hibernicise by sayine, as in his last letter, that when a man stops writing he "takes a stfp in the right direction !'—Yours truly, DAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>REV. EDWARD T. REED AND TEIE TOWN COUNCIL</head>
        <p>REV. EDWARD T. REED AND TEIE TOWN COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Sir,—The good pastor of Emmanuel Church is on the war-path again, denouncing our civic rulers, and calling out loudly for help against the wickedness in high places because—the dominant partner in the municipal business would not elect his pet to the position of alder- man. The weapons of Mr. Reed's warfare have a very carnal ring about them, and were evidently not drawn from the armoury of the man of God thoroughly fur- nished unto all good works. I only know Mr. Roberts by sight, but I have for a long time regarded him as one of the ablest and most useful of our public servants and I feel that in treating him as they have done the Tory majority have cast dishonour on themselves and not on him. Perhaps the real cause of his discomfiture lay in his temperance principles and inability to run after tie° chariot wheels of our local bungs. On a former oceesion when belabouring our naughty Councillore Mr. Reed described himself as "a minister of Christ" thinking, no doubt, that the title would give more force and efficiency to his blows. In the present instance, however, he is content to figure in the hum- bler capacity of "a Christian citizen." But even as "a Christian citizen "Mr. Reed is wholly out of order in railing against the legally constituted authorities of the borough, and when he panned his letter he must have forgotten the admonition of Paul to the Romans " Let every soul be subject unto the 'nigher powers. For there is no power but of God: the pewers that be are ordained of God. Whosoever therefore resisteth the power, re- sisteth the ordinance of God : and they that resist shall • receive to themselves damnation." If the Council Cham- ber of Warrington is the Augean stable which the pastor says it is, it can only be cleansed by replacing its pre- sent occunants with able and righteous men who know nothing of the trickery and j-tibery of party colitics. A splendid opportunity now presents itself to Mr. Reed to ,do something in this direction. Let him offer himself to some of the wardr now seehipg for candidate., and his return at the head of the poll would he assured. And when once in the Council the Augean stable would he cleaned and fumigated and practical Christianity would flow through it like a riven—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>TtIE CHARGE AGAINST MR. ,TORN</head>
        <p>TtIE CHARGE AGAINST MR. ,TORN</p>
        <p>HAMPSON.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER DEFENDANT. At the Warrington Borough Court on Wednesday before Messrs. John Fairclough and John Geddes, Mr. Jenkins, solicitor, informed the bench that he had an application to make in the case of Mr. John Hampson, charged with attempting grievous bodily harm. The prosecutor, he said was out of the Infirm- ary and lying in bed at his'residence:about two min- utes walk from the Court.He will be able to appear next Monday, and was determined to proscute, the woman, his wife, and he, Mr. Jenkins, was in- structed to apply for a summons against her for aid- ing and abetting, and to appear on Monday and answer the charge with Hampson,—The summons was granted.</p>
        <p>CUTLBRY and Blectro-Plated GOODS.—GA.BziND. Bridge-street, WarringtoL—Ativt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>TUBE EXAMINER FRIDAY OCTOBER 23, P.i”G</head>
        <p>TUBE EXAMINER FRIDAY OCTOBER 23, P.i”G</p>
        <p>afraid they were going to be there all afternoon.—Mr. Thornton said it had to do with the case. He had been dragged into that matter by Mr. William Houghton. Mr. Thornton having read a letter containing a refusal to grant a certificate to Mr. Hindley's houses, the Chair- man asked what had that got to do with them.—Mr. Thornton said he was there to give an explanation.—The Chairman said the members wanted to get to the market.—Mr. l'harnten pointed out that in the case he was alluding to there was no drainage and it was a dis- grace to all concerned. In conclusion he charged Mr. Houghton with having made downright falsehoods against him in order to acaomplish his own ends. Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON then moved "That the re- port as moved for on August 19:h setting forth certain work and the cost thereof having been presented to and considered by the Board, the same be approvets.nd nat the waywarden for the township be requested to uncon- ditionally withdraw the charge which he publicly made against the Board in respect of such work at the Parish Council meeting held on September 4th, and also for an apology to the Board for having made the statement containing the charge, and further that the Clerk he directed to write to the Chairman of the Poulton-with- Fearnhead Parish Council informing him of the deoi- sion."—Mr. Thornton : I think it is a most impudent thing to do without attempting to explain y, urself. It only ehows impudence, and want of decency to bring such a thing upon a man. I have made a statement which he cannot controvert successfully.—Mr. Hou,e,hton I think Mr. Tharnton is out of order ?—Mr. Bate said that Mr. Thornton's calculation might be incorrect, and in reply to a question by Mr. Bate, Mr. Thornton ad- mitted that his valuation was made after the work was done.—Mr. Matthews said he thought from their knowledge of the surveyor, trusted as he had been for so long by the Yraywardeas on the Board, that the Board ought not to allow such allegations to go against him on inferior evidence. The evidence of valua- tion was unreliable and the conditions under which it was made were not fair, ;;insomuch as the valuer did not see the work before it was started. They had received the Surveyor's state- ment, and he assured them that was the actual cost,aad he thought they ought to express themselves as being satisfied with it. Mr. Thornton had had the satisfac- tion of giving his explanation, and he had called some very hard names against a gentleman of ability whom they all knew and respected. Had he (Mr. Matthews) been in the chair he would not have allowed it for a moment. The Chairman was a relative of Mr. Houghton, and perhaps he felt that he should allow Mr. Thornton such liberties. In his short experience on the Board he had never heard such impertinent and abominable things la inched against a fellow-member. He thought it would be better to pass a resolution " that the Sur- veyor's report be approved and accepted by the Board as satisfactory," and to allow the matter to drop as far as the Board was concerned. He moved that the resolution be passed.—Mr. Thornton said he certainly should not allow it to drop.—Mr. William Houghton said he would withdraw his resolution and second the one proposed by Mr. Matthews. He would not like the Boaed to think he had been vindictive in the matter. Ha had the great- est admiration for the Surveyor. He had closely ob- served his work for many years, an when he heard such direct charges against Mr. Thompson,hn thought it his duty to bring the matter before the Board. If he had mis-stated Mr. Thornton it had been in his favour. —Mr.Thornton said if Mr. Houghten was going to make a statement he rrust be allowed an explanation.—The Ctkairman :'Order.—Mr.Houghton said Mr.Tbornton used the words he objected to to a large number of persons. —Mr. Thornton said the Surveyor's statement was not satisfactory to him or to the ratepayers of Poulton- with-Fearntrad. He would move an amendment " That the report be referred back to the Surveyor as not being satisfactory so far as the amounts stated are concerned."—No one seconded the amendment, and the original proposition was carried,there being one dissenti- eat—Mr. Thornton. MR. MATTHEWS SAYS FAREWELL.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATTHRWS said he was sorry he had to say fare- well. Ile must say that he had made a lot of friends whilst a member of that Board and he hoped he had been of some assistance. He felt very great regret in leaving but still he was not going out of the neighbour- hood and would have the pleasure of seeing them else- where.—The Chairman saleithe Board was very sorry to lose the servlces of Mr. Mattheos. He had been a most useful member. The Board then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WARRINGTON DISTRICT HIGHWAY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DISTRICT HIGHWAY</p>
        <p>BOA RD.</p>
        <p>LIVELY PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>A general meeting of this Board was held on Wednes- day afternoon in the Board-room, B-3wsey Chambers, Warrington. Mr. J. Houghton occupied the chair and there were also present : Messrs. William Houghton, J.P., H. Thornton, B. B. Matthews, Bate, Lowe, Fair- clough, Clare, and E. A. White (clerk), and Tnomp.on (surveyor). A telegram was read from Captain With- ington, the chairman of the Board, expressing regret at his inability to be present.</p>
        <p>THE ARPLEY NEW ROAD.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATTHEWS asked the Clerk whether the Arpley new road was included in the contract for the mainten- ance of rilain roads for the County Council. Fle thought it ought to be set forth if it was.—The Clerk said it could hardly be considered a main road.—The Surveyor: It would first have to be declared.—Mr. Matthews ex- pressed himself satisfied with the arrangement aud the subject then dropped. THE BOARD'S EXISTENCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a number of reasons, prepared by a committee, which were intended to be submitted to the Lancashire County Council in support of an application that the Board's existence might be continued after Dec. 1897.—Mr. Thornton wished to know whether the initiative in the matter had been taken by the County Council or by the Board.—The Clerk said a letter had been received from the County Council asking the views of the Board,—Mr. Thornton supposed it was likely that the proposal would meet with the approval of the Ceunty Council.—The Clerk : Yes, but they want our views.—Mr. Matthews thought the condition of the Board's roads, when compared with those of urban auth- orities, was hardly put strong enough in the communica- tion to the County Council.—The Clerk said it was rather difficult to speak of the condition of their own roads. They, of course, sp )Ite. for themselves.—Ar. Matthews said the roads would not speak for themselves to the Local Government Board.—The Clerk said the communi- cation was only for the County Council, and that author- ity would afterwards give their reasons for the continu- ance of the Highway Boards to the Local Government Beard.—Mr. Matthews thought it was probable that the County Council would adopt the reasons, and they would go before the Local Government Board with a few remarks from the County Council. He therefore thought something stronoer should be stated with regard to the condition of the Board's roads. He could understand it being left out if they had bad roads.—Mr. Thornton thought something might be said about the roads.—The Chairman thought the Board could reasonably claim to have the best roads in the kingdom.—lt was ultimately decidcd to add to the communication that the Boards roads were not inferior to any to be found in the kiag- dorn.—Mr.Matthews said he believed Lancashire was the only county applying for an extension of that Order to the Local Government Board.—The Board approved of the reasons agreed upon by the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>FINANCM.</head>
        <p>FINANCM.</p>
        <p>The financial statement ',flowed that the balance in the Treasurer's hands waA 153 Ta. The Surveyor aqked that two cheques, one for £5O and another for £l2O should be sign-P for the payment of wages.—This was agreed to on the proposition of the Chairman. THE IMPROVEMEN rs To A DRAIN AT PADGATE.— A WARM DISCUSSION.—A MEMBER ASKED TO APOLOGISB.</p>
        <p>The CLERK slid the special business to come before the Board was to receive and consider the statement re the drain near Padgate Stocks, prepared by the Sur- veyor in pursuance of a resolution of the Board passed at the meeting held on the 19th August,anci to pass such re.olutions thereon as might be thought advisable.— The report of the Surveyor alluded to stated that in December of last year it was necessary to have the ditch on the north side of Tidy-lane and of the field footpath leading from Tidy-lane to Padgate Stocks thoroughly cleaned out, in order to liberate the main road drainage. The cost of cleaning the ditch was £1 is. 4d., end that amount was repayab'e by Mr. William Bennett, Mr. W. J. Hatton, and Mr. Cox in the sums of 9s. 11d., 93. 11d., and la. 61., respectively. The amount chareeable to Mr. W. Bennett was paid on 28-h February, 189 g. The other two amounts were still outstanding, but would be paid to the Board shortly.—The Surveyor afterwards stated that the amounts had now been paid.—Mr. Houghton said as he was the mover of the resolution that the Surveyor should furnish the report, a statement would be expected from him. He was sorry to say that this business, which he regarded as of a disagreeable character, started so early as June last, and it arose out of something a waywarden said at a Parish Council meeting at Poulton-with-Fearnhead. Mr. Thornton was instructed to move that the question of re- pair and work done by the roadmen both on and off the high road during the past year should be investigated and particulars as to what the construction of the drain at Padgate cost and the payments for the same be given. He (Mr. Hough- ton) did not thoroughly understand the resolution, and the Chairman should have ruled it out of order. But as nr. Thornton was chairman he had things all his own way. The statements made at the meeting were mostly what he (Mr. Houghton) objected to. Mr. Thornton had stated that the Highway Board performed its work in an underhand manner and the Parish Council had winked at this work. He also made statements of a similar as- persive character. It was pointed out that there was no one but Mr. Thornton who could investigate the matter, which could be one by going to the Clerk of the Board or Surveyor and making inquiries, he being the wo,y- warden for that distriet. At the meeting of the Board in &lt;rally he (Mr. Houghton) expected Mr. Thornton would bring the matter forward, but he was quite silent and no information was given. As Mr. Thornton did not bring the matter forward, he (Mr. Floughton) had asked for the Surveyor's statement respecting ths matter. There was another statement made by Mr. Thornton at the Parish Council meeting to the effect that he had had the ditch measured up he a competent man, who stated that the cost had been £ll, and not £7 lle. as stated by the Surveyor. These were matters which he (Mr. Houghton) objected to, and as he was the only one con- nected with the Highway Board who heard the remarks, be thought it was his duty to bring the subject for- ward before the Board. It was said in the presence Of thirty or forty ratepaver4, • fie wouq a.k Mr. Tnorn- ton to honourably withdraw his words and apllogtse to I the Board. He had a resolution to submit to the Board, but he would wait until Mr. Thornton had given his ex- planation.—Mr. Thornton thought if he had been al- lowed to make a statement at the last Board meeting after Mr. Houghton had spoken it would have saved the present discussion. But he was then ruled out of order by the Chairman. The mistake was to let Mr. Floughton say what he did which was a serious charge against him (Mr. Thornton) and was also untrue.—Mr. Matthews thought they ought not to discuss the ruling of. their Chairman.—Mr. Thornton said he believed he was at . liberty to make that statement. With regard to Mr. , Houghton's statements he must simply say that he did not utter them. It it was to be found in the report of the meeting he would give way, but if it was not he would take it that the Beard considered the statements made to be untrue.—The Chairman : We don't expect reporters to put everything in the papers that is said.— Mr. Thornton said the statement was net true, and his , word was as good as that of Mr. William Houghton's. He' thought the report published in the newspapee should be I accepted for or aeeinet either of them.—The Chairm an : All right.—Mr. Thornton then proceeded to road a news- paper report of his speech at the Parish Council meeting which did not contain the statements Alluded to. Mr. Thornton remarked that what was published Was all he said. No such words as Mr. Houghton had stated were used, and that gentleman was wrong. With regard to the cost of the work and the payment of it, ratepayers had come to him.—Mr. Matthews : Can you give their names ?—Mr. Thornton : I decline to do so. I sunpes you think lam the only one that has iaieed any objection ? There WAS groat dissatisfaction in the district about the matter,any one who lived there must be aware of it. Be did not wish to come there i with his fingers n his mouth so he asked a. question about it at the Parish Council. He was instructed to see Mr. Thompsoe in company with Mr. Ruyston,but he had not seen Mr. Royston at the meeting for three months successively so that he (Mr. Thornton) hail not been in a position to state his complaint. Mr. %Vattern Hough- ton had prematurely rushed the matter upon the High- way Board and had made the statements which were untrue. It placed him (Mr. 'Thornton) in a very uefair and wrong position. Had he heelpatient enough until he (Mr. Thornton) had seen Mr. Royston he would not have inflicted the matter on the Highway 80-ad, and lie must be responsible for any onus or dainaee which might come out of it. No one had a higher opinion of Mr. Thompson than himself, but if anything sprune up, he (Mr. Thornton) was not the man to run behind the scenes. Mr. Thornton then proceeded to refer to the Surveyor's statement of the cost of the ditching. He (Mr. Thornton) contended that the length wee 305 yd-e, and this the Surveyor stated had been done for El 1441. He (Mr. Thornton) had had a competent in an to e8..1. mate the cost and he gave it as his eninion that the work could not be done for less than £2 sa. 91.—The Sur- veyor pointed out that in some places very little required doing.—Mr. Matthews said the Board did not want to hear of valuations. The Surveyor had tele them the actual cost and that was soffiniente—Mr. Thornton : Will you be quiet ? Proceeding, Mr. There- ton referred to the cost of the pipes whieh he contended would bring the to AI cost to 211 10e 31., or about LI more than the Surveyor stated. He asserted that one party had paid his share of the cost, but another had not. He would ask the Clerk whether the Board was called upon to pipe the ditch.—The Clerk said there was no doubt that they had a perfect right to do so if they thought it desirable.—Mr. 'Thornton : Who wriuei be at the expense of the piping ?—The Clerk :Tie Board ha,e a right to pipe it at their own expense hut if the owuer nfT,red to pay the B )artl would no doubt accept it. --el r. Thornton : One owner has pail and :tnotlier ii as net. There is partiality or something of that ICtitt. Mr. Thornton was then proceeding to refer to WilAl7 he regarded as unfair treatment of a Mr. Hirelley at Pd- gate, when Mr. Matthews asked the Otia.irmari whether they ought not to confine themselves to tee resolution before the Board—the Chairman said the Board could net enter into any other people's business. He was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>RUNAWAY HORSE IN BRIDGE-STREET.</p>
        <p>Abodi-6:i75-on- Tuesday- evening John Arnold, in the employ of Mr. Isherwood, contractor, Tanners'- lane, was engaged at the Firth Mattress Works, Friars'-green, leaving his horse and lurry in the street unattended. While he was in the office the horse, from some cause at presant unexplained, took fright, and b3lted off into Bridge-street,where it and the lurry came into collision with a carriage belonging to Mr. E. Woods, solicitor, smashed the front part of the vehicle, and knocked off one of the hind wheels. The coachman. Jackson Bright (who was the only occupant), jumped into the street; and escaped unhurt. Neither of the animals was injured, nor any of the passengers who were in the street at the time.</p>
        <p>RAI LWA Y SERVANTS' MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon the local annual gathering of railway servants in aid of the widows' and or- phans' fund of the Amalgamated Society of Rail- way Servants took place. The men, to the number of about 100 assembled in front of the Town Hall at two o'clock, and shortly afterwards, headed by the Post Office Rind, marched in processional order through the following streets to the Friars'-green Chapel, where a special service was held :—Legh- Edgeworth-st., Bewsey-st., florsemarket-st, Buttermarket-et., Mersey-st., Bridge-st., and Ry- lands-st. The use of the chapel had been placed at the disposal of the committee by the trustees. An appropriate and excellent sermon was preached by Mr.Vlreneh,of Northwich.lt was listened to with much attention. The collection, which will be devoted to the widows' and orphans' fund, realiz3d 15q.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LATCHFORD GOOD TRMPLARS.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD GOOD TRMPLARS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Bonehill Branch Lodge of ths Independent Order of Good Templars was held in the Temperance Hall on Monday evening, Bro. A. Hampton, P.C.T.,presiding. There was a fair attend- ance of members. Communications were read re— ferring to the expected visit to Warrington of Bros. Councillor J. Malins, J.P.. Grand Chief Templar of England, and the Rev. J. A. Lee, grand chaplain Bro. A. Ford gave a stirring address, and a Lancashire recital, entitled " Bonny Brid." Bro. J. R C)rbett, contributed a brief memoir of the life of John anderton, of Preston, or, the early days of temperance. The Chairman read a newspaper extract with reference to the newly formed Sunday night Men's Church Club, under the leadership of Prebendary Denison, vicar of St. Michael's, North Kensington, for intoxicating drink, billiards and It stated that the Bishop of Chester had full sympathy with the scheme, and the curate re- sided on the premises. A resolution was passed disapproving of such a club connected with a Chris- tian Church. Several further items of interest were given by Bros. F. Blease,F A. Jenkins,H.Hampton, and W. Sullivan. The meeting closed with prayer. RANGlii.—See the Herald Improved Lifting Fire Rtngls.—Spie Agent, GICV.LTPD, Bridge-etreet.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>TV ARRINGTON DISTRICT ORDRR OF DRUIDS.</head>
        <p>TV ARRINGTON DISTRICT ORDRR OF DRUIDS.</p>
        <p>The—r—e-p—r-es-e-a-t-atives of the various lodges of the above district held their annual meeting in their hall, Sankey-st., on Saturday, under the presidency of District Grand Master J. Massey. The accounts of each fund for the past quarter were submitted by D.C.S. T. Roberts, and showed a satisfactory in- crese, likewise the list of new members, which added considerably to the numerical strength of the district. Toe Grand Master presented P.D.G.M. S. Dunbohin with a beautiful framed emblem of merit, as a token of the esteem in which he is held for his valuable services to the district. Bro. Dun- bebin had f)r four years held the position of Grand Master, and many years ago had also held the same position with cr4lit to himself and the members of the order. He wished him health to continue his good services. P.D.G.M. William Bibby also spoke of the eminent services which had been rendered in every branch of the order, and remarked that a more zealous and painstaking and kindly official the district had not had. P.D.G.M. D. Houghton also joined the previous speakers in wishing Bro. Danoohin long life. D.C.S. T. Roberts bore high testimony to Bro. Dunbobin's services. Bro. Dun- bohin on rising to acknowledge the presentation was received with applause. He thanked the brethren for the kind manner in which they had spoken ot his services And for the beautiful em- blem, and feelingly risured them that lie should value it as lonz as he lived, and that, nothing could here given him greater joy to accept from them. lie said though he had been in office for nearly 50 years he always felt he was only doing his duty, , trying to do some little to advance the interests of his order and his flow men. He asked them to forgive his savino, more, as their kindness had really overcome him, but he again and again thanked them sincerelv ani hoped to be spared to do useful work or the district for many years. (Applause .)</p>
        <p>By the bursting of a vessel containing acetylene gas .t the l'ictel Works,l36, Rue Chamoionnet, Montmartre, P triA, on Saturiay afternoon, two men were blown to pieo!,s, the building in which they were at work was wrecked, and ill the other buildings connected with the works greatly damaged, not a pane of glass bein g left ia a broken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EAULESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAULESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WELSH WESLEYANS.</p>
        <p>In! aid of the funds of the above denomination a lecture was given in the Lecture Hall of the Wes- leyan Chapel on Thursday evening by the Rev. Gwynfryn Jones, of Ashton. Mr. Th owes Davies, of Liverpool, presided,and there was a large attend- ance. "the lecture which was of an exceedingly interesting character was entitled " The pleasures of life." The rev, gentleman treated his subject in a masterly manner, dividing his plwas ures into health, rest, work, love, marriage and religion.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT SUNDAY AFTERNOON.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Earlestown Mission and in aid of the funds, the second of the eeries of P.S.A. was held in the Co-operative Hall. The pro- gramme was carried out by the Hope of arlestown lodge of Good Templars, Bro. W. H. Dale, presiding. The speaker for the afternoon was Bro. Councillor J. S. Vorley of Leic,ster and solos were sung by Miss Higgins and Miss F. Holliday. In the evening a temperance ;sermon was preached by Council- lor Vorley and a duet was given by Misses M. and A. Jackson. EARLESTOWN W ESLEYA N SIINDA.Y SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Sunday afternoon was set apart by the Wesleyan Conference for a special effort on behalf of the chil- dren connected with the Sunday school. Prayer meetings were held during the week, on Wednes- day and Saturday, and on Sunday morning at 830, the teachers and officers being present. In the afternoon a meeting was held in the upper school- room for the senior scholars, when Mr. J. H. Profit presided. Addresses were given by the Revs. J. T. Wainnaan and J. Bartley. In the junior school, Mr. W. Clare had charge, and addresses were given by Messrs. G. Bunting and G. E. Warbuiton. 1.0 G.T. EA.RLESTOWN EBENEZER LODGE. rhis lodge held its usual weekly session on rues- day last, Bro. J. Taylor, C.T. presiding over a good attendance of members and friends. One candi- date was admitted to membership. The Secretary read an announcement of a great public temperance meeting to be held on Saturday, October 24th, at Warrington, when Councillor Joseph Malins, 1.P., and Grand Chief Templar of England, will address the meeting. The programme for the evening was "conundrums",and was in the hands of Sister Owens, who provided a very amusing entertainment. Bro. Quartermaster-Sergeant Roberts,of Warrington,also gave a very able address. A vote of thanks to Sister Owens closed a very pleasant evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>BIRTHDAY PARTY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>BIRTHDAY PARTY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the teachers, and choir of St. John's Church and Sunday School, there is what is termed a birthday society,which is managed for the social benefit of the members. It appears that the members whose birthday falls upon a particular month, are expected to organise a party for the benefit of the other members. On Tuesday evening it fell to the lot of Mr. J. Clarke to arrange the tea to celebrate his birthday. The party was held in the mission hall, there being about 50 pr?,ient. The tea was very nicely served, and much enjoyed. After the tables bad been cleared the evening was spent in a social manner, games Sze., being indulged in. During an interval refreshments were handed round, and Mr. J. Iluddleston gave a couple of humourous songs. The gathering, which was an enjoyable one, broke up about 11 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>NEWTON NURSERY BOWLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>NEWTON NURSERY BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The above club brought their season to a close on Saturday afternoon, by the usual members'handicap On the completion of play a large number of mem- bers and friends assembled at the White Lion Inn, where a substantial knife and fork tea was pro- vided. The meal was very much enjoyed, and on the removal of the clot h,Councillor J. Simpson pre- sided and distributed the prizes which had been won in the handicap. The first priz a china tea service was taken by P. Clarke ; 2nd prize, carved oak timepiece, Mr. J. Chapman ; 3rd prize, copper kettle, Mr. W. Kent,prizes of less value were given, each competitor securing one. Various toasts were proposed and duly honoured, including "Nursery Bowling Club," "The Donors of prizes," "The Committee" &amp;c. The meeting was of a ery pleasant character songs &amp;c.',being given by Messrs. J. J. Randall, J. Braddock, G. McCormack, W. Kent, A. Boardman, J. McDonald, J. Appleton, and whist- ling solos by Mr. J. Howard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>Instead of the ordinary business meeting the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Template on Monday night had a special meeting for children in the Co-eperative Hall, to which the parents of the youngsters and friends were invited. Bro. Councillor J. S. Vorley, Ci.S.J.T., presided, and gave an earnest and interesting address on temperance matters. Bro. A. Newell, ).Co., of Liverpool, deli- vered his model lecture on the " Four barred gate," which was attentively listened to. Several songs, recitations, &amp;c., were given by the members, and the meeting was altogether of a very pleasant character. At the close of this meeting and after the departure of the children, an open meeting was held for the adults, over which Bro. Newman pre- sided, the subject of the gathering being " Table talks with members and friends." Addressee were given by Bro.. J. S. Vorley,James Woods,aud Jona Tayler, and solos were sung by Miss A. Negman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONA.RY SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONA.RY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The annual special services, in aid of the Wes- leyan Foreign Missionary Society were held on Sunday in the Wesleyan Chapel. Sermons were preached morning and evening by the Rev. J. T. Weinman, missionary at the Transvaal, who gave very ite,eresting discourses upon his work in South Africa. Special anthems were sung by the choir, solos being given by Miss Olive Clare. On Monday evening, the annual meeting was held in the same Place when there was a large attendance of the Public. Mr. J. Cook, of St. Helens presided, and he was accompanied on the platform by Rev. J.Arrow- smith of St. Helens, and the Rev. J. T. Wainman (Missionary) and Rev. J. Bartley. After a brief in- troductory address b 3. the Chairman, the Rev. J. Bartley read the annual report of the society, and said that Earlestown had contributed £4l 41. to- wards missionary purposes, the total amount col- lected by the Juvenile Association being ..£7O 138.11 d. An address was given by the Rev. J. Arrowsmith,and an interresting account of mission- ary :work in South Africa was given by the Rev. J. P. Wainman,in which he said 45,000 black people had been converted. A collection was made at the close on behalf of the work, and a vote of thanks was accorded the chairman and speakers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLAR ANNIVERSARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The tio—p-e-of liarlestownlodge of Good Templars celebrate,l their 24th Anniversary in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Saturday. At five o'clock a public tea meeting was held, when there was a moderate attendance. Mr. B. J. George of Ilaydock after- wards presided ever a public meeting in the large hall ,which was filled. The principal speaker was Bro. Rev. J. A. Lee. G. chap. of Liverpool who re- marked that he was very pleased with the good work the lodge had accomplished in the past,aud he trusted the future would be as successful. The Good Templars' work was one for which they asked God's blessing, and it was -impossible to raise the masses of our large towns and cities without a total abstinance from intoxicants, It was the dnty of every individual to see after this matter to do his utmost by precept, and example to bring it about. DrunkeLness was physically as well as morally a disease. They must be clean if they were to be successful. There was a necessity for abstinence it only for the sake of setting the young an example. A varied programme of vocal and instrumental music, &amp;c, was submittdd by Messers Willis, A. Banton, W. Nurse,( Warrington ) G. Clarke, Misses A. Newman, L. Barr M. A. Jackson, and H. Warburton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>The Samuel Morley Section of Cadets met on Wednesday evening, in the Co-operative Hall, at 7 o'clock. The worthy patron presided. After open- ing, a spelling contest for boys was held which re- sulted in Arthur Smith being the victor. An enter- tainment took place at 7.30 in the same room, Bro: James Marsh presiding. The following took part —Bro. T. Wheatly, recitation, " Mind your ,wn business" ; Mr, W. Wilson, "Peggy and Joan "; Bro. W. Clare, song, " Anchor's weighed" Address by Bro. W. Pearson who spoke of the evils of drunken- ness and the danger to young men of societies which met in public houses and safety of the sons of temperance by not meeting in such places. He also spoke of the benefits to be derived from the society. itlro. 0. Pickett, reading " Jone OTee's Courtship " ; Bro. J. Clark, song, " Water Bright Bro. G. Warburton, recitation, " Free Seat " ; Bro. NI. P. Clare, song "Good ;Company. Miss Higgins accompanieel on the piano, which was kindly lent for the occasion by the Hope of Earlestown Lodge, P.O.G.T. The adult members afterwards met. Bro R. Robinson presided. After opening, matters of im- portance to the society were brought. forward by Bro. Pearson and liscussed. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Good Templars for use of piano for entertainment.</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAX AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>BY IN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDE NT.</p>
        <p>The disease of scarlet fever, which I commended upon a few weeks ago, has hl m ost reached the epi• demical stage, there having been altogether some- thing like 65 cases,the majority of which have been removed to the fever hospital at Warrington. I am not in a position to say how the outbreak has arisen, but I am afraid our sanitary arrangements are not quite so perfect as they might be. Of course the question of disinfection in these cases is an important one, and sometimes it is difficult to get hold of an efficient disinfectant. The difficulty may, however, be very easily got over, for I understand that disinfectants may be obtained by any householder within the district free of charge, upon application to the Inspector of Nuisances, at the Town Hall, Ternworth-street entrance, between 9 and 10 a.m., and 5 and 5.30 p.m., and also upon application to the caretaker, Town Hall, Newton, at any reasonable hour.</p>
        <p>The lighting question proceeds apace, and very soon we shall have one of the best illuminated towns in Laucashire. I am glad to hear that my appeal to the Council for an improvement in the lighting at Haydock Crossing has borne fruit and that some alteration will be made there. This is a very awkward place, and the committee will need to be very careful in choosing a proper site for the light.</p>
        <p>The fatality which happened at blessrs. Vicar's works the other week, when a boy lost his life whilst larking with another boy, should come as a serious warning. In all large works this larking is freely indulged in, and missiles of every kind are thrown. It will now be seen what danger lies in throwing at each other even in play, and perhos the misfortune will have a salutary effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>BUTCHER.</p>
        <p>NO CASE PROVED. THE NEED OF A PUBLIC ABBATOIR.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning At the Warrington Enough Police Court before the Mayor ( n the chair), Alderman Harrison, Mr. J. W. Hay vtard.and Mr. S. Wright, Harry Mather, butcher, 75 Church-street,was charged with ex- posing for sale in his shop a steep's head, lungs, and liver, which were unfit for food ; he was further charged with being the owner of a carcase of sheep,unfit for food, in a slaughter-house in Williamson-square.—George Blackstock, shepherd,was charged with aiding and abet- ting in the latter offence.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Browne appeared for the defendants who pleaded not guilty. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) prosecuted, and said that on the 9.h inst. Inspector Norman visited the slaughter-house of Mr. Bowers, Williamson-square; and found the carcase of a sheep hanging there, which was unfit for food. From information he re- ceived he went to Mather's shop where he learnt it was one of a consignment of 200 from Scotland to George Blackstock. The carcase was certified to be unfit for food and was destroyed on the authority of Mr. Hayward. He (the Town Clerk) added that the sheep had been crushed to death in the railway truck and taken to Bowers' slaughter-house and dressed for sale. Cases of this sort pointed out the absolute necessity of a public abbatoir where all animals would be destroyed and passed by a certificated Inspector. Mr;Brewne denied that the animal had been crushed to death.</p>
        <p>Inspector Norman said that on the day in question, about half-past five in the evening, he visited Bowers's slaughter-house in Church-street, and there saw hanging the carcase of a sheep, dressed in the usual way for sale. The shoulders of the animal were pinned, and on exam- ining it he found on the right side that it was badly crushed, trodden or worried very badly, and, of course, he came to the conclusion that it was unfit for food. He went to Mather's shop, 75, Church-street, which was about a hundred yards from the slaughter-house. Wit- ness told defendanehe had been in the slaugliter-house and seized the carcase as being unfit for human food. He charged him with being the owner of it, to which he replied." Yes, it's mine." He asked Lim where he got it from, and he said" From 0:d George, the shepherd.' Mather also produced the pluck from others in the shop. He then informed defendant that he would report the case to the Medical Officer of Health, and ottain an order for the animal's destruction. Defendant followed wit- ness out of the shop and shouted him back, and asked him if be thought he would be summoned,that he would sooner give £lO than be summoned. It would mean simply ruining him and closicg his shop. Defendant called him back three times. In the evening both defen- dants called at his house and begged hard of him to over- look the case.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browne : He did not know six butchers slaughtered at the slaughter-house. Bowers was res- ponsible for it. It was not locked, and he believed it never was locked. The sheep was discoloured down one side, and he did not think anyone would have bought it for food.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gornall, medical officer of health for the borough, said that on the morning of the 10th of October, Satur- day morning, about Hine o'clock, he examined the car- case of this sheep. He made a thorough examination, and he certified it as unfit for human food. Its condition was that the muscular portion was a very dark claret colour, and it was flabby and damp, and on making an incision into the muscular tissue there was an effusion of blood into the joints, and in his opinion the animal had not been killed in the ordinary way. On the right side there were bruises. He thought it possible for the meat to be bought by undiseerning people in the even- ing. The animal might have been suffocated or trampled to death.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said the case was one exactly similar to a ease which happened a few weeks age, but which was not brought before the court but as settled by the committee, and he said it was very unfair of the Cor- poration to make fish of one and flesh of another. His defence was that Blackstock was a tenter of sheep for Mr. Johnson, of Dumfries, and on Thursday night, the Bth inst., there arrived a consignment of 200 animals from Leckarbie at the Central Station, addreised to the order of Blaekstock. The sheep in question was badly crushed. In every consignment some unfortunate cheep was crushed or trampled down on the journey. The one hundred and ninety-nine were taken out of the trucks and taken to a field. The following morning he found the sheep in question walking about and gave it some food. It was taken to the slaugh- ter-house in Church-street, where six different butchers slaughtered. There he met Mather who saw the animal alive. Before any butcher or any doctor could tell whether the sheep was unfit for feed they had to dress it and take the skin off, and B ackstock's in- structions were that Mather should get it dressed, so that he could send the skin to his master. Mather dressed it in the presence of two witness as in the usual way. As several butchers slaugh- tered there each took his own pluck away and Mather took his to his shop. It was not for sale and was refused to a woman who wanted to buy it. The sheep was dressed about half-past one, and was waiting for Mr. Biackstock to come back from Lymm. Before he came back someone informed Mr. Norman of the cent- rence. The animal was waiting for Mr. Blacksto3k's inspection. It was never meant to be sold for human food, but was kept simply for the inspection of the man Blackstock. _ .</p>
        <p>Joseph Percival, assistant to Mr. Bowers, said he had dressed hundreds of sheep, and he was in the slaughter- house on the dav in question. He saw the sheen walk- ing about and he saw it dressed. It wag badly crushed on one side. The animal was bled in the ordinary way. Abou- six or seven butchers killed in the slaughter- house. It was the custom for each butcher to take away the offal, as otherwise it would get mixed up. The de-, fondant told him the sheep belonged to " Old George.'</p>
        <p>By the Town Clerk : He had not heard the sheep was carried to the slaughter-house. It wss killed whilst lie was there. The flesh was bruised on one side. It was the rule to send fox Inspector Norman when there was a doubt about the condition of an animal. He supposed Mr. Norman was not sent for in this case because the sheep was not intended for food. Martha Leach, 7 Bowles-lane, deposed to visiting Mather's shop about five p.m. on Friday evening and asking for the pluck. She was told it was not for sale. Thomas Clare' assistant to Mather. said he saw the sheep alive in defendant's shop. It was taken to Bowers' slaughter-house. It was not lame when he saw it.—By the Town Clerk: It was brought in a cart and carried into the shop. George Blackstock deposed to receiving a large num- ber of sheep at the station. The aniinal in question had been trampled. He took it to the defendant's and left it in the shop. It was not sold to him.—By the Town Clerk: The animal was only trampled ; that was all that was the matter with it. It could walk on the Friday morning. _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Barry Mather said the animal was alive when it was brought to his shop. Re was asked to dress it, and he did so. It was discoloured. One of the terms of the tenancy was that all the offal had to be taken away im- mediately. Mrs. Leech asked for the pluck, but he refused to let her have it.</p>
        <p>By the Town Clerk: They could not have skewered the animal after it had gone cold, as it would b? stilt The boy had an opportunity of selling the pluck during the afternoon. He carried the sheep because ho thought it might rue away. The Mayor said they must dismiss the case though they thought the Inspector had done quite right in bringing it before them.</p>
        <p>A Bombay telegram reports that during Friday night a bucket of tar was poured over the statue of the Qieen there, the senseless perpetrttor of the outrage adding to his folly by tying a pair of old sandals round the neck of the statue.</p>
        <p>_ It is stated that the ez%r'a visit to France has cost the French Government £280,000.</p>
        <p>EXTENSIVE THEFTS Fll,Oll THE - MARKET. A CART-LOAD OF GOODS RECOVERED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court,on Thursday. before Mr. S. Chandley (chairman), Alderman Sutton, and Lieut.-Colonel Ridgway; Catherine Bolton, widow. 10g Bostock-st., Warrington, was charged on remand with stealing from a stall in the Markdt-place at 4.37 p.m. on Wednesday, the 14th inst., a ladies' cape value 14s. 6d., the property of Rachael Tasker,draper,s, Rowe- st. ; she was further charged with having on the 2nd inst. and other dates in the last two months stolen a lady's fur boa, a boy's overcoat, three pairs of b ots, a piece of satin, and similar articles, valued at £3 105., the property of Thomas Halliwell, draper, 33 Long-st.. Middleton; further charged with stealing from the stall of Edward Leigh, draper, 14 School Brow, on the 14th October and other dates during the past three months, a lady's singlet, a quantity of dress stuffs, flannel and similar articles, valued in all at £2 10s.—Defendant ad- mitted all the offences.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (Mr. L. Talbot) informed the bench that defendant appeared after a week's remand oh thtee charges of having committed robberies from stalls in the market. This was not one of the ordinary cases in which a woman had succumbed to sudden temptatiou. for the inquiries of the police had disclosed a system of robbery carried on by the defendant for at least 12- months. He related the circumstances of defendant's arrest on the first charge, as reported by us last week. Briefly, Mary Fisher, who was in charge of Taeker's stall had met defendant in possession of a lady's cape which, was like one she was offering for sale on her stall. She discovered it was missing and caught defendant, giving her in charge of Sergeant Monaghan, who appeared on the scene. Defendant's house,added the Chief Constable.. was searched and almost a cart-load of goods was found in the place. Altogether articles to the value of almost £lO had been identified by various stall-holders in the market, as having been stolen from time to time. There was also a large quantity of similar goods, all new,found in defendant's house, which owing to their common na- ture could not be sworn to although many people were morally certain they were theirs. In the three charges before the bench the goods had been positively identified. A pitiable feature of the case was that the woman had been seen from time to time accompanied by either her daughter aged 16 or her boy aged 12, and the police bad found out that those children had been used as mediums to take away the stolen goods. Defendant had thus not only carried on a systematic robbery herself but had demoralised her own children. In her favour he wished to say that when she was apprehended defendant made an open confession to the police and materially assisted them in getting the goods properly classified. No evidence was called, as defendant admitted the' thefts,though the Chief Constable mentioned that there- was ample evidence to prove all the charges. In answer to queries from the Bench, the Chief Con- stable said they could have taken a dozen charges. He did not know what defendant intended to do with the geods. They had not found that she had disposed of any. She seemed to have been using them for her own and children's purposes. He would like to say that there was a feeling amoog the stall-holders that tbp Bench thought they were careless in the matter. They had to take the stalls as they found them and if they were not safe-guarded it was not the stall-holders' fault.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chandley : I don't see that there is any blame attached to Ulf+ stall-holders.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sutton: I have sometimes seen what has ap- peared to be carelessness on the part of the stallholders in the matter.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said he was reminded that defen- dant had no occasion to commit these thefts from want as she had two sons in good situations and worked as a tailoress herself. The sentences passed recently did not seem to have had any effect at all. One of the prose— cutors in this case had a robbery from her stall after the defendant was arrested in the market last Wednesday. il-tendant pleaded for mercy as it was "her first offence."</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that with every d;sposition to be lenient they did not see a single extenuating circum- stance that would cause then) to give defendant a small punishment. The offences were barefaced robberies which could only proceed from a desire tn stcal. Defend- ant would have to go to gaol for three months' hard labour, one month on each charge. They hoped the sentence would have a deterrent effect and protect the prnperty of the stall-holders.</p>
        <p>Defendant was led from the court crying loudly.</p>
      </div>
