        <p>MUNICIPAL BLE ,TION, 1896. TO THE BURGESSES OF ORFORD WARD. LADTBS ,AND GENTLEMEN.- Having received a hearty and unanimous invitation from a large representative body of the TtetPpayors, to offer myself as' s Candidate to represent you in the Town Council, I have great pleasure in consenting to place my services at your disposal. I have alreadyfroceived promises of support from'influential andhighly respected members of both political parties, which Is especially gratifying. because I am most anal us to do my utmost for the benefit of the working classes, irrespective of Party. Hating the time at my disposal,'! shall, if elected, make it my special duty to find out wh is required to improve the condition bf the inhabitants of Orford Ward, and If there is anytkrhag-walch demands Immeidate attention you may depend.upon my using every effort to corry what I may con- sider tobe for the beat interests otthe Ward. Itis pay intention to personally wait upon every' voter during the election, and I shall esteem it a favour if you will kiodly reserve your votes until I have had the pleasure of galling upoa you. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant. HENRY THORNTON. '" The'Laurels," Pearnhead, Oct. 3rd, 1896.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. WIJNICIPAL ELECTION, NOV 2ND, 189 G. 01 TO THE ELECTORS OF LATCHFORD WARD. 'LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, thaws much pleasure in complying with the numerous 'requests which I have received during the past few weeks and ' beg tooffer myself as a Candidate for your suffrages at the forthcoming Municipal Election. Au you are aware, I have a very substantial interest in the Ward, both in regard to my business and as a property owner andadaving lalso resided for a quarter of a century amongst you, I claim to be thoroughly acquainted with your needs and the spxial requirements of the district. *Seeing how rapidly all vacant land is being acquired for 'building purpeees, I would stronglycupport any scheme which •would provide open spaces and play grounds for children in every Ward of the town. In-regard to the repayment of the cost of street making, which bears so heavily upon property owners, and, therefore, also ..upon tenants, I am of opinion that the system of deferred payments might be at once extended to a period of sevenor ten years. I am glad to 'see that the opposition to the provision of wharfage accommodation on the river has becit overcome, and I shall support a still farther extensio 1 thereof, so that every assistance may be given to manufacturers and others who desire t, take full advantage of the ,great natural facilities which the Mersey afiords. I think it is quite time that we were supplied with better and eheaper gas and that eie trio lighting should be intro- ,diseed‘ without delay. If you'd° me the honour to elect 1119 YOU will find in., as .your friend and neighbour, always ready to investigate any weß•founded,complaint and to do my best to remedy it; and atthe same time most willing to assist in promoting any soliesne for the improvement of the town and for the redae- ,tion.cf our expenditure. I am, Ladies &amp; Gentlemen. Tour obedient escant, JAMES STRANGE. BOUGH OF WARRINGTON. AIIUNICIPAL ELECTION, NOV. 2ND. 189 G. in. TO THE ELECTORS OF ST. AUSTIN'S WARD. LAMBS AND GELTLEMEN,— I have carefully considered-ethe proposals of 'the Manchester Cerporation with regard to the scheme for the disposal ot their sewage, and sin strongly of opinion thalathey are. as at present disclosed, full of possibilities of the graeest danger to theinterests o' our lawn, both from a sanitary as well as a commercial Mandpoint. It is of ,paramount importune the nothing should be permitted ondhe parte the Manchester Corporation, which amyl* the-slightest degree either prejudice or !impair the navigation of the Mersey at Wareington. or intensify the present veryiunentisfactory conditions of the water sin regard to ita alike upon the public health. It was not rnylatention to again reeame public work, but In the hope of being of some assistance in strengthening the Volley of thaDorporation at this, to my mind, most serious crisis in our town's history, have consented to be nominated for the ensuing Municipal Eiettion. I hope that the manner in wbich 1 pertormed my dirties during the many years I formerly had the honour of repre- senting you, will justify you in again entrusting me with your can ddenc.e I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Tours faittfully, JuLOT CROSFIELD. Walton Lea, Oct. 13th, 1896,</p>
