        <p>POROtiGli OF WARRINGTON. MU N TOI CTI HP EA LB uRFG, VisCHTsIOONF. STN NOV. JOHN'S ls, WARD. 1896 . LADIES AND GENILEMEN,— A large and representative number of the Ratepayers of this Ward are anxious that I shmld represent them on the Town Council, and I have at their earnest request decided to contest the Ward and now seek your support. During the nine years I was in the Town Council I endeavoured to serve you to the best of my ability. I always, both by my voice and vote, opposed what I believed to be extravagant and unnecessary expenditure, and will not hesitate to do so in the future. At the same time. I strongly -supported every movement which in my judgment was conducive to the best [interest of the ratepayers.' I cordially supported the freeing of our Public Library, the erection of the New Baths, and you will doubtless remember the very active part I took in ad7ocating and completing the purchase of the Water Works (and but far my action in the matter it might still luve been in abeyance; and if the pur- chase hadto be made now it could only be done at a greatly increased cost, which would have meant another £5),000 out of the ratepayers' pockets) which has proved co be a great and Increasing advantage to the Borough of Warrington. I, therefore, appeal to you for your support on November let, bellowing that I shall command your confidence and approval. a I am, La ties and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, JOHN HENDERSON FLEMING, Springfield Street, October 7th, 1898.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. NI lINICIPAL ELECTION, NOVEMBER, 1896. HOWLEY WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— I have much pleasure In complying with the numerous requests which I have received during the past few weeks and beg-to offer myself as a candidate for your suffrages at the forthcoming Municipal Election. As you are aware, I have a substantial interest in the Ward, both as an employer of labour, and as a property I owner, and having also resided in the Ward for upwards of thirty years. I claim to he thoroughly acquainted with your mends,, and the special requirements of the district. The land which was sec red in the Ward for an open space or playground Las so far been quite useless, owing to its nob havingteeu 'laid out fo^ the purpose intended. I should sup- port any well-considered scheme for its improvement. I am of opinion that true ec-morny and efficiency go band In hand, and I should support a policy of steady progress. and shoull oppose the expenditure of large sums of Money upon fanclfLil or impracticable objects. The River Mersey affords great natural facilities to the town generally, and to this Ward' in particular. which as yet have only been very slightly utilized, and I would support Any roposFils by means of which the business of the town -would be improved, trade encouraged, and the public benefited. There are other matter of vital importance involving Iserieustesponsibility upon the town which will shortly have to be dealt with, and these shall receive my most careful ' onsideration. Should you favour me with your confidence, you will find me the friend of al: and •enemy of none, always ready to fascist in promoting any scheme for the welfare and prosperity of the town. and Prepared to do my duty at all times with- nut fear or 'favour. remain, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, JOSEPH MOUNFIELD. Walton, October Ilst</p>
