        <p>BOROUGIT OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL ELEc rioN, NOVEMBER 2ND' 1896. TO TRH BLBOTORS OF ST. AUSTIN'S WARD. LAnyas AND GENTLEMEN,— Owing to theextenelon of the borough and the large in- crease of voters in this Ward. I have to vacate my seat in the Town Council. and I again offer myself as a Candidate for re-election on the First of November next. Daring the past twelve months I have tried to serve you faithfully, and to the best of my ability have promoted the Interests of the borough, and of this Ward in particalsr, by regular attendance on Committee and at the Council Meet- ings and by vote and voice assisting and promoting such measures as I thought were for the benefit of the Borough of Warrington. As a member of the Sanitary Works Committee I have done my utmost to improve the sanitary conditioa of the town, and perfect the Plant and Process at the Longford Depot, and on the Paving and Sewerage Committee, I have taken a special interest in the work of paving the back Passages and improving the principal streets and, where desirable, laying down permanent paving. The unsanitary condition of the River Mersey. and the Importance of maintaining the rights of the Manufacturers and Traders to enjoy the advantage of a good waterway for carriage of goods, ac., has had, and will continue to have my constant attention. I am in favour of providing open spaces and play grounds for children, where possible ,in every ward in the town, and any scheme of this kind shall have my strongest possible support. The Town Council having taken over the Gymnasium. I am in favour of the public schools having the privilege of using the same and giving the boys and girls a thorough training in athletics, and will endeavour if re-elected to assist such a scheme. As a ratepayer in the parish of Little Sankey, I shall en- deavour to secure for the ratepayers and inhabitants, all the privileges and advantages they a ie. entitled to, by being absorbed into the borough. As a Manufacturer and Bra ployerof labour and a resident In the Ward. I am well acquainted with the needs of the Ward, and trust my past services are a guarantee of my fit- ness to represent you in the Town Council. If you do me the hollow to re-elect me, I will do all I can I. advance and secure the eornmercial prosperity and social and moral improvement of the town. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen. Yours Faithfully, DAVID TINNION. Arpley Street, Warrington, October Ist, 1890.</p>
